window.SUBJECTS=[{"id": "maths", "name": "Maths", "weeks": [{"t": "Rounding, estimation & significant figures", "intro": "Rounding makes numbers simpler so they are easier to work with and talk about. Estimating uses rounded numbers to get a quick, sensible answer, which helps you check whether a calculator answer is right. You will need these skills in almost every GCSE maths topic.", "how": ["Find the digit you are rounding to, then look at the very next digit on its right.", "If that next digit is 5 or more, round up; if it is 4 or less, keep the digit the same.", "The first significant figure is the first digit that is not zero, reading from the left.", "Fill in with zeros so a big number keeps its size, e.g. 4,682 to 1 sf is 5,000, not 5.", "To estimate, round every number to 1 significant figure, then do the calculation."], "example": {"q": "Estimate the value of (8.9 × 41.2) ÷ 0.52", "steps": ["Round each number to 1 significant figure: 8.9 → 9, 41.2 → 40, 0.52 → 0.5.", "Work out the top: 9 × 40 = 360.", "Divide by 0.5: dividing by a half is the same as doubling, so 360 ÷ 0.5 = 720.", "Check it is sensible: the real answer is about 705, which is close."], "a": "A good estimate is 720."}, "walk": [{"h": "Rounding to decimal places", "b": "Decimal places (dp) are the digits after the decimal point. To round 6.347 to 1 dp, look at the second decimal digit, 4. As 4 is less than 5, the answer is 6.3. To round it to 2 dp, look at the 7, so it rounds up to 6.35."}, {"h": "Significant figures", "b": "Count significant figures from the first non-zero digit. In 0.0582 the first significant figure is 5, so to 2 sf it is 0.058 (the next digit, 2, is less than 5). For 3,476 to 2 sf, look at the 7: round up to 3,500, keeping zeros as placeholders."}, {"h": "Estimating calculations", "b": "Round each number to 1 significant figure and then calculate. For 31.6 × 4.8, use 30 × 5 = 150. Use ≈ to show an answer is approximate: 31.6 × 4.8 ≈ 150. Estimates catch mistakes, like pressing a wrong calculator button."}], "qs": [["Round 3.476 to 1 decimal place.", ["3.5", "3.4", "3.48", "3.0"], "The second decimal digit is 7, which is 5 or more, so 3.4 rounds up to 3.5."], ["Round 4,682 to the nearest hundred.", ["4,700", "4,600", "4,680", "5,000"], "The tens digit is 8, so 4,600 rounds up to 4,700."], ["How many significant figures does 0.0306 have?", ["3", "2", "4", "5"], "Start counting at the first non-zero digit 3: the digits 3, 0 and 6 are significant."], ["Round 0.04537 to 2 significant figures.", ["0.045", "0.046", "0.05", "0.0454"], "The first two significant figures are 4 and 5; the next digit is 3, so it stays 0.045."], ["Round 72,849 to 3 significant figures.", ["72,800", "72,900", "728", "72,850"], "The third significant figure is 8 and the next digit is 4, so it stays 72,800 with zeros as placeholders."], ["Round 9.96 to 1 decimal place.", ["10.0", "9.9", "9.10", "9.0"], "The 6 makes the 9 tenths round up, which carries over to make 10.0."], ["Estimate 48.7 × 3.1", ["150", "151", "160", "120"], "Round to 1 sf: 50 × 3 = 150."], ["Estimate (19.8 + 30.4) ÷ 4.9", ["10", "50", "12", "26"], "Round to 1 sf: (20 + 30) ÷ 5 = 50 ÷ 5 = 10."], ["Estimate 21.3² ÷ 3.8", ["100", "400", "10", "120"], "Round to 1 sf: 20² ÷ 4 = 400 ÷ 4 = 100."], ["Estimate 612 × 0.48 ÷ 0.21", ["1500", "150", "15,000", "60"], "Round to 1 sf: 600 × 0.5 = 300, then 300 ÷ 0.2 = 1500."]], "bonus": ["What is the value of the digit 7 in 4.073?", ["7 hundredths", "7 tenths", "7 thousandths", "7 tens"], "After the decimal point the columns are tenths (0), hundredths (7), thousandths (3)."]}, {"t": "Fractions, decimals & percentages", "intro": "Fractions, decimals and percentages are three different ways of writing parts of a whole. Being able to switch between them and calculate with them is used everywhere, from discounts in shops to interest on savings. They come up in lots of GCSE questions.", "how": ["To turn a fraction into a decimal, divide the top by the bottom, e.g. 3/4 = 3 ÷ 4 = 0.75.", "To turn a decimal into a percentage, multiply by 100, e.g. 0.6 = 60%.", "To add or subtract fractions, make the denominators the same first.", "To multiply fractions, multiply the tops and multiply the bottoms, then simplify.", "To divide by a fraction, flip the second fraction and multiply."], "example": {"q": "Find 35% of £240 without a calculator.", "steps": ["Find 10%: £240 ÷ 10 = £24.", "Find 30%: £24 × 3 = £72.", "Find 5%: half of 10%, so £24 ÷ 2 = £12.", "Add them: 30% + 5% = £72 + £12 = £84."], "a": "35% of £240 is £84."}, "walk": [{"h": "Converting between forms", "b": "Percent means 'out of 100', so 45% = 45/100 = 0.45. To simplify 45/100, divide top and bottom by 5 to get 9/20. Going the other way, 1/8 = 1 ÷ 8 = 0.125 = 12.5%."}, {"h": "Calculating with fractions", "b": "To add 1/3 + 1/4, use a common denominator of 12: 4/12 + 3/12 = 7/12. To multiply, 2/3 × 3/5 = 6/15 = 2/5. To divide, 1/2 ÷ 1/4 becomes 1/2 × 4/1 = 4/4 = 2."}, {"h": "Percentages of amounts", "b": "Build percentages from 10% and 1%. For 15% of 60: 10% is 6 and 5% is 3, so 15% is 9. To increase £50 by 20%, find 20% (£10) and add it on to get £60, or use a multiplier: £50 × 1.2 = £60."}], "qs": [["Write 3/4 as a decimal.", ["0.75", "0.34", "0.7", "0.43"], "3 ÷ 4 = 0.75."], ["Write 0.6 as a percentage.", ["60%", "6%", "0.6%", "600%"], "Multiply by 100: 0.6 × 100 = 60%."], ["Simplify 18/24 fully.", ["3/4", "9/12", "6/8", "2/3"], "The highest common factor of 18 and 24 is 6, and 18 ÷ 6 = 3, 24 ÷ 6 = 4."], ["Write 35% as a fraction in its simplest form.", ["7/20", "35/10", "3/5", "7/2"], "35% = 35/100, and dividing top and bottom by 5 gives 7/20."], ["Work out 15% of 80.", ["12", "1.2", "15", "20"], "10% of 80 is 8 and 5% is 4, so 15% is 8 + 4 = 12."], ["Work out 2/3 + 1/4", ["11/12", "3/7", "3/12", "2/12"], "Use twelfths: 8/12 + 3/12 = 11/12."], ["Work out 3/5 × 10/9 and simplify.", ["2/3", "30/14", "13/14", "3/2"], "3 × 10 = 30 and 5 × 9 = 45, and 30/45 simplifies to 2/3."], ["Work out 4/5 ÷ 2/3", ["6/5", "8/15", "5/6", "2/5"], "Flip and multiply: 4/5 × 3/2 = 12/10 = 6/5."], ["Increase £60 by 20%.", ["£72", "£80", "£62", "£12"], "20% of £60 is £12, and £60 + £12 = £72."], ["Put in order, smallest first: 0.4, 3/8, 42%", ["3/8, 0.4, 42%", "0.4, 3/8, 42%", "0.4, 42%, 3/8", "42%, 0.4, 3/8"], "As decimals they are 0.375, 0.4 and 0.42, so 3/8 is smallest."]], "bonus": ["What is the lowest common multiple of 4 and 6?", ["12", "24", "2", "10"], "12 is the smallest number in both the 4 and 6 times tables, which helps when adding quarters and sixths."]}, {"t": "Algebra: simplifying, expanding & factorising", "intro": "Algebra uses letters to stand for numbers, so we can write general rules. Simplifying, expanding and factorising are the basic tools for tidying up and rearranging expressions. You will use them for equations, formulae and graphs all through GCSE.", "how": ["Collect like terms: only add or subtract terms with exactly the same letters, e.g. 3x + 2x = 5x.", "When multiplying terms, multiply the numbers and write the letters together, e.g. 2a × 3b = 6ab.", "To expand a bracket, multiply every term inside by the term outside.", "To factorise, take out the highest common factor and put the rest in a bracket.", "To expand double brackets, multiply each term in the first bracket by each term in the second."], "example": {"q": "Expand and simplify 4(2x + 3) − 3(x − 5)", "steps": ["Expand the first bracket: 4 × 2x = 8x and 4 × 3 = 12, giving 8x + 12.", "Expand the second bracket: −3 × x = −3x and −3 × −5 = +15, giving −3x + 15.", "Put it together: 8x + 12 − 3x + 15.", "Collect like terms: 8x − 3x = 5x and 12 + 15 = 27."], "a": "The answer is 5x + 27."}, "walk": [{"h": "Collecting like terms", "b": "Like terms have exactly the same letter part. In 5a + 2b − 3a + b, the a terms give 5a − 3a = 2a and the b terms give 2b + b = 3b. So it simplifies to 2a + 3b. You cannot combine 2a and 3b because they are different terms."}, {"h": "Expanding brackets", "b": "The number outside multiplies everything inside: 3(x + 4) = 3x + 12. Watch the signs: −2(y − 5) = −2y + 10. For double brackets, multiply every pair: (x + 2)(x + 3) = x² + 3x + 2x + 6 = x² + 5x + 6."}, {"h": "Factorising", "b": "Factorising is the reverse of expanding. For 10x + 15, the highest common factor is 5, so it becomes 5(2x + 3). For a quadratic like x² + 5x + 6, find two numbers that multiply to 6 and add to 5 (2 and 3), giving (x + 2)(x + 3)."}], "qs": [["Simplify 3a + 5a − 2a", ["6a", "10a", "4a", "6a³"], "3 + 5 − 2 = 6, so the answer is 6a."], ["Simplify 4x + 3y − x + 2y", ["3x + 5y", "5x + 5y", "3x + y", "8xy"], "4x − x = 3x and 3y + 2y = 5y."], ["Simplify 3m × 4n", ["12mn", "7mn", "12m + n", "34mn"], "Multiply the numbers (3 × 4 = 12) and write the letters together."], ["Expand 5(2x − 3)", ["10x − 15", "10x − 3", "7x − 15", "10x + 15"], "5 × 2x = 10x and 5 × −3 = −15."], ["Expand and simplify 3(x + 4) + 2(x − 1)", ["5x + 10", "5x + 14", "5x + 11", "5x + 3"], "3x + 12 + 2x − 2 = 5x + 10."], ["Factorise 6x + 9", ["3(2x + 3)", "6(x + 9)", "3(2x + 9)", "2(3x + 9)"], "The highest common factor is 3, and 3 × 2x = 6x, 3 × 3 = 9."], ["Factorise fully 8x² + 12x", ["4x(2x + 3)", "4(2x² + 3x)", "2x(4x + 6)", "x(8x + 12)"], "The highest common factor of 8x² and 12x is 4x."], ["Expand (x + 3)(x + 5)", ["x² + 8x + 15", "x² + 15", "x² + 8x + 8", "x² + 15x + 8"], "x² + 5x + 3x + 15 = x² + 8x + 15."], ["Expand (x − 4)(x + 2)", ["x² − 2x − 8", "x² + 2x − 8", "x² − 2x + 8", "x² − 6x − 8"], "x² + 2x − 4x − 8 = x² − 2x − 8."], ["Factorise x² + 7x + 12", ["(x + 3)(x + 4)", "(x + 2)(x + 6)", "(x + 1)(x + 12)", "(x + 7)(x + 12)"], "3 and 4 multiply to 12 and add to 7."]], "bonus": ["What is −3 × −5?", ["15", "−15", "−8", "8"], "A negative times a negative gives a positive, so −3 × −5 = 15."]}, {"t": "Solving linear equations", "intro": "An equation is like a balance: both sides are equal. Solving it means finding the value of the letter that makes it true. This is one of the most important skills in GCSE maths and is used in almost every algebra question.", "how": ["Whatever you do to one side of the equation, do exactly the same to the other side.", "Use inverse operations to undo things: + undoes −, and × undoes ÷.", "Undo adding and subtracting first, then undo multiplying and dividing.", "If there are brackets, expand them first.", "If the letter is on both sides, move the smaller letter term over first, then check by substituting."], "example": {"q": "Solve 5x − 3 = 2x + 12", "steps": ["Subtract 2x from both sides: 3x − 3 = 12.", "Add 3 to both sides: 3x = 15.", "Divide both sides by 3: x = 5.", "Check: 5 × 5 − 3 = 22 and 2 × 5 + 12 = 22, so it works."], "a": "x = 5"}, "walk": [{"h": "One-step equations", "b": "Do the opposite operation to get the letter alone. For x + 9 = 14, subtract 9 from both sides to get x = 5. For 6x = 42, divide both sides by 6 to get x = 7. For x/3 = 4, multiply both sides by 3 to get x = 12."}, {"h": "Two-step equations", "b": "Undo the adding or subtracting first, then the multiplying. For 4x + 1 = 21, subtract 1 to get 4x = 20, then divide by 4 to get x = 5. Always check by putting your answer back in: 4 × 5 + 1 = 21."}, {"h": "Brackets and both sides", "b": "Expand any brackets first: 2(x + 1) = 14 becomes 2x + 2 = 14, so 2x = 12 and x = 6. With letters on both sides, like 7x − 2 = 3x + 10, subtract 3x from both sides to get 4x − 2 = 10, so 4x = 12 and x = 3."}], "qs": [["Solve x + 7 = 12", ["x = 5", "x = 19", "x = −5", "x = 84"], "Subtract 7 from both sides: 12 − 7 = 5."], ["Solve 4x = 28", ["x = 7", "x = 24", "x = 32", "x = 112"], "Divide both sides by 4: 28 ÷ 4 = 7."], ["Solve 3x − 5 = 16", ["x = 7", "x = 21", "x = 11", "x = 5"], "Add 5 to get 3x = 21, then divide by 3 to get x = 7."], ["Solve x/5 + 2 = 6", ["x = 20", "x = 40", "x = 4", "x = 0.8"], "Subtract 2 to get x/5 = 4, then multiply by 5 to get x = 20."], ["Solve 2(x + 3) = 18", ["x = 6", "x = 7.5", "x = 12", "x = 9"], "Expand to 2x + 6 = 18, so 2x = 12 and x = 6."], ["Solve 7 − 2x = 1", ["x = 3", "x = −3", "x = 4", "x = 6"], "Subtract 7 to get −2x = −6, then divide by −2 to get x = 3."], ["Solve 5x + 4 = 2x + 19", ["x = 5", "x = 3", "x = 7.5", "x = 15"], "Subtract 2x to get 3x + 4 = 19, so 3x = 15 and x = 5."], ["Solve (x + 4)/3 = 5", ["x = 11", "x = 19", "x = 15", "x = 1"], "Multiply by 3 to get x + 4 = 15, then subtract 4 to get x = 11."], ["Solve 3(2x − 1) = 4x + 9", ["x = 6", "x = 4", "x = 5", "x = 3"], "Expand to 6x − 3 = 4x + 9, so 2x = 12 and x = 6."], ["I think of a number, multiply it by 4 and subtract 7. The answer is 25. What is my number?", ["8", "4.5", "18", "32"], "4n − 7 = 25, so 4n = 32 and n = 8."]], "bonus": ["What is the inverse (opposite) of 'multiply by 3'?", ["Divide by 3", "Subtract 3", "Add 3", "Square it"], "Dividing by 3 undoes multiplying by 3, e.g. 5 × 3 = 15 and 15 ÷ 3 = 5."]}]}, {"id": "english-lang", "name": "English Language", "weeks": [{"t": "Finding explicit and implicit information", "intro": "Reading questions often start by asking you to find information in a text. Some information is stated clearly (explicit), and some is only hinted at, so you have to work it out (implicit). Getting these right is quick marks and is the base for every other reading skill.", "how": ["Explicit information is stated directly in the text, so you can point to it.", "Implicit information is suggested, so you must infer it from clues.", "Only use the lines the question tells you to look at.", "Keep answers short and accurate, either in your own words or as a brief quotation.", "For an inference, ask yourself: what does this clue suggest about a person or place?"], "example": {"q": "Read: 'Maya pulled her hood tighter as the rain hammered the bus shelter. The 7:15 had already gone. She checked her phone again – 3% battery – and sighed.' Give one explicit fact and one inference about Maya.", "steps": ["Look for something stated directly: 'her phone' is on '3% battery'.", "Look for clues about feelings: she 'sighed' and her bus 'had already gone'.", "Work out what the clues suggest: she is fed up and may be stuck or late.", "Write both answers clearly and briefly."], "a": "Explicit: Maya's phone is on 3% battery; implicit: she feels frustrated because she has missed her bus."}, "walk": [{"h": "Spot explicit facts", "b": "Explicit means 'out in the open'. In 'The old house had a red door and broken windows', two explicit facts are that the door is red and the windows are broken. You can underline the exact words that prove it."}, {"h": "Make inferences", "b": "To infer means to read between the lines. In 'Tom slammed his bag down and refused to speak', the text never says Tom is angry, but the clues 'slammed' and 'refused' suggest he is. That is implicit information."}, {"h": "Back it up", "b": "Always link your idea to the text. A good inference sounds like: 'Tom seems angry because he slammed his bag down.' The clue proves your point, so the examiner can see how you worked it out."}], "qs": [["What is explicit information?", ["Information stated directly in the text", "Information you have to work out from clues", "The writer's private opinion of the reader", "A fact you look up outside the text"], "Explicit information is clearly stated, so you can point to the exact words."], ["What is implicit information?", ["Meaning that is suggested, so you must infer it", "A fact written in the first line", "Information given in a title", "Any number mentioned in the text"], "Implicit meaning is hinted at rather than stated, so the reader works it out."], ["What does it mean to 'infer'?", ["To work out meaning from clues", "To copy out a quotation exactly", "To count the techniques used", "To retell the whole plot"], "Inferring means using clues in the text to reach a sensible conclusion."], ["'The rain hammered the bus shelter.' What do we learn explicitly about the weather?", ["It is raining", "It is snowing", "It is sunny", "It is foggy"], "The text directly says 'the rain', so this is stated, not inferred."], ["In the same sentence, which word best shows the rain is heavy?", ["hammered", "shelter", "the", "bus"], "'Hammered' suggests strong, loud, forceful rain."], ["'She checked her phone again – 3% battery – and sighed.' Which is explicit?", ["Her phone battery is at 3%", "She is feeling cold", "She is late for school", "She forgot her charger"], "Only the 3% battery is stated directly; the others are guesses or inferences."], ["From 'she sighed' after missing her bus, what can we infer about Maya?", ["She feels frustrated", "She feels excited", "She feels proud", "She feels relaxed"], "A sigh after a setback usually suggests frustration or disappointment."], ["'Mr Hale's garden was a jungle of nettles. The gate hung from one hinge, and letters spilled out of the letterbox.' What can you infer?", ["The house seems neglected or empty", "Mr Hale is a keen gardener", "The house has just been built", "Mr Hale rarely gets any letters"], "Overgrown nettles, a broken gate and piled-up post suggest no one is looking after it."], ["'Sam's hands shook as he opened the envelope with his exam results.' Best inference?", ["Sam is nervous about his results", "Sam is angry with his teacher", "Sam already knows his results", "Sam does not care about exams"], "Shaking hands while opening results suggests nerves about what they say."], ["A question asks for four things from lines 1–5. What should you do?", ["Pick four short, true points from lines 1–5 only", "Write your opinion of the text", "Analyse the writer's techniques", "Use points from anywhere in the text"], "These questions test careful retrieval from the exact lines given."]], "bonus": ["What is a fact?", ["Something that can be proved true", "A personal belief", "A guess about the future", "A feeling someone has"], "A fact can be checked and proved, unlike an opinion or feeling."]}, {"t": "Analysing language (techniques and their effects)", "intro": "Writers choose words and techniques carefully to make readers feel or picture something. Analysing language means spotting these choices and explaining their effect. It is the skill examiners reward most in reading questions.", "how": ["Find a precise word or phrase and quote it briefly.", "Name the technique if you can, such as simile, metaphor or personification.", "Explain the effect: what it makes the reader picture, feel or think.", "Zoom in on single words and their connotations (the ideas linked to them).", "Effect matters more than naming lots of techniques."], "example": {"q": "Analyse: 'The fog crept through the empty streets like a thief.'", "steps": ["Pick out key words: 'crept' and 'like a thief'.", "Name the techniques: 'crept' is personification and 'like a thief' is a simile.", "Explore connotations: 'crept' suggests quiet, sneaky movement; a thief is secretive and threatening.", "Explain the effect: the fog seems alive and dangerous, creating a tense, uneasy mood."], "a": "The personification and simile make the fog seem sneaky and threatening, so the reader feels uneasy."}, "walk": [{"h": "Know the techniques", "b": "A simile compares using 'like' or 'as' (as cold as ice). A metaphor says something is something else (the room was an oven). Personification gives human actions to non-human things (the trees whispered). Alliteration repeats starting sounds (bright blue balloons)."}, {"h": "Zoom in on words", "b": "Single words carry connotations. 'He strolled' and 'he stormed' both mean walking, but 'stormed' suggests anger and force. Ask what the word makes you think of, and why the writer chose it over a plainer word."}, {"h": "Explain the effect", "b": "Use a simple pattern: point, quote, effect. For example: 'The writer makes the room feel unbearable. The metaphor “the room was an oven” suggests extreme heat, so the reader imagines how uncomfortable the characters are.'"}], "qs": [["'Her smile was as bright as the sun.' Which technique is this?", ["Simile", "Metaphor", "Personification", "Onomatopoeia"], "It compares using 'as … as', which makes it a simile."], ["'The classroom was a zoo.' Which technique is this?", ["Metaphor", "Simile", "Alliteration", "Onomatopoeia"], "It says the classroom is a zoo, without 'like' or 'as', so it is a metaphor."], ["'The angry sea attacked the cliffs.' Which technique is this?", ["Personification", "Simile", "Alliteration", "Onomatopoeia"], "The sea is given human feelings and actions ('angry', 'attacked')."], ["'Slippery snakes slid silently.' Which technique is this?", ["Alliteration", "Onomatopoeia", "Personification", "Simile"], "Several nearby words start with the same 's' sound."], ["'The bacon sizzled and the kettle hissed.' Which technique is used?", ["Onomatopoeia", "Metaphor", "Simile", "Rhetorical question"], "'Sizzled' and 'hissed' sound like the noises they describe."], ["'I've told you a million times!' Which technique is this?", ["Hyperbole", "Simile", "Personification", "Onomatopoeia"], "It is a deliberate exaggeration for effect, which is hyperbole."], ["A football match is described with 'battle', 'wounded' and 'retreat'. What is this?", ["A semantic field of war", "A simile", "Onomatopoeia", "A rhetorical question"], "A group of words linked to one topic (war) is a semantic field."], ["Why might a writer choose 'trudged' instead of 'walked'?", ["It suggests slow, heavy, tired movement", "It shows the character is moving fast", "It is an adjective describing the road", "It makes the sentence rhyme"], "'Trudged' has connotations of effort and tiredness that 'walked' lacks."], ["A one-word sentence, 'Silence.', follows a noisy scene. Likely effect?", ["It creates a sudden, tense pause", "It shows the writer forgot a verb", "It speeds up the action", "It introduces a new character"], "A very short sentence after noise makes the reader stop and feel the tension."], ["Which approach gets the most credit when analysing language?", ["Quote a word, name the technique, explain its effect", "List as many techniques as possible", "Retell what happens in the passage", "Say whether you enjoyed it"], "Examiners reward explaining how the language affects the reader."]], "bonus": ["In 'The tiny dog barked', which word is an adjective?", ["tiny", "dog", "barked", "The"], "An adjective describes a noun; 'tiny' describes the dog."]}, {"t": "Analysing structure", "intro": "Structure is how a writer organises a text: what comes first, what changes and how it ends. Writers order ideas on purpose to build interest, tension or surprise. Structure questions ask you to explain these choices and their effect on the reader.", "how": ["Look at the beginning, middle and end and ask what the focus is at each point.", "Notice shifts: changes in time, place, character, mood or viewpoint.", "Spot features like flashbacks, foreshadowing, short paragraphs and repetition.", "Explain why the writer put things in that order and how it affects the reader.", "Talk about the whole text, not single words; that is language, not structure."], "example": {"q": "A short story opens with a calm description of a beach, then a dark cloud appears, and it ends with a single line: 'Nobody saw the boat again.' How is it structured to interest the reader?", "steps": ["Beginning: the calm beach sets a peaceful mood and lets the reader relax.", "Middle: the dark cloud is a shift that hints something bad will happen (foreshadowing).", "End: the short, separate final line makes the mystery stand out.", "Effect: the change from calm to threat builds tension and leaves the reader wanting answers."], "a": "It moves from calm to threat and ends on a short, mysterious line, building tension and curiosity."}, "walk": [{"h": "Openings and endings", "b": "Writers can open with a setting, a character or right in the middle of the action ('The door exploded inwards.'). Endings may resolve everything, leave a cliffhanger, or return to the start (a cyclical structure). Ask how each one hooks or affects the reader."}, {"h": "Shifts in focus", "b": "Track where the writer points the 'camera'. A text might zoom in from a whole city to one lit window, or jump back in time with a flashback. Each shift tells the reader what is important now."}, {"h": "Paragraphs and pace", "b": "Long paragraphs can slow things down for detail. A one-line paragraph, like 'Then the lights went out.', makes a key moment stand out. Explain the effect: it creates suspense, emphasis or surprise."}], "qs": [["What does 'structure' mean in a text?", ["How the text is organised and ordered", "The individual words and techniques used", "Only the punctuation marks", "The number of pages it has"], "Structure is about order, shape and shifts across the whole text."], ["What is a flashback?", ["A jump back in time to earlier events", "A jump forward to the future", "A detailed description of a setting", "The final sentence of a story"], "A flashback takes the reader back to something that happened before."], ["What is foreshadowing?", ["A hint about what will happen later", "A description of shadows", "A repeated word in one sentence", "A summary at the end of a story"], "Foreshadowing drops clues early that suggest future events."], ["A story opens with 'The door exploded inwards.' How does it begin?", ["In the middle of the action", "With a flashback", "With a slow description of setting", "At the end of the story"], "It throws the reader straight into a dramatic event."], ["Which of these is a structural feature?", ["A change of setting", "A simile", "An adjective", "Alliteration"], "A setting change is about how the text is organised; the others are language."], ["A text moves from a view of a whole city to one lit window. This is…", ["Zooming in, or narrowing the focus", "Foreshadowing", "A flashback", "A cyclical structure"], "The focus narrows from wide to close-up, like a camera zooming in."], ["What is a cyclical structure?", ["The ending returns to the opening idea", "The story is told backwards", "Every paragraph is the same length", "The story has no ending"], "In a cyclical structure the text comes back round to where it began."], ["What is the likely effect of a one-line paragraph at a key moment?", ["It makes that moment stand out", "It shows the writer ran out of ideas", "It slows the story right down", "It introduces new characters"], "Isolating a line on its own draws the reader's attention to it."], ["A text describes a calm beach, then storm clouds roll in. This is…", ["A shift in mood", "A flashback", "A cyclical structure", "A piece of dialogue"], "The atmosphere changes from peaceful to threatening."], ["Which is the best kind of structure answer?", ["Explains how the order of events affects the reader", "Lists the similes and metaphors", "Retells what happens", "Counts the paragraphs"], "Structure answers should explain why the writer ordered things that way and its effect."]], "bonus": ["When should you start a new paragraph?", ["At a new time, place, topic or speaker", "Every three lines", "Only at the very end", "Whenever you use a comma"], "Remember TiPToP: new Time, Place, Topic or Person speaking."]}, {"t": "Descriptive and narrative writing (plus spelling, punctuation & grammar)", "intro": "In the writing section you create your own description or story. Marks come from interesting, well-organised ideas and from accurate spelling, punctuation and grammar. Careful planning and checking can boost your grade a lot.", "how": ["Spend a few minutes planning: decide your opening, middle, key moment and ending.", "Show, don't tell: describe actions and senses instead of naming feelings.", "Vary sentence lengths and openings, e.g. start with an adverb or a short sentence.", "Use a range of punctuation accurately, including commas, apostrophes, colons and semicolons.", "Leave time at the end to check spelling and punctuation."], "example": {"q": "Write the opening two sentences of a description of a busy market.", "steps": ["Choose a focus: start close up on one sense rather than listing everything.", "Add precise words: 'sizzling onions' is better than 'nice food'.", "Vary the sentences: one longer, detailed sentence, then a short one for impact.", "Check punctuation: commas in lists and a full stop at the end of each sentence."], "a": "Example: 'Sizzling onions and sweet, sticky doughnuts filled the air as traders shouted over each other for attention. Everyone was in a hurry.'"}, "walk": [{"h": "Plan and structure", "b": "A simple story plan has an opening, a build-up, a key moment and an ending. A description can zoom from a wide view to small details. Even a five-line plan stops you rambling and helps you finish strongly."}, {"h": "Show, don't tell", "b": "Instead of 'I was scared', show it: 'My heart thudded and my hands would not stop shaking.' Use the senses (sight, sound, smell, touch, taste) and precise verbs and adjectives so the reader can picture the scene."}, {"h": "Accurate SPaG", "b": "Avoid comma splices: 'It was late, the road was empty' should use a full stop or semicolon. Use apostrophes for possession (the girl's bag) and missing letters (don't). Check tricky spellings like 'necessary', 'separate' and 'their/there/they're'."}], "qs": [["Which sentence uses their / there / they're correctly?", ["They're putting their coats over there.", "Their putting there coats over they're.", "There putting their coats over their.", "They're putting there coats over their."], "They're = they are, their = belonging to them, there = a place."], ["Which word is spelt correctly?", ["necessary", "neccessary", "necesary", "neccesary"], "Necessary has one c and two s's."], ["Which shows that a character is afraid, rather than telling us?", ["My heart thudded and my palms were slick with sweat.", "I was really scared.", "I felt afraid of the dark.", "Fear was what I felt."], "It shows fear through physical details instead of naming the feeling."], ["Which sentence uses the most senses to describe the seaside?", ["The salty air stung my lips as gulls screamed above.", "The seaside was very nice.", "We went to the seaside.", "The beach was big and good."], "It uses taste or touch (salty, stung) and sound (screamed)."], ["Which sentence starts with an adverb?", ["Silently, the fox slipped through the hedge.", "The fox slipped silently through the hedge.", "A fox slipped through the hedge.", "It was a fox in the hedge."], "'Silently' is an adverb and it opens the sentence."], ["Which is punctuated correctly?", ["It was late; the streets were empty.", "It was late, the streets were empty.", "It was late the streets were empty.", "it was late. The streets were empty."], "A semicolon can join two linked complete sentences; a comma alone cannot."], ["One dog has lost its lead. Which is correct?", ["The dog's lead was missing.", "The dogs lead was missing.", "The dogs' lead was missing.", "The dog's lead's was missing."], "For one owner, add apostrophe + s: dog's."], ["Which uses speech punctuation correctly?", ["“Wait for me,” she called.", "“Wait for me”, she called.", "“Wait for me.” She called.", "Wait for me, “she called.”"], "The comma goes inside the speech marks, and the sentence carries on in lower case."], ["Which sentence uses a colon correctly?", ["I packed three things: a torch, a map and water.", "I packed: three things a torch, a map and water.", "I: packed three things, a torch, a map and water.", "I packed three things, a torch: a map and water."], "A colon introduces a list after a complete clause."], ["Which is the best plan for a narrative?", ["Opening, build-up, key moment, ending", "Ending, opening, ending again", "Just start writing and see what happens", "Lots of characters and no setting"], "A clear shape keeps the story focused and builds to a climax."]], "bonus": ["Which of these is a complete sentence?", ["The cat slept by the fire.", "Running down the road.", "Because it was raining.", "The old man with a stick."], "It has a subject (the cat) and a verb (slept) and makes sense on its own."]}]}, {"id": "english-lit", "name": "English Literature", "weeks": [{"t": "Macbeth: plot and characters", "intro": "Macbeth is a tragedy by William Shakespeare about a brave Scottish soldier who murders his king to take the crown. Knowing the story and the main characters really well is the base for every Macbeth essay you will write.", "how": ["Learn the plot act by act: there are five acts, and each one moves Macbeth further from hero to tyrant.", "Know who each main character is and how they link to Macbeth.", "Track how Macbeth and Lady Macbeth change from the start of the play to the end.", "Learn a few short quotations for each main character so you can back up your points.", "Always say what Shakespeare is showing through a character, not just what the character does."], "example": {"q": "How does Lady Macbeth change across the play?", "steps": ["Act 1: she is powerful and determined, asking the spirits to ‘unsex me here’ so she can be cruel.", "She persuades Macbeth to kill Duncan by questioning his courage and his manhood.", "Act 3: Macbeth plans Banquo's murder without her, so she starts to lose control of him.", "Act 5: she sleepwalks, trying to wash imaginary blood away and crying ‘Out, damned spot!’", "She dies offstage, and Malcolm suggests she took her own life."], "a": "Lady Macbeth changes from a strong, controlling woman into someone broken and destroyed by guilt."}, "walk": [{"h": "Acts 1–2: the murder", "b": "Macbeth and Banquo meet three witches who say Macbeth will be Thane of Cawdor and then king, and that Banquo's descendants will be kings. When Macbeth is made Thane of Cawdor, he starts to believe it. Urged on by Lady Macbeth, he kills King Duncan at his castle in Inverness, and Duncan's sons Malcolm and Donalbain flee."}, {"h": "Act 3: king and tyrant", "b": "Macbeth is crowned but feels unsafe because of the prophecy about Banquo. He hires murderers to kill Banquo and his son Fleance, but Fleance escapes. At a feast, Banquo's ghost appears and only Macbeth can see it, which frightens his guests."}, {"h": "Acts 4–5: the downfall", "b": "The witches show Macbeth apparitions: beware Macduff, no man ‘of woman born’ can harm him, and he is safe until Birnam Wood comes to Dunsinane. Macduff flees to England, so Macbeth has his wife and children killed. Malcolm's soldiers carry branches from Birnam Wood, and Macduff, who was born by being ‘untimely ripped’ from his mother, kills Macbeth."}], "qs": [["In which country is Macbeth mainly set?", ["Scotland", "England", "Denmark", "Italy"], "Macbeth is a Scottish thane and the play takes place mostly in Scotland."], ["How many witches meet Macbeth and Banquo on the heath?", ["Three", "Two", "Four", "Five"], "The three witches (the ‘weird sisters’) give the prophecies in Act 1."], ["What new title is Macbeth given in Act 1?", ["Thane of Cawdor", "Thane of Fife", "Prince of Cumberland", "Thane of Ross"], "Duncan makes Macbeth Thane of Cawdor after the old thane is found to be a traitor."], ["Who persuades Macbeth to murder King Duncan?", ["Lady Macbeth", "Banquo", "Malcolm", "Ross"], "Lady Macbeth questions his courage and plans the murder with him."], ["Where is King Duncan murdered?", ["In Macbeth's castle at Inverness", "On the battlefield", "In his palace at Forres", "At Dunsinane"], "Duncan is a guest at Macbeth's castle in Inverness when he is killed in his sleep."], ["Who escapes when Banquo is murdered?", ["Fleance, his son", "Macduff", "Donalbain", "Seyton"], "Fleance escapes, so the witches' prophecy about Banquo's descendants can still come true."], ["Who appears at the banquet that only Macbeth can see?", ["Banquo's ghost", "Duncan's ghost", "The three witches", "Lady Macduff"], "Banquo's ghost sits in Macbeth's seat, showing his guilt and fear."], ["Why does Macbeth have Macduff's wife and children killed?", ["Macduff has fled to England to join Malcolm", "Macduff killed Banquo", "Lady Macduff insulted the queen", "The witches order him to"], "Macbeth lashes out at Macduff's family because Macduff has gone to join Malcolm against him."], ["How does Birnam Wood ‘come to Dunsinane’?", ["Soldiers carry branches cut from the wood", "The wood is set on fire", "The witches move the trees by magic", "A storm blows the trees down"], "Malcolm's army use branches as camouflage, so the wood seems to move."], ["Who calls Macbeth and his wife ‘this dead butcher and his fiend-like queen’?", ["Malcolm", "Macduff", "Banquo", "Ross"], "Malcolm says this in the final scene, showing how far Macbeth has fallen from ‘brave Macbeth’."]], "bonus": ["In a play, what are the smaller sections that each act is split into?", ["Scenes", "Chapters", "Stanzas", "Paragraphs"], "Plays are divided into acts, and each act is divided into scenes."]}, {"t": "Macbeth: themes and context", "intro": "Themes are the big ideas Shakespeare explores in Macbeth, like ambition, guilt and the supernatural. Context is what was happening when he wrote it, around 1606, under King James I, and examiners reward you for linking the two.", "how": ["Pick a theme and find two or three moments in the play where it appears.", "Link each theme to Jacobean beliefs, such as the Divine Right of Kings or fear of witches.", "Remember the play was written for King James I, so think about what would please or warn him.", "Use short quotations as evidence and explain the effect of key words.", "Ask what message Shakespeare is giving the audience about each theme."], "example": {"q": "How does Shakespeare present guilt in Macbeth?", "steps": ["Straight after the murder, Macbeth asks if ‘all great Neptune's ocean’ could wash the blood from his hand.", "He hears a voice cry that ‘Macbeth does murder sleep’, so guilt takes away his peace.", "Banquo's ghost at the banquet shows that guilt is haunting his mind.", "Lady Macbeth sleepwalks and tries to scrub away blood that is not there.", "Context: a Jacobean audience believed killing a king was a terrible sin, so they would expect punishment."], "a": "Shakespeare uses blood, sleeplessness and visions to show that guilt slowly destroys both Macbeth and Lady Macbeth."}, "walk": [{"h": "Ambition", "b": "Macbeth admits he has only ‘vaulting ambition’ to push him to kill Duncan. Ambition is his hamartia, or fatal flaw, which makes him a tragic hero who falls. Shakespeare warns that ambition without morals leads to ruin."}, {"h": "Guilt and the supernatural", "b": "Blood and sleep are motifs (repeated images) that show guilt. The witches, the floating dagger and Banquo's ghost add the supernatural. King James I was fascinated by witchcraft and even wrote a book about it, Daemonologie, so the witches would grip him."}, {"h": "Jacobean context", "b": "Jacobeans believed in the Divine Right of Kings: God chose the king, so regicide (killing a king) was a sin against God. After Duncan dies, nature goes wrong, with darkness in the day and horses eating each other. James I was believed to be descended from Banquo, and the Gunpowder Plot of 1605 had just tried to kill him."}], "qs": [["Which monarch was on the English throne when Macbeth was written?", ["James I", "Elizabeth I", "Henry VIII", "Charles I"], "Macbeth was written around 1606, during the reign of James I."], ["What does ‘regicide’ mean?", ["Killing a king", "Killing a brother", "Stealing a crown", "Betraying a friend"], "Regicide is the murder of a king, which Jacobeans saw as a terrible sin."], ["What was the Divine Right of Kings?", ["The belief that God chose the king", "A law letting kings be elected", "A tax paid to the king", "The king's right to fight wars"], "Jacobeans believed kings were chosen by God, so harming one went against God."], ["Which 1605 plot to kill James I made treason a hot topic?", ["The Gunpowder Plot", "The Spanish Armada", "The Great Fire of London", "The Babington Plot"], "Guy Fawkes and others tried to blow up Parliament and James I in 1605."], ["What did King James I write a book about?", ["Witchcraft", "Sailing", "Farming", "Sword fighting"], "James wrote Daemonologie (1597), showing his fear of and interest in witches."], ["Why does Shakespeare show Banquo as noble and good?", ["James I was believed to descend from him", "He was Shakespeare's friend", "He was the real king of Scotland", "He was English, like the audience"], "Showing James's ancestor as noble would please the king watching the play."], ["Which motif is most linked to guilt in Macbeth?", ["Blood that will not wash away", "Flowers and gardens", "Gold and money", "Music and dancing"], "Both Macbeths feel they cannot wash away the blood, which shows their guilt."], ["Macbeth calls it ‘a dagger of the mind’. What does this suggest?", ["It may be a hallucination from his guilty thoughts", "It is a real dagger he can touch", "Lady Macbeth placed it there", "It belongs to Banquo"], "Calling it ‘of the mind’ suggests his imagination and guilt are creating it."], ["What is Macbeth's hamartia (fatal flaw)?", ["His ambition", "His cowardice", "His jealousy of Macduff", "His laziness"], "His ‘vaulting ambition’ drives him to murder and leads to his downfall."], ["Why does nature go wrong (darkness by day) after Duncan's murder?", ["It shows killing God's chosen king upsets natural order", "It shows the weather in Scotland is bad", "It shows the witches want rain", "It shows Duncan was a weak king"], "Jacobeans believed regicide broke the natural order set by God, so nature itself rebels."]], "bonus": ["In a story or play, what is a ‘theme’?", ["A big idea the writer explores", "The place where it is set", "The name of the main character", "The final scene"], "A theme is a big idea, like ambition or guilt, that runs through a whole text."]}, {"t": "A Christmas Carol", "intro": "A Christmas Carol is a novella by Charles Dickens, published in 1843, about a mean old man called Ebenezer Scrooge who is visited by ghosts and changes his ways. Dickens wrote it to make Victorian readers care more about the poor.", "how": ["Learn the plot stave by stave: Dickens called his five chapters ‘staves’, like verses of a carol.", "Know what each ghost shows Scrooge and why it matters.", "Track how Scrooge changes from cold and greedy to warm and generous.", "Link the story to Victorian context, such as poverty, workhouses and the 1834 Poor Law.", "Remember Dickens's message: we are all responsible for helping others."], "example": {"q": "How does Dickens show Scrooge changing in A Christmas Carol?", "steps": ["Stave 1: Scrooge is ‘solitary as an oyster’, says ‘Bah! Humbug!’ and refuses to give to charity.", "Staves 2–3: the ghosts show him his lonely past and the poor but loving Cratchit family, and he starts to feel pity.", "Stave 4: he sees his own lonely grave and Tiny Tim's death, and begs to change.", "Stave 5: he buys the Cratchits a prize turkey, raises Bob's salary and becomes a ‘second father’ to Tiny Tim.", "Dickens uses this change to show readers that anyone can become kinder."], "a": "Dickens shows Scrooge changing from a cold, greedy miser into a generous, joyful man, to teach readers to help the poor."}, "walk": [{"h": "Staves 1–2", "b": "On Christmas Eve, Scrooge refuses his nephew Fred's invitation and turns away men collecting for charity. His dead business partner Jacob Marley appears in chains, saying ‘I wear the chain I forged in life’, and warns him three ghosts will come. The Ghost of Christmas Past shows Scrooge his lonely school days, his kind old boss Fezziwig, and Belle, who left him because he loved money more than her."}, {"h": "Staves 3–5", "b": "The Ghost of Christmas Present shows the Cratchits' happy but poor Christmas and the sick child Tiny Tim, then reveals two starving children, Ignorance and Want. The Ghost of Christmas Yet to Come shows people stealing from a dead man, who turns out to be Scrooge, and Tiny Tim's death. Scrooge wakes on Christmas Day a changed man, and Tiny Tim does not die."}, {"h": "Victorian context", "b": "The 1834 Poor Law sent the poorest people into harsh workhouses, which is why Scrooge asks ‘Are there no workhouses?’ He talks of decreasing the ‘surplus population’, echoing the ideas of Thomas Malthus. Dickens knew poverty himself: at 12 he worked in a blacking factory while his father was in a debtors' prison."}], "qs": [["Who wrote A Christmas Carol?", ["Charles Dickens", "William Shakespeare", "Robert Louis Stevenson", "Jane Austen"], "Charles Dickens wrote A Christmas Carol in 1843."], ["In which year was A Christmas Carol first published?", ["1843", "1812", "1863", "1901"], "It was published in December 1843 (1812 is the year Dickens was born)."], ["What is Bob Cratchit's job?", ["Scrooge's clerk", "Scrooge's nephew", "A shopkeeper", "A schoolteacher"], "Bob works for Scrooge as a poorly paid clerk."], ["What does Dickens call the five chapters of the book?", ["Staves", "Acts", "Scenes", "Cantos"], "A stave is a verse of a song, fitting the idea of a ‘carol’."], ["Who is the first ghost to visit Scrooge?", ["Jacob Marley", "The Ghost of Christmas Past", "The Ghost of Christmas Present", "Fezziwig"], "Marley, Scrooge's dead partner, comes first to warn him about the three spirits."], ["Which ghost shows Scrooge Fezziwig's Christmas party?", ["The Ghost of Christmas Past", "The Ghost of Christmas Present", "The Ghost of Christmas Yet to Come", "Jacob Marley"], "The Ghost of Christmas Past takes Scrooge back to his time as Fezziwig's apprentice."], ["Who are the two children hidden under the robe of Christmas Present?", ["Ignorance and Want", "Hope and Charity", "Fred and Belle", "Peter and Martha"], "They stand for the lack of education and the poverty Dickens wanted society to fix."], ["What is Dickens criticising with ‘Are there no workhouses?’", ["Cruel attitudes to the poor under the Poor Law", "The cost of Christmas presents", "Bad food served in prisons", "Laziness among rich businessmen"], "Scrooge thinks workhouses are enough, but Dickens knew they were harsh and cruel."], ["Scrooge talks of decreasing the ‘surplus population’. Whose ideas does this echo?", ["Thomas Malthus", "Charles Darwin", "Karl Marx", "Isaac Newton"], "Malthus argued that population would outgrow food, and some used this to excuse ignoring the poor."], ["Which childhood experience shaped Dickens's sympathy for the poor?", ["He worked in a factory at 12 while his father was in prison", "He grew up in a rich family in the countryside", "He lived in a workhouse until he was 18", "He was a sailor as a boy"], "At 12 he worked in a blacking factory while his father was in a debtors' prison."]], "bonus": ["What is the ‘moral’ of a story?", ["The lesson the story teaches", "The very first line", "Where the story is set", "The name of the villain"], "A moral is the lesson the writer wants readers to learn, like being kind to others."]}, {"t": "Poetry: form, structure and language", "intro": "To analyse a poem you look at its language (the words and images), its form (the type of poem) and its structure (how it is organised). Knowing the right terms helps you explain how a poet creates meaning and feeling.", "how": ["Language: spot techniques like simile, metaphor, personification and alliteration, then explain their effect.", "Form: identify the type of poem, such as a sonnet (14 lines) or free verse (no fixed rhyme or rhythm).", "Structure: look at stanzas, line breaks, enjambment, caesura and where the mood changes.", "Always use the formula: name the technique, give the quotation, explain the effect.", "Don't just list techniques; say why the poet chose them."], "example": {"q": "Explain the technique in this line: ‘The storm prowled round the house, scratching at the windows.’", "steps": ["Notice that the storm is doing things a living creature does: it ‘prowled’ and was ‘scratching’.", "Name the technique: this is personification, giving human or animal actions to something that is not alive.", "Zoom in on a word: ‘prowled’ makes the storm sound like a hunting animal looking for prey.", "Explain the effect: the storm feels dangerous and threatening, creating a tense, scary mood."], "a": "The poet uses personification, with verbs like ‘prowled’, to make the storm seem like a predator and create a threatening mood."}, "walk": [{"h": "Language techniques", "b": "A simile compares using ‘like’ or ‘as’: ‘her laugh was like a bell’. A metaphor says something IS something else: ‘the classroom was a zoo’. Personification gives human qualities to objects, alliteration repeats first sounds (‘big bold bears’), and onomatopoeia uses sound words like ‘crash’ or ‘hiss’."}, {"h": "Form", "b": "A sonnet has 14 lines and is often about love; it usually has a volta, a turn where the mood or argument changes. Many poems use iambic pentameter: ten syllables per line in five da-DUM beats, like ‘The cold grey sea came rolling to the shore’. Free verse has no fixed rhyme scheme or rhythm."}, {"h": "Structure", "b": "Stanzas are the ‘paragraphs’ of a poem: a couplet has 2 lines, a tercet 3 and a quatrain 4. Enjambment is when a sentence runs over a line break without punctuation, which can create speed or surprise. A caesura is a pause in the middle of a line, often from a full stop or comma, like ‘I ran. I stopped. I turned.’"}], "qs": [["‘Her smile was like a summer sunrise.’ Which technique is this?", ["Simile", "Metaphor", "Personification", "Onomatopoeia"], "It compares using the word ‘like’, so it is a simile."], ["‘The classroom was a zoo.’ Which technique is this?", ["Metaphor", "Simile", "Alliteration", "Rhyme"], "It says the classroom IS a zoo, without ‘like’ or ‘as’, so it is a metaphor."], ["‘The wind whispered secrets through the trees.’ Which technique is this?", ["Personification", "Simile", "Onomatopoeia", "Caesura"], "The wind is given the human action of whispering secrets."], ["‘The fire crackled and hissed.’ Which technique is used?", ["Onomatopoeia", "Simile", "Metaphor", "Enjambment"], "‘Crackled’ and ‘hissed’ sound like the noises they describe."], ["How many lines does a sonnet have?", ["14", "12", "10", "16"], "A sonnet always has 14 lines."], ["What is a four-line stanza called?", ["A quatrain", "A couplet", "A tercet", "A sestet"], "Quatrain comes from the French for four; a couplet has 2 lines and a tercet 3."], ["What is enjambment?", ["A sentence running on over a line break", "A pause in the middle of a line", "Two lines that rhyme in a row", "Repeating the first sound of words"], "Enjambment carries a sentence over the end of a line with no punctuation."], ["What is a caesura?", ["A pause in the middle of a line", "A sentence running over a line break", "The last line of a poem", "A word that copies a sound"], "A caesura is a mid-line pause, often made by a full stop or comma."], ["How many syllables are in a line of iambic pentameter?", ["Ten", "Eight", "Five", "Twelve"], "Pentameter means five beats, and each iamb has two syllables, so 5 × 2 = 10."], ["In a sonnet, what is the volta?", ["A turn where the mood or argument changes", "A line of ten syllables", "A four-line stanza", "A repeated chorus"], "The volta (Italian for ‘turn’) marks a shift in the poem's ideas or feelings."]], "bonus": ["In ‘The silver moon rose slowly’, which word is an adjective?", ["silver", "moon", "rose", "slowly"], "An adjective describes a noun; ‘silver’ describes the moon (‘rose’ is a verb here)."]}]}, {"id": "biology", "name": "Biology", "weeks": [{"t": "Cell structure", "intro": "All living things are made of cells. In this topic you will learn the parts inside cells, how plant, animal and bacterial cells are different, and how some cells are specially shaped to do one job.", "how": ["Animal and plant cells are eukaryotic: their DNA is kept inside a nucleus.", "Bacteria are prokaryotic: they are much smaller, have no nucleus, and their DNA is one loop plus small rings called plasmids.", "All cells have a cell membrane, cytoplasm and ribosomes; animal and plant cells also have mitochondria.", "Plant cells also have a cellulose cell wall, chloroplasts and a permanent vacuole full of cell sap.", "Specialised cells have special features that help them do one job, like sperm cells and root hair cells."], "example": {"q": "A cell has a cell membrane, cytoplasm, a nucleus, mitochondria, a cell wall and chloroplasts. What type of cell is it?", "steps": ["It has a nucleus, so it is eukaryotic, not a bacterium.", "Eukaryotic cells include animal cells and plant cells.", "It has a cell wall and chloroplasts, which animal cells never have.", "Chloroplasts are found in plant cells, so this must be a plant cell."], "a": "It is a plant cell."}, "walk": [{"h": "Parts of every cell", "b": "The cell membrane controls what goes in and out, and the cytoplasm is a jelly where most chemical reactions happen. Ribosomes make proteins. In animal and plant cells, the nucleus holds the genetic material and mitochondria carry out aerobic respiration to release energy."}, {"h": "Plants versus bacteria", "b": "Plant cells add a cell wall made of cellulose for strength, chloroplasts that absorb light for photosynthesis, and a permanent vacuole full of cell sap. Bacteria are prokaryotic: they have a cell wall (not made of cellulose) but no nucleus, no mitochondria and no chloroplasts. Their DNA is one big loop in the cytoplasm, plus small rings of DNA called plasmids."}, {"h": "Specialised cells and sizes", "b": "A sperm cell has a tail to swim and lots of mitochondria to release energy, while a root hair cell has a long thin hair that gives a large surface area for taking in water and mineral ions. Cells are tiny, so we measure them in micrometres (µm): 1 mm = 1000 µm, so 0.02 mm = 20 µm. Bacteria are usually only a few micrometres long, while animal and plant cells are about 10 to 100 µm."}], "qs": [["Which part of an animal cell contains the genetic material?", ["Nucleus", "Ribosome", "Cell membrane", "Cytoplasm"], "The nucleus holds the DNA, which carries the genetic instructions for the cell."], ["Where does aerobic respiration happen in a cell?", ["Mitochondria", "Ribosomes", "Nucleus", "Chloroplasts"], "Mitochondria carry out aerobic respiration, which releases energy for the cell."], ["What is the job of ribosomes?", ["Making proteins", "Carrying out photosynthesis", "Controlling what enters the cell", "Storing cell sap"], "Ribosomes are where proteins are made."], ["Which part is found in plant cells but NOT in animal cells?", ["Chloroplasts", "Mitochondria", "Ribosomes", "Cell membrane"], "Chloroplasts absorb light for photosynthesis, which animal cells do not do."], ["What is a plant cell wall made of?", ["Cellulose", "Starch", "Protein", "Glucose"], "Plant cell walls are made of cellulose, which makes them strong."], ["Which of these is true for a bacterial cell?", ["It has no nucleus", "It has a nucleus", "It has chloroplasts", "It has mitochondria"], "Bacteria are prokaryotic, so their DNA is not inside a nucleus."], ["What are plasmids?", ["Small rings of DNA found in bacteria", "Organelles that make proteins", "Tiny vacuoles in plant cells", "Parts of the nucleus in animal cells"], "Plasmids are small rings of DNA found in bacteria, as well as their main DNA loop."], ["Why does a sperm cell contain lots of mitochondria?", ["To release energy for swimming", "To make food by photosynthesis", "To store the genetic material", "To absorb water from around it"], "Mitochondria release energy by respiration, which the tail needs to swim."], ["How is a root hair cell adapted to absorb water?", ["It has a large surface area", "It has lots of chloroplasts", "It has a tail to move through soil", "It has no cell membrane"], "The long thin hair gives a large surface area for taking in water and mineral ions."], ["A bacterium is 0.003 mm long. How long is it in micrometres (µm)?", ["3 µm", "0.3 µm", "30 µm", "3000 µm"], "1 mm = 1000 µm, so 0.003 × 1000 = 3 µm."]], "bonus": ["What is a cell?", ["The basic building block of all living things", "A group of organs working together", "A chemical found only in plants", "A tiny particle inside an atom"], "Every living thing is made of one or more cells, the basic building blocks of life."]}, {"t": "Microscopy and cell division", "intro": "Microscopes let us see cells, and a simple formula tells us how much bigger the image is than the real thing. You will also learn how cells copy themselves by mitosis, and what makes stem cells so special.", "how": ["Magnification = image size ÷ real size, with both sizes in the same units.", "Total magnification of a light microscope = eyepiece lens × objective lens.", "Electron microscopes have a much higher magnification and resolution than light microscopes, so they show finer detail.", "Before a cell divides it copies its DNA, then mitosis makes two genetically identical cells for growth and repair.", "Stem cells are unspecialised cells that can turn into other types of cell."], "example": {"q": "Under a microscope, the image of a cell is 24 mm wide. The magnification is ×400. What is the real width of the cell in µm?", "steps": ["Rearrange the formula: real size = image size ÷ magnification.", "Real size = 24 ÷ 400 = 0.06 mm.", "Convert to micrometres: 1 mm = 1000 µm.", "0.06 × 1000 = 60 µm."], "a": "The cell is 60 µm wide."}, "walk": [{"h": "Using magnification", "b": "Magnification tells you how many times bigger the image is than the real object: magnification = image size ÷ real size, with both in the same units. For example, an image 10 mm wide of a cell 0.1 mm wide is magnified 10 ÷ 0.1 = ×100. Resolution is how well you can tell two close points apart; electron microscopes have much better resolution, so they can show tiny things like ribosomes."}, {"h": "The cell cycle and mitosis", "b": "Before dividing, a cell grows, makes more organelles such as ribosomes and mitochondria, and copies its DNA. In mitosis, one set of chromosomes is pulled to each end of the cell, then the cytoplasm and cell membrane split to make two cells. Each new cell is genetically identical to the original, so a human body cell with 46 chromosomes makes two cells with 46 each."}, {"h": "Stem cells", "b": "A stem cell is unspecialised and can become other kinds of cell. Embryonic stem cells can turn into almost any type of cell, while adult stem cells, such as those in bone marrow, can only form a few types, like blood cells. In plants, stem cells are found in meristems at the tips of roots and shoots, and they can make any type of plant cell throughout the plant's life."}], "qs": [["Which formula is used to work out magnification?", ["image size ÷ real size", "real size ÷ image size", "image size × real size", "image size − real size"], "Magnification is how many times bigger the image is, so you divide the image size by the real size."], ["A microscope has a ×10 eyepiece and a ×40 objective lens. What is the total magnification?", ["×400", "×50", "×30", "×4"], "Multiply the two lenses: 10 × 40 = 400."], ["An image is 20 mm long and the real object is 0.1 mm long. What is the magnification?", ["×200", "×2", "×20", "×2000"], "Magnification = 20 ÷ 0.1 = 200."], ["Why can an electron microscope show more detail than a light microscope?", ["It has a higher resolution", "It uses coloured stains", "It is smaller and lighter", "It uses a brighter light bulb"], "Higher resolution means it can tell apart points that are very close together, showing finer detail."], ["What happens to a cell's DNA before the cell divides by mitosis?", ["It is copied", "It is destroyed", "It is cut in half", "It leaves the cell"], "The DNA is copied so that each new cell gets a full set of chromosomes."], ["What does mitosis produce?", ["Two genetically identical cells", "Four genetically different cells", "Two cells with half the chromosomes", "One larger cell"], "Mitosis makes two new cells that are genetically identical to the parent cell."], ["A human body cell has 46 chromosomes. How many does each new cell have after mitosis?", ["46", "23", "92", "12"], "The cells made by mitosis are identical to the parent, so each has 46 chromosomes."], ["Which type of stem cell can turn into almost any type of body cell?", ["Embryonic stem cells", "Bone marrow stem cells", "Red blood cells", "Root hair cells"], "Embryonic stem cells can become almost any cell, while adult stem cells form only a few types."], ["Where are stem cells found in plants?", ["In meristems at root and shoot tips", "In the cell sap of the vacuole", "Only inside flowers", "Inside the chloroplasts"], "Meristems at the tips of roots and shoots contain plant stem cells."], ["A cell's image is 30 mm long at a magnification of ×1000. What is its real length?", ["30 µm", "0.03 µm", "3 µm", "30 000 µm"], "Real size = 30 ÷ 1000 = 0.03 mm, and 0.03 × 1000 = 30 µm."]], "bonus": ["What is 12 ÷ 1000?", ["0.012", "0.12", "1.2", "0.0012"], "Dividing by 1000 moves each digit three places to the right, giving 0.012."]}, {"t": "Transport in cells", "intro": "Cells need to take in useful substances like oxygen and glucose, and get rid of waste like carbon dioxide. They do this by diffusion, osmosis and active transport, three ways of moving substances into and out of cells.", "how": ["Diffusion is the spreading out of particles from an area of higher concentration to an area of lower concentration.", "Diffusion is faster with a bigger concentration difference, a higher temperature and a larger surface area.", "Osmosis is the diffusion of water from a dilute solution to a more concentrated solution through a partially permeable membrane.", "Active transport moves substances from a lower to a higher concentration, against the gradient, using energy from respiration.", "Percentage change in mass = (change in mass ÷ starting mass) × 100."], "example": {"q": "A potato cylinder has a mass of 2.0 g. After 30 minutes in pure water its mass is 2.5 g. What is the percentage change in mass?", "steps": ["Change in mass = 2.5 − 2.0 = 0.5 g.", "Divide by the starting mass: 0.5 ÷ 2.0 = 0.25.", "Multiply by 100: 0.25 × 100 = 25%.", "The mass went up because water entered the potato cells by osmosis."], "a": "The mass increased by 25%."}, "walk": [{"h": "Diffusion", "b": "Particles in liquids and gases move about randomly, so they spread from where there are lots of them to where there are few; in the lungs, oxygen diffuses from the alveoli into the blood. Diffusion is faster when the concentration gradient is steeper, the temperature is higher or the surface area is larger. For a cube, surface area = 6 × side × side and volume = side × side × side, so a 1 cm cube has a surface area to volume ratio of 6:1. Small organisms have a large ratio, so diffusion alone can supply them."}, {"h": "Osmosis", "b": "Osmosis is a special type of diffusion: it is only about water moving across a partially permeable membrane. Water moves from a dilute solution (lots of water) to a more concentrated solution (less water). A plant cell in pure water takes in water and becomes firm, or turgid, and its cell wall stops it bursting. An animal cell has no cell wall, so in pure water it can swell and burst."}, {"h": "Active transport", "b": "Sometimes a cell needs to collect a substance even when there is more inside than outside. Active transport moves it against the concentration gradient, from lower to higher concentration, using energy from respiration. Root hair cells use it to take in mineral ions from the soil, where the ions are less concentrated than in the cell, and the gut uses it to absorb glucose."}], "qs": [["What is diffusion?", ["Particles spreading from higher to lower concentration", "Particles moving from lower to higher concentration", "Water moving out of a cell only", "Particles moving using energy from respiration"], "Diffusion is the net movement of particles from where they are more concentrated to where they are less concentrated."], ["Which gas diffuses from the alveoli into the blood?", ["Oxygen", "Carbon dioxide", "Nitrogen", "Hydrogen"], "There is more oxygen in the alveoli than in the blood, so oxygen diffuses into the blood."], ["Which change would make diffusion faster?", ["Raising the temperature", "Making the surface area smaller", "Making the concentration gradient smaller", "Making the membrane thicker"], "At a higher temperature particles move faster, so they diffuse faster."], ["Which substance moves during osmosis?", ["Water", "Glucose", "Oxygen", "Mineral ions"], "Osmosis is the diffusion of water only."], ["What kind of membrane is needed for osmosis?", ["Partially permeable", "Fully permeable", "Completely waterproof", "One made only of cellulose"], "A partially permeable membrane lets small water molecules through but not larger solute molecules."], ["A plant cell is placed in pure water. What happens?", ["It takes in water and becomes turgid", "It loses water and becomes floppy", "It takes in water and bursts", "Nothing happens to it"], "Water enters by osmosis, and the cell wall stops the cell from bursting, so it becomes firm."], ["Where does the energy for active transport come from?", ["Respiration", "Photosynthesis", "Osmosis", "Diffusion"], "Active transport works against the gradient, so it needs energy released by respiration."], ["Why do root hair cells need active transport to take in mineral ions?", ["Ions are less concentrated in the soil than in the cell", "Ions are more concentrated in the soil than in the cell", "Mineral ions are made of water", "Root hair cells have no cell membrane"], "The ions must move against the concentration gradient, which needs active transport."], ["A potato piece goes from 4.0 g to 3.0 g in salty water. What is the percentage change in mass?", ["−25%", "−33%", "+25%", "−1%"], "Change = −1.0 g, and −1.0 ÷ 4.0 × 100 = −25%."], ["What is the surface area to volume ratio of a 3 cm cube?", ["2:1", "3:1", "6:1", "1:2"], "Surface area = 6 × 3 × 3 = 54 cm², volume = 3 × 3 × 3 = 27 cm³, and 54 ÷ 27 = 2, so 2:1."]], "bonus": ["Glass A has 1 spoon of squash in 200 ml of water. Glass B has 3 spoons in 200 ml. Which is more concentrated?", ["Glass B", "Glass A", "They are the same", "You cannot tell"], "Glass B has more squash in the same amount of water, so it is more concentrated."]}, {"t": "Organisation and digestion", "intro": "Your body is built in levels, from cells up to whole organ systems like the digestive system. Digestion uses enzymes to break big food molecules into small ones that can be absorbed into your blood. You will also learn simple tests to find out which nutrients a food contains.", "how": ["Organisation goes: cells → tissues → organs → organ systems → organism.", "Enzymes are biological catalysts: proteins that speed up reactions without being used up.", "Each enzyme has an active site that fits only one substrate, like a lock and key.", "Amylase breaks starch into sugars, protease breaks proteins into amino acids, and lipase breaks lipids into fatty acids and glycerol.", "Food tests: iodine for starch, Benedict's for sugars, Biuret for protein and Sudan III for lipids."], "example": {"q": "A food sample turns iodine solution blue-black and stays blue with Biuret reagent. What does the food contain?", "steps": ["Iodine solution starts orange-brown.", "It turned blue-black, so starch is present.", "Biuret reagent starts blue and turns purple if protein is present.", "It stayed blue, so there is no protein."], "a": "The food contains starch but no protein."}, "walk": [{"h": "Levels of organisation", "b": "Cells are the building blocks, and a tissue is a group of similar cells doing one job, like muscle tissue. An organ is made of several tissues working together, like the stomach. Organs work together in an organ system, such as the digestive system, which breaks down and absorbs food."}, {"h": "Enzymes and digestion", "b": "Enzymes are biological catalysts; the substrate fits the enzyme's active site like a key in a lock, and too much heat or the wrong pH changes the active site's shape so the enzyme is denatured. Amylase turns starch into sugars, protease turns proteins into amino acids, and lipase turns lipids into fatty acids and glycerol. Bile is made in the liver and stored in the gall bladder. It is not an enzyme: it neutralises stomach acid and emulsifies fat into small droplets, giving lipase a bigger surface area to work on."}, {"h": "Testing foods", "b": "Iodine solution turns from orange-brown to blue-black if starch is present. Benedict's solution must be heated in a water bath, and turns from blue to green, yellow or brick-red if a sugar such as glucose is present. Biuret reagent turns from blue to purple if protein is present, and Sudan III stain makes a red layer on top if lipids (fats and oils) are present."}], "qs": [["Which list is in the correct order, from smallest to largest?", ["Cells, tissues, organs, organ systems", "Tissues, cells, organs, organ systems", "Cells, organs, tissues, organ systems", "Organs, tissues, cells, organ systems"], "Cells make tissues, tissues make organs, and organs work together in organ systems."], ["What are enzymes?", ["Biological catalysts", "Types of carbohydrate", "Cells that fight disease", "Waste products of digestion"], "Enzymes are proteins that speed up reactions in living things without being used up."], ["What does amylase break down?", ["Starch into sugars", "Proteins into amino acids", "Fats into fatty acids", "Sugars into starch"], "Amylase is the enzyme that breaks starch down into sugars."], ["What does lipase break lipids into?", ["Fatty acids and glycerol", "Amino acids", "Sugars", "Starch and water"], "Lipase breaks lipids (fats and oils) into fatty acids and glycerol."], ["Which result shows that starch is present?", ["Iodine turns blue-black", "Iodine stays orange-brown", "Biuret turns purple", "Benedict's turns brick-red"], "Iodine solution changes from orange-brown to blue-black when starch is present."], ["A food is heated with Benedict's solution and turns brick-red. What does this show?", ["Sugar is present", "Starch is present", "Protein is present", "Fat is present"], "Benedict's solution turns brick-red when heated with a sugar such as glucose."], ["What colour does Biuret reagent turn if protein is present?", ["Purple", "Brick-red", "Blue-black", "Orange-brown"], "Biuret reagent changes from blue to purple when protein is present."], ["Why does an enzyme stop working at a very high temperature?", ["Its active site changes shape", "The enzyme is killed", "The substrate melts", "The enzyme gets used up"], "High temperatures denature the enzyme, changing the shape of its active site so the substrate no longer fits."], ["Where is bile made and stored?", ["Made in the liver, stored in the gall bladder", "Made in the gall bladder, stored in the liver", "Made in the pancreas, stored in the liver", "Made in the stomach, stored in the gall bladder"], "Bile is made in the liver and kept in the gall bladder until it is needed."], ["How does bile help lipase digest fat?", ["It breaks fat into small droplets, increasing surface area", "It is an enzyme that digests fat itself", "It makes the small intestine more acidic", "It turns fat into starch"], "Bile emulsifies fat into small droplets, giving lipase a bigger surface area to work on."]], "bonus": ["Which nutrient does your body mainly need for growth and repair?", ["Protein", "Carbohydrate", "Fat", "Fibre"], "Protein is used to build and repair cells, which is why protease and the Biuret test matter."]}]}, {"id": "chemistry", "name": "Chemistry", "weeks": [{"t": "Atomic structure", "intro": "Everything is made of atoms, and each atom has a tiny nucleus with electrons around it. Knowing what is inside atoms explains how elements react, and the story of how scientists found this out shows how ideas change with new evidence.", "how": ["Protons (charge +1, mass 1) and neutrons (charge 0, mass 1) are in the nucleus; electrons (charge −1, almost no mass) are in shells around it.", "Atomic number = number of protons, and in a neutral atom the number of electrons equals the number of protons.", "Mass number = protons + neutrons, so neutrons = mass number − atomic number.", "Electrons fill shells in order: 2 in the first shell, then up to 8 in the second and 8 in the third (for the first 20 elements).", "Isotopes are atoms of the same element with the same number of protons but different numbers of neutrons."], "example": {"q": "Sodium has atomic number 11 and mass number 23. How many protons, neutrons and electrons does a sodium atom have, and what is its electronic structure?", "steps": ["Protons = atomic number = 11.", "Neutrons = mass number − atomic number = 23 − 11 = 12.", "Electrons = protons in a neutral atom = 11.", "Fill the shells: 2 in the first, 8 in the second, and 1 left over, so 2,8,1."], "a": "Sodium has 11 protons, 12 neutrons and 11 electrons, arranged 2,8,1."}, "walk": [{"h": "Inside the atom", "b": "An atom has a tiny nucleus containing protons and neutrons, which holds almost all of its mass. Electrons move around the nucleus in shells, and most of the atom is empty space. Atoms have no overall charge because they have equal numbers of protons (+) and electrons (−); for example, carbon has 6 protons and 6 electrons."}, {"h": "Numbers and isotopes", "b": "The atomic number is the number of protons, and the mass number is protons plus neutrons. Chlorine-35 and chlorine-37 are isotopes: both have 17 protons, but they have 18 and 20 neutrons. Relative atomic mass is an average that takes into account how common each isotope is. If 75% is chlorine-35 and 25% is chlorine-37, it is (35 × 75 + 37 × 25) ÷ 100 = 35.5."}, {"h": "How the model changed", "b": "Dalton thought atoms were solid spheres that could not be divided. Thomson discovered the electron and suggested the plum pudding model: a ball of positive charge with electrons dotted through it. Rutherford's alpha scattering experiment found most alpha particles went straight through gold foil, so atoms are mostly empty space with a small, dense, positive nucleus. Bohr then placed electrons in shells, and Chadwick later discovered the neutron."}], "qs": [["What is the charge on a proton?", ["+1", "−1", "0", "+2"], "Protons have a relative charge of +1."], ["Where is almost all the mass of an atom?", ["In the nucleus", "In the electron shells", "Spread evenly through the atom", "In the empty space"], "Protons and neutrons are in the nucleus and electrons have almost no mass."], ["What does the atomic number tell you?", ["The number of protons", "The number of neutrons", "Protons plus neutrons", "The number of shells"], "The atomic number is the number of protons in the nucleus."], ["Aluminium has atomic number 13 and mass number 27. How many neutrons does it have?", ["14", "13", "27", "40"], "Neutrons = 27 − 13 = 14."], ["How many electrons are in a neutral magnesium atom (atomic number 12)?", ["12", "24", "2", "10"], "A neutral atom has the same number of electrons as protons, which is 12."], ["What are isotopes?", ["Atoms of an element with different numbers of neutrons", "Atoms of an element with different numbers of protons", "Atoms of different elements with the same mass", "Atoms that have lost electrons"], "Isotopes have the same number of protons but different numbers of neutrons."], ["What is the electronic structure of chlorine (atomic number 17)?", ["2,8,7", "2,7,8", "2,8,8", "8,8,1"], "17 electrons fill 2 in the first shell, 8 in the second and 7 in the third."], ["Who discovered the electron and suggested the plum pudding model?", ["Thomson", "Dalton", "Rutherford", "Chadwick"], "Thomson discovered the electron and pictured it dotted through a ball of positive charge."], ["Most alpha particles passed straight through gold foil. What did this show?", ["Atoms are mostly empty space", "Atoms are solid spheres", "Electrons are in shells", "The nucleus is negatively charged"], "If most particles pass straight through, most of the atom must be empty space."], ["An element is 75% isotope-35 and 25% isotope-37. What is its relative atomic mass?", ["35.5", "36.0", "36.5", "72.0"], "(35 × 75 + 37 × 25) ÷ 100 = 3550 ÷ 100 = 35.5."]], "bonus": ["What is an element?", ["A substance made of only one type of atom", "A mixture of two or more substances", "Different atoms chemically joined together", "Any liquid found in nature"], "An element contains only one type of atom, such as oxygen or iron."]}, {"t": "The periodic table", "intro": "The periodic table lists all the elements in order of atomic number, arranged so that elements with similar properties line up in columns called groups. Once you know the patterns, you can predict how an element will behave even if you have never seen it.", "how": ["Elements are arranged in order of atomic number; columns are groups and rows are periods.", "Elements in the same group have the same number of electrons in their outer shell, so they react in similar ways.", "Metals are on the left and in the middle and form positive ions; non-metals are on the right and do not form positive ions.", "Group 1 metals get more reactive going down the group, but Group 7 halogens get less reactive going down.", "Group 0 noble gases have full outer shells, so they are very unreactive."], "example": {"q": "Will bromine react with potassium iodide solution? If so, what forms?", "steps": ["Bromine and iodine are both halogens in Group 7.", "Reactivity decreases down Group 7, and bromine is above iodine, so bromine is more reactive.", "A more reactive halogen displaces a less reactive one from a solution of its salt.", "Bromine + potassium iodide → potassium bromide + iodine."], "a": "Yes: bromine displaces iodine, forming potassium bromide and iodine."}, "walk": [{"h": "Building the table", "b": "Mendeleev arranged the elements by their properties and left gaps for elements that had not been discovered yet, even predicting their properties. When those elements were found, they fitted his predictions, which convinced other scientists. Today the elements are arranged by atomic number, and for Groups 1 to 7 the group number tells you the number of outer electrons: sodium in Group 1 has 1 outer electron."}, {"h": "Group 1: alkali metals", "b": "Lithium, sodium and potassium are soft metals with 1 outer electron. They react with water to make a metal hydroxide and hydrogen, for example sodium + water → sodium hydroxide + hydrogen. They get more reactive going down the group because the outer electron is further from the nucleus, so it is lost more easily."}, {"h": "Groups 7 and 0", "b": "The halogens, such as chlorine and bromine, have 7 outer electrons and exist as pairs of atoms (diatomic molecules) like Cl₂. They get less reactive going down the group, and a more reactive halogen will displace a less reactive one from a solution of its salt. The noble gases in Group 0, such as helium, neon and argon, have full outer shells so they hardly react at all, and their boiling points increase going down the group."}], "qs": [["In what order are elements arranged in the modern periodic table?", ["Atomic number", "Mass number", "Alphabetical order", "Date of discovery"], "The modern periodic table is arranged in order of atomic number (number of protons)."], ["What do elements in the same group have in common?", ["The same number of outer electrons", "The same number of neutrons", "The same mass number", "The same number of shells"], "Elements in a group have the same number of outer electrons, so they react in similar ways."], ["What kind of ions do metals form?", ["Positive ions", "Negative ions", "Both positive and negative ions", "No ions at all"], "Metals lose electrons when they react, so they form positive ions."], ["What forms when sodium reacts with water?", ["Sodium hydroxide and hydrogen", "Sodium oxide and oxygen", "Sodium chloride and water", "Sodium hydroxide and oxygen"], "Group 1 metals react with water to form a metal hydroxide and hydrogen gas."], ["Why is potassium more reactive than sodium?", ["Its outer electron is further from the nucleus", "It has more outer electrons", "It has a full outer shell", "Its outer electron is closer to the nucleus"], "Potassium's outer electron is further from the nucleus, so it is lost more easily."], ["How do the halogens exist as elements?", ["As diatomic molecules, like Cl₂", "As single atoms, like Cl", "As positive ions, like Cl⁺", "As giant metal lattices"], "Halogen atoms pair up to form diatomic molecules such as Cl₂ and Br₂."], ["What happens to the reactivity of the halogens going down Group 7?", ["It decreases", "It increases", "It stays the same", "It goes up then down"], "Halogens get less reactive going down Group 7."], ["Chlorine is bubbled into sodium bromide solution. What forms?", ["Sodium chloride and bromine", "Sodium bromide and chlorine", "No reaction happens", "Sodium bromide and hydrogen"], "Chlorine is more reactive than bromine, so it displaces bromine from sodium bromide."], ["Why are the noble gases so unreactive?", ["They have full outer shells", "They have one outer electron", "They are all metals", "They have no electrons"], "With a full outer shell, noble gas atoms do not need to gain, lose or share electrons."], ["Why did Mendeleev leave gaps in his periodic table?", ["For elements not yet discovered", "Because he ran out of space", "To separate metals from non-metals", "For elements he thought did not exist"], "He left gaps for undiscovered elements and predicted their properties, which later proved correct."]], "bonus": ["What is the chemical symbol for sodium?", ["Na", "So", "S", "Sd"], "Sodium's symbol Na comes from its Latin name, natrium."]}, {"t": "Bonding", "intro": "Atoms join together by bonding, and the type of bond explains a substance's properties, like why salt has a high melting point but water boils at only 100 °C. There are three main types: ionic, covalent and metallic.", "how": ["Ionic bonding happens between a metal and a non-metal: the metal loses electrons and the non-metal gains them.", "The ions formed have opposite charges, so they attract strongly in a giant ionic lattice.", "Covalent bonding happens between non-metals: atoms share pairs of electrons.", "Metallic bonding is a lattice of positive metal ions in a sea of delocalised electrons.", "To write an ionic formula, balance the charges so they add up to zero."], "example": {"q": "What is the formula of magnesium chloride?", "steps": ["Magnesium is in Group 2, so it loses 2 electrons to form Mg²⁺.", "Chlorine is in Group 7, so it gains 1 electron to form Cl⁻.", "To cancel the 2+ charge, you need two Cl⁻ ions.", "So the formula is MgCl₂."], "a": "The formula of magnesium chloride is MgCl₂."}, "walk": [{"h": "Ionic bonding", "b": "When sodium reacts with chlorine, sodium loses its 1 outer electron to become Na⁺ and chlorine gains it to become Cl⁻. Metals in Groups 1, 2 and 3 form 1+, 2+ and 3+ ions, while non-metals in Groups 6 and 7 form 2− and 1− ions. The oppositely charged ions are held in a giant lattice by strong electrostatic forces, so ionic compounds have high melting points. They conduct electricity when melted or dissolved, because the ions are then free to move."}, {"h": "Covalent bonding", "b": "Non-metal atoms share pairs of electrons, and each shared pair is one covalent bond, as in H₂O or CH₄. Small molecules have strong bonds inside them but only weak forces between molecules, so they have low melting and boiling points and do not conduct. Giant covalent structures like diamond and silicon dioxide have millions of strong bonds, so they have very high melting points. Graphite is giant covalent too, but each carbon makes only 3 bonds, leaving one delocalised electron per atom, so graphite conducts electricity."}, {"h": "Metallic bonding", "b": "In a metal, the outer electrons leave their atoms and become delocalised, free to move through the whole structure. The positive metal ions are held together by their attraction to this sea of electrons. Because the electrons can move, metals conduct electricity and heat, and because the layers of ions can slide over each other, metals can be bent and shaped."}], "qs": [["Ionic bonding happens between which elements?", ["A metal and a non-metal", "Two non-metals", "Two metals", "Two noble gases"], "In ionic bonding, a metal gives electrons to a non-metal."], ["What is a covalent bond?", ["A shared pair of electrons", "An electron passed from one atom to another", "An attraction between oppositely charged ions", "A sea of delocalised electrons"], "A covalent bond forms when two non-metal atoms share a pair of electrons."], ["What ion does a sodium atom form?", ["Na⁺", "Na⁻", "Na²⁺", "Na²⁻"], "Sodium is in Group 1, so it loses 1 electron and forms Na⁺."], ["What ion does an oxygen atom (Group 6) form?", ["O²⁻", "O²⁺", "O⁻", "O⁶⁺"], "Oxygen gains 2 electrons to fill its outer shell, forming O²⁻."], ["Why does molten sodium chloride conduct electricity?", ["Its ions are free to move", "Its electrons are delocalised", "Its molecules are free to move", "Its covalent bonds break"], "When ionic compounds melt, the charged ions can move and carry the current."], ["Why does water have a low boiling point?", ["The forces between molecules are weak", "Its covalent bonds are weak", "It is made of ions", "It has delocalised electrons"], "Boiling only overcomes the weak forces between molecules, not the strong covalent bonds."], ["Which substance has a giant covalent structure?", ["Diamond", "Water", "Methane", "Sodium chloride"], "Diamond is a giant structure of carbon atoms joined by strong covalent bonds."], ["Why can metals conduct electricity?", ["They have delocalised electrons", "They have ions that are free to move", "They have shared pairs of electrons", "Their atoms have full outer shells"], "Delocalised electrons can move through the metal and carry the current."], ["Why does graphite conduct electricity?", ["Each carbon has one delocalised electron", "Each carbon atom forms 4 bonds", "It is a metal", "It contains ions"], "Each carbon in graphite forms 3 bonds, leaving one electron free to move."], ["Aluminium forms Al³⁺ and oxygen forms O²⁻. What is the formula of aluminium oxide?", ["Al₂O₃", "AlO", "Al₃O₂", "AlO₃"], "Two Al³⁺ give 6+ and three O²⁻ give 6−, so the charges cancel in Al₂O₃."]], "bonus": ["What is a compound?", ["Two or more elements chemically joined", "A substance made of one type of atom", "A mixture that can be filtered", "Any metal that conducts"], "A compound contains two or more different elements chemically joined, like water (H₂O)."]}, {"t": "States of matter and separating mixtures", "intro": "Substances can be solids, liquids or gases, and the particle model explains how they change state. Most things around us are mixtures, so chemists need ways to separate them, such as filtering, distilling and chromatography.", "how": ["In solids particles are close and fixed, in liquids close but moving past each other, and in gases far apart and moving fast.", "Below its melting point a substance is solid, between its melting and boiling points it is liquid, and above its boiling point it is a gas.", "A pure substance is one element or compound and has a sharp, fixed melting point; a mixture melts over a range.", "Use filtration for an insoluble solid, crystallisation for a dissolved solid, and distillation to collect a liquid.", "In chromatography, Rf = distance moved by the substance ÷ distance moved by the solvent."], "example": {"q": "In a chromatogram, a dye spot moved 3.0 cm and the solvent front moved 7.5 cm. What is the Rf value?", "steps": ["Rf = distance moved by substance ÷ distance moved by solvent.", "Rf = 3.0 ÷ 7.5.", "3.0 ÷ 7.5 = 0.4.", "An Rf value is always between 0 and 1, so 0.4 makes sense."], "a": "The Rf value is 0.4."}, "walk": [{"h": "Particles and states", "b": "Solid particles are packed in a regular pattern and only vibrate, liquid particles are close together but move past each other, and gas particles are far apart and move quickly in all directions. Melting is solid to liquid, boiling is liquid to gas, condensing is gas to liquid, and freezing is liquid to solid. State symbols show the state in equations: (s) solid, (l) liquid, (g) gas and (aq) dissolved in water."}, {"h": "Pure substances and mixtures", "b": "In chemistry, a pure substance contains only one element or one compound, so it melts and boils at sharp, fixed temperatures. A mixture contains different substances that are not chemically joined, so they can be separated by physical methods. Filtration separates an insoluble solid like sand from a liquid. Crystallisation gets a dissolved solid like salt back out: heat the solution to evaporate some water, then leave it to cool so crystals form."}, {"h": "Distillation and chromatography", "b": "Simple distillation gets a pure liquid, like water from seawater: the water boils off, then the vapour cools and condenses in a condenser. Fractional distillation separates liquids that mix, like ethanol and water, because they have different boiling points. Paper chromatography separates the dyes in an ink, because each dye moves a different distance up the paper. A spot that moves 2 cm while the solvent moves 8 cm has Rf = 2 ÷ 8 = 0.25."}], "qs": [["How are particles arranged in a solid?", ["Close together in a regular pattern", "Far apart and moving quickly", "Close together and moving past each other", "Far apart in a regular pattern"], "Solid particles are tightly packed in a regular pattern and only vibrate."], ["What is the change from a gas to a liquid called?", ["Condensing", "Freezing", "Melting", "Evaporating"], "Condensing is when a gas cools and turns into a liquid."], ["A substance melts at −7 °C and boils at 59 °C. What state is it in at 20 °C?", ["Liquid", "Solid", "Gas", "Both solid and gas"], "20 °C is above its melting point but below its boiling point, so it is a liquid."], ["What does the state symbol (aq) mean?", ["Dissolved in water", "Liquid", "Gas", "Solid"], "(aq) stands for aqueous, which means dissolved in water."], ["Which method separates sand from water?", ["Filtration", "Crystallisation", "Chromatography", "Fractional distillation"], "Sand is an insoluble solid, so it is caught by the filter paper while water passes through."], ["How can you get salt crystals from salt solution?", ["Crystallisation", "Filtration", "Chromatography", "Condensing"], "Crystallisation evaporates some water and leaves the solution to cool so salt crystals form."], ["Which method gets pure water from seawater?", ["Simple distillation", "Filtration", "Chromatography", "Crystallisation"], "Simple distillation boils off the water and condenses it, leaving the salt behind."], ["Which method separates a mixture of ethanol and water?", ["Fractional distillation", "Filtration", "Crystallisation", "Chromatography"], "Ethanol and water mix together but have different boiling points, so fractional distillation separates them."], ["How can you tell a substance is pure?", ["It has a sharp, fixed melting point", "It melts over a range of temperatures", "It is a clear liquid", "It dissolves in water"], "A pure substance melts at one exact temperature, while a mixture melts over a range."], ["A spot moves 3.6 cm and the solvent moves 8.0 cm. What is the Rf value?", ["0.45", "2.22", "4.4", "0.36"], "Rf = 3.6 ÷ 8.0 = 0.45."]], "bonus": ["What happens to particles when a substance is heated?", ["They gain energy and move faster", "They get bigger", "They lose energy and slow down", "They turn into different particles"], "Heating gives particles more energy, so they move or vibrate faster."]}]}, {"id": "physics", "name": "Physics", "weeks": [{"t": "Energy stores and transfers", "intro": "Energy is never used up. It just moves from one store to another. Understanding where energy goes helps us design better machines, save money on bills and see why nothing can run forever.", "how": ["Name the energy stores: kinetic, gravitational potential, elastic potential, thermal, chemical, magnetic, electrostatic and nuclear.", "Energy moves between stores by four pathways: mechanically (a force), electrically, by heating and by radiation (like light or sound waves).", "Energy cannot be created or destroyed, only transferred, stored or dissipated (spread out).", "Energy that is not useful is called wasted energy and usually ends up in the thermal store of the surroundings.", "Total energy in = useful energy out + wasted energy out."], "example": {"q": "A lamp is given 100 J of energy electrically. It gives out 20 J of useful light. How much energy is wasted, and where does it go?", "steps": ["Energy cannot be destroyed, so all 100 J must go somewhere.", "Useful energy out = 20 J (light).", "Wasted energy = total in − useful out = 100 − 20 = 80 J.", "The wasted energy heats the lamp and the room, so it ends up in the thermal store of the surroundings."], "a": "80 J is wasted, and it ends up in the thermal store of the surroundings."}, "walk": [{"h": "Energy stores", "b": "An energy store is a place energy is kept. A moving bike has energy in its kinetic store. A book on a high shelf has energy in its gravitational potential store, and a battery has energy in its chemical store."}, {"h": "Energy pathways", "b": "Energy moves between stores along pathways. When you kick a ball, energy goes mechanically from your chemical store to the ball's kinetic store. A kettle transfers energy electrically, then by heating, into the thermal store of the water."}, {"h": "Conservation and waste", "b": "The total amount of energy always stays the same, but some spreads out in ways that are not useful. When a car brakes, friction moves energy from the car's kinetic store into the thermal store of the brakes and the air. We reduce waste with lubrication (less friction) and thermal insulation (less heat loss)."}], "qs": [["Which of these is an energy store?", ["Kinetic", "Electrical", "Sound", "Light"], "Kinetic is a store; electrical, sound and light describe ways energy is transferred."], ["A stretched spring stores energy in which store?", ["Elastic potential", "Gravitational potential", "Kinetic", "Chemical"], "Stretching, squashing or bending an object stores energy in its elastic potential store."], ["What does the law of conservation of energy say?", ["Energy cannot be created or destroyed", "Energy is used up when things move", "Energy is only made by power stations", "Energy always turns into light"], "Energy can only be transferred, stored or dissipated, never made or destroyed."], ["A ball is thrown straight up. As it rises, energy moves from…", ["Kinetic store to gravitational potential store", "Gravitational potential store to kinetic store", "Thermal store to kinetic store", "Chemical store to elastic store"], "The ball slows as it rises, so its kinetic store empties into its gravitational potential store."], ["A battery powers a motor. How is energy transferred from the battery?", ["Electrically", "By radiation", "By heating", "By sound"], "A current in a circuit transfers energy electrically."], ["When a car brakes, where does most of its kinetic energy go?", ["Thermal store of the brakes and surroundings", "Chemical store of the fuel", "Elastic store of the tyres", "Nuclear store of the engine"], "Friction in the brakes heats them up, moving energy into thermal stores."], ["Wasted energy usually ends up in…", ["The thermal store of the surroundings", "The chemical store of the device", "The kinetic store of the device", "Nowhere, it is destroyed"], "Wasted energy spreads out and slightly warms the surroundings."], ["A TV is given 200 J and gives out 50 J as useful light and sound. How much is wasted?", ["150 J", "250 J", "50 J", "4 J"], "Wasted energy = 200 J − 50 J = 150 J."], ["Which change reduces energy wasted by friction in a bike chain?", ["Oiling (lubricating) the chain", "Adding more weight to the bike", "Making the chain rusty", "Pedalling faster"], "Lubrication reduces friction, so less energy is dissipated as heat."], ["Why do houses lose less heat if walls are thicker and made of low thermal conductivity materials?", ["Energy is transferred through them more slowly", "They create extra energy", "They destroy the heat", "They make the house hotter than the heater"], "Thick, low-conductivity walls slow the rate of energy transfer by heating."]], "bonus": ["What is the unit of energy?", ["Joule (J)", "Newton (N)", "Watt (W)", "Kilogram (kg)"], "Energy is measured in joules; newtons measure force and watts measure power."]}, {"t": "Energy calculations", "intro": "Physicists don't just say energy moves, they work out how much. In this topic you use a few key equations to calculate kinetic energy, gravitational potential energy, power and efficiency. Always show your working and include units.", "how": ["Kinetic energy: Ek = ½ × m × v², where m is mass in kg and v is speed in m/s.", "Gravitational potential energy: Ep = m × g × h, with g = 9.8 N/kg and h in metres.", "Power = energy transferred ÷ time, measured in watts (1 W = 1 J per second).", "Efficiency = useful energy out ÷ total energy in; multiply by 100 for a percentage.", "Write the equation, put in the numbers, then calculate and give the unit."], "example": {"q": "A 50 kg student climbs stairs 3 m high in 6 s. How much gravitational potential energy does she gain, and what is her power?", "steps": ["Use Ep = m × g × h.", "Ep = 50 × 9.8 × 3 = 1470 J.", "Use power = energy ÷ time.", "Power = 1470 ÷ 6 = 245 W."], "a": "She gains 1470 J of gravitational potential energy and her power is 245 W."}, "walk": [{"h": "Kinetic energy", "b": "Ek = ½ × m × v². Square the speed first, then multiply by the mass and halve it. A 1000 kg car at 10 m/s has Ek = ½ × 1000 × 10² = ½ × 1000 × 100 = 50 000 J."}, {"h": "Gravitational potential energy", "b": "Ep = m × g × h tells you the energy gained when something is lifted. Lifting a 2 kg book up 1.5 m gives Ep = 2 × 9.8 × 1.5 = 29.4 J."}, {"h": "Power and efficiency", "b": "Power is how fast energy is transferred: 300 J in 10 s is 30 W. Efficiency shows how much energy is useful: a motor given 200 J that does 150 J of useful work is 150 ÷ 200 = 0.75, or 75% efficient. Efficiency can never be more than 100%."}], "qs": [["What is the unit of power?", ["Watt (W)", "Joule (J)", "Newton (N)", "Volt (V)"], "Power is energy per second, measured in watts."], ["What is the kinetic energy of a 4 kg ball moving at 2 m/s?", ["8 J", "16 J", "4 J", "32 J"], "Ek = ½ × 4 × 2² = ½ × 4 × 4 = 8 J."], ["A 5 kg box is lifted 2 m. How much GPE does it gain? (g = 9.8 N/kg)", ["98 J", "10 J", "49 J", "196 J"], "Ep = 5 × 9.8 × 2 = 98 J."], ["A motor transfers 600 J in 20 s. What is its power?", ["30 W", "12 000 W", "0.03 W", "620 W"], "Power = 600 ÷ 20 = 30 W."], ["A device takes in 400 J and gives 100 J of useful energy. What is its efficiency?", ["25%", "75%", "4%", "300%"], "Efficiency = 100 ÷ 400 = 0.25 = 25%."], ["If an object's speed doubles, its kinetic energy…", ["Becomes 4 times bigger", "Doubles", "Stays the same", "Becomes 8 times bigger"], "Ek depends on v², and 2² = 4, so the kinetic energy is 4 times bigger."], ["A 2000 W kettle is on for 60 s. How much energy does it transfer?", ["120 000 J", "33.3 J", "2060 J", "2000 J"], "Energy = power × time = 2000 × 60 = 120 000 J."], ["Why can no machine be more than 100% efficient?", ["Energy cannot be created", "Machines are too heavy", "Power is always too low", "Friction creates extra energy"], "Useful energy out can never be more than energy in, because energy cannot be created."], ["A motor is 0.8 efficient and is given 500 J. How much useful energy does it give out?", ["400 J", "100 J", "625 J", "580 J"], "Useful out = efficiency × total in = 0.8 × 500 = 400 J."], ["A 0.5 kg ball falls 10 m with no air resistance. How fast is it going at the bottom?", ["14 m/s", "98 m/s", "7 m/s", "196 m/s"], "Ep = 0.5 × 9.8 × 10 = 49 J = ½ × 0.5 × v², so v² = 196 and v = 14 m/s."]], "bonus": ["What is 6²?", ["36", "12", "8", "66"], "6² means 6 × 6 = 36; squaring is needed for the v² in kinetic energy."]}, {"t": "Electric circuits", "intro": "Electric circuits power everything from torches to phones. In this topic you learn what current, potential difference and resistance are, and how they link together in the equation V = I × R. You also compare series and parallel circuits.", "how": ["Current (I) is the flow of charge, measured in amps (A) with an ammeter placed in series.", "Potential difference (V) is the energy push, measured in volts (V) with a voltmeter placed in parallel.", "Resistance (R) opposes current, measured in ohms (Ω); use V = I × R.", "In series: the current is the same everywhere, the p.d. is shared, and total resistance = R₁ + R₂.", "In parallel: each branch gets the full p.d., the current splits between branches, and total resistance goes down."], "example": {"q": "A 4 Ω and a 6 Ω resistor are in series with a 12 V battery. What is the current, and what is the p.d. across the 6 Ω resistor?", "steps": ["In series, add the resistances: 4 + 6 = 10 Ω.", "Rearrange V = I × R to I = V ÷ R.", "I = 12 ÷ 10 = 1.2 A, and this is the same everywhere in series.", "P.d. across 6 Ω: V = I × R = 1.2 × 6 = 7.2 V."], "a": "The current is 1.2 A and the p.d. across the 6 Ω resistor is 7.2 V."}, "walk": [{"h": "Current and potential difference", "b": "Current is how much charge flows each second, like water flowing in a pipe. Potential difference is the push that drives it, given by the battery. An ammeter goes in series in the loop, but a voltmeter goes in parallel across a component."}, {"h": "Using V = I × R", "b": "Resistance makes it harder for current to flow. The three quantities are linked by V = I × R. If 2 A flows through a 5 Ω resistor, the p.d. is 2 × 5 = 10 V. Rearrange to I = V ÷ R or R = V ÷ I when needed."}, {"h": "Series and parallel", "b": "In a series circuit there is one loop: the same current goes through every component and the p.d. is shared. In a parallel circuit there are branches: each branch gets the full battery p.d. and the currents in the branches add up to the total. Adding resistors in parallel lowers the total resistance."}], "qs": [["What is the unit of resistance?", ["Ohm (Ω)", "Amp (A)", "Volt (V)", "Watt (W)"], "Resistance is measured in ohms."], ["How should an ammeter be connected?", ["In series with the component", "In parallel with the component", "Across the battery only", "It does not matter"], "An ammeter measures the current flowing through, so it goes in the loop in series."], ["A current of 3 A flows through a 4 Ω resistor. What is the p.d. across it?", ["12 V", "0.75 V", "7 V", "1.33 V"], "V = I × R = 3 × 4 = 12 V."], ["There is 6 V across a 2 Ω resistor. What current flows?", ["3 A", "12 A", "0.33 A", "8 A"], "I = V ÷ R = 6 ÷ 2 = 3 A."], ["A heater has 230 V across it and a current of 0.5 A. What is its resistance?", ["460 Ω", "115 Ω", "230.5 Ω", "0.002 Ω"], "R = V ÷ I = 230 ÷ 0.5 = 460 Ω."], ["In a series circuit the current at one point is 0.4 A. What is it at another point?", ["0.4 A", "0.2 A", "0.8 A", "0 A"], "In series the current is the same everywhere in the loop."], ["What is the total resistance of a 3 Ω and a 5 Ω resistor in series?", ["8 Ω", "15 Ω", "2 Ω", "1.875 Ω"], "In series, total resistance = 3 + 5 = 8 Ω."], ["Two lamps are in parallel, directly across a 6 V battery. What is the p.d. across each lamp?", ["6 V", "3 V", "12 V", "0 V"], "Each parallel branch gets the full battery p.d."], ["What happens to the total resistance when you add another resistor in parallel?", ["It decreases", "It increases", "It stays the same", "It doubles"], "A new branch gives the current another path, so total resistance goes down."], ["A 2 Ω and a 4 Ω resistor are in series with a 12 V battery. What is the p.d. across the 4 Ω resistor?", ["8 V", "4 V", "6 V", "12 V"], "Total R = 6 Ω, so I = 12 ÷ 6 = 2 A, and V = 2 × 4 = 8 V."]], "bonus": ["What does a circuit need for a current to flow?", ["A complete loop with no gaps", "A switch that is open", "Only a bulb", "Wires made of plastic"], "Current can only flow if there is a complete closed path back to the battery."]}, {"t": "Particle model and density", "intro": "Everything is made of tiny particles, and how they are arranged explains why solids, liquids and gases behave differently. This model also explains density and what happens to energy when we heat things up.", "how": ["Density = mass ÷ volume, in kg/m³ or g/cm³.", "Solids: particles close together in a regular pattern, vibrating. Liquids: close together but random, moving past each other. Gases: far apart, moving fast and randomly.", "Internal energy is the total kinetic and potential energy of all the particles in a substance.", "Heating a substance either raises its temperature or changes its state, not both at the same time.", "Changes of state are physical changes: mass stays the same and the change can be reversed."], "example": {"q": "A block of aluminium has a mass of 54 g and a volume of 20 cm³. What is its density?", "steps": ["Write the equation: density = mass ÷ volume.", "Put in the numbers: density = 54 ÷ 20.", "Calculate: 54 ÷ 20 = 2.7.", "Mass in g and volume in cm³ give units of g/cm³."], "a": "The density of aluminium is 2.7 g/cm³."}, "walk": [{"h": "Three states of matter", "b": "In a solid the particles are packed tightly and only vibrate. In a liquid they are still close but can slide past each other, so liquids flow. In a gas the particles are far apart and zoom around, so gases spread out to fill their container."}, {"h": "Working out density", "b": "Density tells you how much mass is packed into a space. A stone of 45 g that pushes water in a measuring cylinder up from 50 cm³ to 65 cm³ has a volume of 15 cm³, so its density is 45 ÷ 15 = 3 g/cm³. Gases have low density because their particles are spread out."}, {"h": "Internal energy and changes of state", "b": "Heating gives particles more energy, which increases internal energy. Usually this makes the temperature rise. But while ice is melting, the energy is used to break the bonds between particles, so the temperature stays at 0 °C until it has all melted."}], "qs": [["What is the equation for density?", ["Density = mass ÷ volume", "Density = volume ÷ mass", "Density = mass × volume", "Density = mass + volume"], "Density is the mass per unit volume."], ["An object has a mass of 120 g and a volume of 40 cm³. What is its density?", ["3 g/cm³", "4800 g/cm³", "0.33 g/cm³", "160 g/cm³"], "Density = 120 ÷ 40 = 3 g/cm³."], ["In which state are particles far apart and moving fast and randomly?", ["Gas", "Solid", "Liquid", "All three states"], "Gas particles are widely spaced and move quickly in all directions."], ["Why is a gas much less dense than the same substance as a solid?", ["Its particles are much further apart", "Its particles are much smaller", "Its particles have less mass each", "It has fewer protons"], "The same mass of particles is spread over a far bigger volume."], ["What is internal energy?", ["Total kinetic and potential energy of the particles", "The temperature of the object", "The kinetic energy of the whole object moving", "Energy from chemical reactions only"], "Internal energy is the energy stored by all the particles in a system."], ["100 g of ice melts completely. What is the mass of the water?", ["100 g", "More than 100 g", "Less than 100 g", "0 g"], "Mass is conserved in a change of state because no particles are added or lost."], ["Ice at 0 °C is heated while it melts. What happens to its temperature?", ["It stays at 0 °C until all is melted", "It rises steadily", "It falls below 0 °C", "It jumps to 100 °C"], "During melting the energy breaks bonds between particles instead of raising temperature."], ["What is the change from a gas to a liquid called?", ["Condensing", "Evaporating", "Freezing", "Melting"], "When a gas cools and turns into a liquid it condenses."], ["Water has a density of 1000 kg/m³. What is the mass of 0.002 m³ of water?", ["2 kg", "500 000 kg", "0.000002 kg", "1000.002 kg"], "Mass = density × volume = 1000 × 0.002 = 2 kg."], ["A 45 g stone raises water in a measuring cylinder from 50 cm³ to 65 cm³. What is its density?", ["3 g/cm³", "0.9 g/cm³", "0.69 g/cm³", "0.33 g/cm³"], "Volume = 65 − 50 = 15 cm³, so density = 45 ÷ 15 = 3 g/cm³."]], "bonus": ["What is the volume of a cuboid 2 cm by 3 cm by 4 cm?", ["24 cm³", "9 cm³", "52 cm³", "12 cm³"], "Volume = length × width × height = 2 × 3 × 4 = 24 cm³."]}]}, {"id": "history", "name": "History", "weeks": [{"t": "The Weimar Republic 1918–1923", "intro": "After losing the First World War in 1918, Germany's Kaiser gave up the throne and Germany became a democracy called the Weimar Republic. Its first five years were full of crises, and understanding them helps explain how Hitler later rose to power.", "how": ["Remember the key dates: armistice on 11 November 1918, Treaty of Versailles signed 28 June 1919.", "Learn the Treaty terms with LAMB: Land, Army, Money (reparations), Blame (the War Guilt Clause).", "Know the threats from the left (Spartacists, 1919) and the right (Kapp Putsch, 1920).", "Understand the chain of events in 1923: missed payments, the Ruhr invasion, then hyperinflation.", "When explaining a problem, always say who it affected and why it made people angry."], "example": {"q": "Explain why hyperinflation happened in Germany in 1923.", "steps": ["Germany fell behind on its reparations payments in late 1922.", "In January 1923, French and Belgian troops occupied the Ruhr, Germany's main industrial area, to take goods instead.", "The government told Ruhr workers to go on strike (passive resistance) and kept paying them.", "With fewer goods being made, the government printed huge amounts of money to pay the workers.", "Too much money chasing too few goods made prices soar: by November 1923 a loaf of bread cost about 200 billion marks."], "a": "Hyperinflation happened because the government printed vast amounts of money after the Ruhr invasion, so the mark became almost worthless."}, "walk": [{"h": "A new democracy", "b": "The Kaiser abdicated on 9 November 1918 and the armistice was signed two days later. The new constitution, written in the town of Weimar in 1919, gave all adults over 20 the vote and used proportional representation. Its weakness was Article 48, which let the President rule by decree in an emergency."}, {"h": "The hated Treaty", "b": "The Treaty of Versailles took about 13% of Germany's land, cut its army to 100,000 men and banned tanks and an air force. Article 231, the War Guilt Clause, blamed Germany for the war, and reparations were later set at £6.6 billion. Many Germans called it a Diktat (dictated peace) and blamed the ‘November Criminals’ who had signed the armistice, spreading the false ‘stab in the back’ myth."}, {"h": "Uprisings and 1923", "b": "In January 1919 the communist Spartacists tried to seize power in Berlin but were crushed by the Freikorps (ex-soldiers). In March 1920 the right-wing Kapp Putsch took Berlin until a general strike by workers defeated it. In 1923 hyperinflation wiped out people's savings, hurting the middle classes most, though people with debts could pay them off easily."}], "qs": [["When was the armistice that ended fighting in the First World War?", ["11 November 1918", "28 June 1919", "1 September 1939", "9 November 1923"], "The armistice came into effect at 11am on 11 November 1918."], ["Who led Germany before the Weimar Republic?", ["Kaiser Wilhelm II", "Adolf Hitler", "Friedrich Ebert", "Gustav Stresemann"], "Kaiser Wilhelm II abdicated on 9 November 1918."], ["What was the maximum size of Germany's army under the Treaty?", ["100,000 men", "10,000 men", "500,000 men", "1 million men"], "The Treaty of Versailles limited the German army to 100,000 men."], ["What did Article 231 of the Treaty of Versailles do?", ["Blamed Germany for starting the war", "Gave the President emergency powers", "Banned Germany from having a navy", "Made Germany pay for the Ruhr"], "Article 231 was the War Guilt Clause, which humiliated many Germans."], ["At what amount were reparations set in 1921?", ["£6.6 billion", "£66 million", "£660 billion", "£6.6 million"], "The Reparations Commission set the total at £6.6 billion in 1921."], ["Which group tried to start a communist revolution in Berlin in January 1919?", ["The Spartacists", "The Freikorps", "The Nazis", "The SS"], "The Spartacists, led by Rosa Luxemburg and Karl Liebknecht, were crushed by the Freikorps."], ["How was the Kapp Putsch of 1920 defeated?", ["A general strike by Berlin's workers", "The army fought and won", "The Allies invaded", "Hitler arrested the leaders"], "Workers went on strike, so Berlin had no power or transport and Kapp gave up."], ["Why did France and Belgium occupy the Ruhr in 1923?", ["Germany had failed to pay reparations", "To stop a communist revolution", "To help Germany's economy", "Germany had invaded Belgium again"], "They took goods from the Ruhr's mines and factories because Germany missed payments."], ["Who was hurt most by hyperinflation in 1923?", ["People with savings, like the middle classes", "People with large debts", "Farmers who grew their own food", "Foreigners holding dollars"], "Savings became worthless, while debts became easy to pay off."], ["What was the ‘stab in the back’ myth?", ["A false claim Germany lost because politicians betrayed the army", "A true story about a murdered Kaiser", "The French plan to invade the Ruhr", "A plot to kill President Ebert"], "Many falsely claimed the army was undefeated and betrayed by the ‘November Criminals’."]], "bonus": ["What does ‘democracy’ mean?", ["A system where people vote for their government", "Rule by one person with total power", "Rule by a king or queen who inherits power", "Rule by the army"], "In a democracy, citizens choose who governs them by voting in elections."]}, {"t": "Hitler's rise to power 1923–1933", "intro": "In 1923 Hitler was a failed rebel in prison, but by January 1933 he was Chancellor of Germany. This topic explains how the Nazis went from a small party to the largest in the Reichstag.", "how": ["Know what happened in the Munich Putsch (November 1923) and why it helped Hitler in the long run.", "Understand that the Nazis did badly in the ‘Golden Years’ of 1924–1929, when Germany recovered under Stresemann.", "Link the Wall Street Crash (October 1929) and the Great Depression to rising Nazi votes.", "Learn the key election results: 12 seats in 1928, 230 seats in July 1932.", "Remember Hitler did not win power by force: he was appointed Chancellor by Hindenburg on 30 January 1933."], "example": {"q": "Explain why the Great Depression helped Hitler gain support.", "steps": ["The Wall Street Crash in October 1929 made US banks call in their loans to Germany.", "German businesses closed and unemployment rose to about 6 million by 1932.", "The Weimar government seemed unable to fix the crisis, so people turned to extreme parties.", "Hitler promised ‘work and bread’ and used propaganda to blame others for Germany's problems.", "Nazi seats in the Reichstag rose from 12 in 1928 to 230 in July 1932."], "a": "The Depression caused mass unemployment and anger at the government, so many desperate Germans turned to Hitler's promises."}, "walk": [{"h": "The Munich Putsch", "b": "On 8–9 November 1923, Hitler tried to seize power in Munich, starting in a beer hall. The police stopped the march, killing 16 Nazis, and Hitler was arrested. His trial gave him national publicity, and in Landsberg prison he wrote Mein Kampf and decided to win power through elections instead."}, {"h": "Recovery, then crash", "b": "From 1924 to 1929, Gustav Stresemann helped Germany recover with the Dawes Plan (US loans) and the Rentenmark, a new currency. Life improved, so few people voted Nazi: just 12 seats in 1928. Then the Wall Street Crash of October 1929 caused the Great Depression, and unemployment in Germany shot up."}, {"h": "Becoming Chancellor", "b": "In July 1932 the Nazis won 230 seats, making them the largest party in the Reichstag. President Hindenburg disliked Hitler but politicians von Papen and von Schleicher thought they could control him. On 30 January 1933 Hindenburg appointed Hitler Chancellor, with von Papen as Vice-Chancellor."}], "qs": [["In which city did the 1923 Putsch take place?", ["Munich", "Berlin", "Weimar", "Hamburg"], "The Munich Putsch began in a beer hall in Munich, Bavaria."], ["Which book did Hitler write in prison?", ["Mein Kampf", "The Communist Manifesto", "Daemonologie", "The Prince"], "Hitler wrote Mein Kampf (‘My Struggle’) in Landsberg prison."], ["Who helped Germany recover during the ‘Golden Years’ 1924–1929?", ["Gustav Stresemann", "Joseph Goebbels", "Ernst Röhm", "Wolfgang Kapp"], "Stresemann introduced the Rentenmark and agreed the Dawes Plan."], ["What was the Dawes Plan (1924)?", ["US loans and easier reparations payments", "A Nazi plan to seize power", "A plan to invade the Ruhr", "A new German constitution"], "It gave Germany US loans and spread out reparations to help recovery."], ["When did the Wall Street Crash happen?", ["October 1929", "January 1933", "November 1923", "June 1919"], "The US stock market crashed in October 1929, starting the Great Depression."], ["About how many Germans were unemployed by 1932?", ["6 million", "600,000", "60 million", "1 million"], "Unemployment reached about 6 million in 1932."], ["How many Reichstag seats did the Nazis win in 1928?", ["12", "230", "107", "288"], "In 1928, during the good years, the Nazis won only 12 seats."], ["How many seats did the Nazis win in July 1932?", ["230", "12", "107", "196"], "230 seats made the Nazis the largest party in the Reichstag."], ["Who appointed Hitler Chancellor?", ["President Hindenburg", "Gustav Stresemann", "Franz von Papen", "Friedrich Ebert"], "President Hindenburg appointed Hitler on 30 January 1933."], ["Why did von Papen persuade Hindenburg to make Hitler Chancellor?", ["He thought he could control Hitler", "He was a loyal Nazi party member", "Hitler had won a majority of seats", "The army forced him to"], "Von Papen believed Hitler could be used and controlled, a huge miscalculation."]], "bonus": ["What does ‘unemployment’ mean?", ["Being without a paid job", "Earning a very high wage", "Working two jobs at once", "Being retired from work"], "Unemployed people want to work but have no paid job."]}, {"t": "Creating the Nazi dictatorship", "intro": "Hitler became Chancellor in January 1933, and within about 18 months he had become a dictator with total power. This topic shows how he removed opposition, legally and by force, and then kept control through terror and propaganda.", "how": ["Learn the order of events: Reichstag Fire, Enabling Act, banning trade unions and other parties, Night of the Long Knives, Hindenburg's death.", "Understand how the Nazis used each event to grab more power.", "Know the difference between the SA, the SS and the Gestapo.", "Remember Goebbels ran propaganda and censorship to control what people thought.", "Explain how terror (fear) and propaganda (persuasion) worked together."], "example": {"q": "How did the Reichstag Fire help Hitler gain more power?", "steps": ["On 27 February 1933 the Reichstag (parliament) building burned down.", "A Dutch communist, Marinus van der Lubbe, was found at the scene and blamed.", "Hitler claimed communists were plotting a revolution and persuaded Hindenburg to issue an emergency decree.", "The decree removed basic rights, so thousands of communists were arrested.", "This weakened opposition before the March 1933 election and the Enabling Act."], "a": "Hitler used the Reichstag Fire to blame communists, remove people's rights and arrest his opponents."}, "walk": [{"h": "Legal power: 1933", "b": "After the Reichstag Fire in February 1933, the Enabling Act was passed on 23 March 1933. It let Hitler make laws without the Reichstag for four years, making him a dictator by law. In May trade unions were banned, and by July 1933 the Nazis were the only legal party."}, {"h": "Night of the Long Knives", "b": "Ernst Röhm led the SA (stormtroopers), with millions of members, and Hitler feared he was a rival; the army also disliked the SA. On 30 June 1934, the SS arrested and killed Röhm and many others. When Hindenburg died in August 1934, Hitler became Führer, and the army swore an oath of loyalty to him personally."}, {"h": "Terror and propaganda", "b": "The SS, led by Heinrich Himmler, were the elite black-shirted force, and the Gestapo were the secret police who relied on informers. Opponents could be sent to concentration camps such as Dachau, opened in 1933. Joseph Goebbels controlled newspapers, radio, films and rallies, and cheap radios (the ‘People's Receiver’) spread Nazi messages into homes."}], "qs": [["What was the Reichstag?", ["The German parliament", "The Nazi secret police", "Hitler's private army", "A concentration camp"], "The Reichstag was Germany's parliament and its building burned in 1933."], ["When was the Reichstag Fire?", ["27 February 1933", "30 January 1933", "30 June 1934", "9 November 1938"], "The fire was on 27 February 1933, under a month after Hitler became Chancellor."], ["Who was blamed for the Reichstag Fire?", ["Marinus van der Lubbe, a Dutch communist", "Ernst Röhm", "Franz von Papen", "Joseph Goebbels"], "Van der Lubbe was found at the scene and executed in 1934."], ["What did the Enabling Act (March 1933) allow Hitler to do?", ["Make laws without the Reichstag", "Become President", "Invade Poland", "Hold free elections"], "It let Hitler pass laws without the Reichstag's approval for four years."], ["Who led the SA?", ["Ernst Röhm", "Heinrich Himmler", "Joseph Goebbels", "Hermann Göring"], "Röhm led the SA until he was killed in the Night of the Long Knives."], ["When was the Night of the Long Knives?", ["30 June 1934", "27 February 1933", "2 August 1934", "23 March 1933"], "The purge of the SA leadership began on 30 June 1934."], ["What did Hitler become when Hindenburg died in August 1934?", ["Führer", "Chancellor for the first time", "Kaiser", "Head of the SA"], "Hitler merged the roles of Chancellor and President and called himself Führer."], ["Who were the Gestapo?", ["The secret police", "The army", "The Hitler Youth", "The stormtroopers"], "The Gestapo were the secret state police, who used informers to find opponents."], ["Who was in charge of Nazi propaganda?", ["Joseph Goebbels", "Heinrich Himmler", "Ernst Röhm", "Paul von Hindenburg"], "Goebbels was Minister for Propaganda from 1933."], ["Why did the army support the Night of the Long Knives?", ["It saw the SA as a threat to its position", "Röhm had insulted Hindenburg", "It wanted to join the SA", "Hitler promised to cut its size"], "Army leaders feared Röhm wanted the SA to take over the army."]], "bonus": ["What is a ‘dictator’?", ["A ruler with total power who cannot be voted out", "A leader chosen in a free election", "A judge in a court of law", "A member of parliament"], "A dictator holds total power and does not allow free elections or opposition."]}, {"t": "Life in Nazi Germany", "intro": "Once in control, the Nazis tried to shape every part of people's lives, from what children learned to what jobs women did. They also persecuted groups they wrongly saw as not belonging, above all Jewish people, and this topic must be studied with care and respect.", "how": ["Know how the Nazis controlled young people through schools and the Hitler Youth.", "Learn the Nazi view of women: the ‘three Ks’ of Kinder, Küche, Kirche (children, kitchen, church).", "Understand how unemployment fell, but also that wages and freedoms were limited.", "Learn the key steps in persecuting Jewish people: the 1933 boycott, the 1935 Nuremberg Laws, Kristallnacht in 1938.", "Remember that other groups were persecuted too, including Roma and Sinti people, disabled people and gay men."], "example": {"q": "Describe two ways the Nazis persecuted Jewish people between 1933 and 1938.", "steps": ["Pick a first example and give the date: the Nuremberg Laws of 1935.", "Explain it: Jewish people lost German citizenship and could not marry non-Jewish Germans.", "Pick a second example and give the date: Kristallnacht, 9–10 November 1938.", "Explain it: synagogues were burned, Jewish shops smashed, around 91 Jews killed and about 30,000 Jewish men sent to concentration camps."], "a": "The Nazis took away Jewish people's rights with the 1935 Nuremberg Laws and attacked them violently in Kristallnacht in 1938."}, "walk": [{"h": "Young people and women", "b": "Boys joined the Hitler Youth and girls the League of German Maidens; a 1936 law made it the official youth group, and from 1939 joining was compulsory. School subjects were changed to teach Nazi ideas, with lots of PE to make children fit. Women were encouraged to stay at home and have many children, and mothers of large families got the Mother's Cross."}, {"h": "The economy", "b": "Unemployment fell from about 6 million in 1933 to under 1 million by 1938. Schemes like the National Labour Service (RAD) and building autobahns created jobs, and rearmament after 1935 created many more. But trade unions were replaced by the German Labour Front, and Strength Through Joy offered workers holidays in place of higher wages."}, {"h": "Persecution", "b": "In April 1933 the Nazis organised a boycott of Jewish shops, and the 1935 Nuremberg Laws took away Jewish citizenship. On Kristallnacht (‘Night of Broken Glass’), 9–10 November 1938, Nazis attacked Jewish homes, shops and synagogues across Germany. Roma and Sinti people, disabled people and gay men were also persecuted, and during the war this led to the Holocaust, the murder of around six million Jewish people."}], "qs": [["What was the Nazi youth group for boys called?", ["The Hitler Youth", "The SA", "The Gestapo", "The Freikorps"], "Boys joined the Hitler Youth; membership was made compulsory by 1939."], ["What was the Nazi group for girls called?", ["The League of German Maidens", "The Hitler Youth for Girls", "The Women's Labour Front", "The Strength Through Joy"], "Girls joined the League of German Maidens (BDM)."], ["What were the Nazi ‘three Ks’ for women?", ["Kinder, Küche, Kirche", "Kaiser, Kunst, Krieg", "Kraft, Kampf, Kultur", "Kinder, Kunst, Krieg"], "They meant children, kitchen and church: the Nazi idea of a woman's role."], ["What did mothers of large families receive?", ["The Mother's Cross", "The Iron Cross", "A free car", "A seat in the Reichstag"], "The Mother's Cross was a medal for women with many children."], ["Which project created jobs building new roads?", ["The autobahns", "The Dawes Plan", "The Ruhr occupation", "The Kapp Putsch"], "Building motorways called autobahns was one of the job creation schemes."], ["What did Strength Through Joy offer workers?", ["Cheap holidays and leisure activities", "Higher wages", "The right to strike", "Shorter school days"], "It offered cheap holidays and trips to keep workers happy instead of raising pay."], ["What did the Nuremberg Laws of 1935 do?", ["Took German citizenship away from Jewish people", "Banned all political parties", "Made the Hitler Youth compulsory", "Gave women the vote"], "They made Jewish people ‘subjects’ rather than citizens and banned mixed marriages."], ["When was Kristallnacht?", ["9–10 November 1938", "30 June 1934", "27 February 1933", "1 April 1933"], "Kristallnacht happened on the night of 9–10 November 1938."], ["Why was it called Kristallnacht, the ‘Night of Broken Glass’?", ["Windows of Jewish shops and synagogues were smashed", "A glass factory was set on fire", "Hitler banned the sale of glass", "Bombs broke windows in Berlin"], "The streets were covered in broken glass from attacked Jewish property."], ["Which was a main reason unemployment fell after 1935?", ["Rearmament created jobs in factories and the army", "Women were given more jobs", "Wages doubled for all workers", "Trade unions won new rights"], "Building weapons and conscription into the army created many jobs."]], "bonus": ["What does ‘persecution’ mean?", ["Treating a group cruelly because of who they are", "Paying a group extra money", "Moving house to a new country", "Holding a free election"], "Persecution means cruel or unfair treatment of people because of their identity or beliefs."]}]}, {"id": "geography", "name": "Geography", "weeks": [{"t": "Tectonic hazards", "intro": "The Earth's crust is broken into huge slabs called tectonic plates that slowly move. Where they meet, earthquakes and volcanoes happen. Learning why helps us understand how people can protect themselves and why richer and poorer countries are affected differently.", "how": ["Learn the three plate margins: destructive (plates move together), constructive (plates move apart) and conservative (plates slide past each other).", "Volcanoes form at destructive and constructive margins; earthquakes happen at all three.", "Effects can be primary (caused directly, like collapsed buildings) or secondary (happening later, like fires or disease).", "Responses can be immediate (rescue, shelter, food) or long-term (rebuilding, improving building rules).", "Reduce risk with the 4 Ps: monitoring, prediction, protection and planning."], "example": {"q": "Explain why an earthquake of the same strength often causes more deaths in a low-income country (LIC) than in a high-income country (HIC).", "steps": ["Think about buildings: HICs can afford earthquake-resistant designs, LICs often have poorly built homes.", "Think about emergency services: HICs have well-trained rescue teams and hospitals.", "Think about planning: HICs run earthquake drills and have warning systems.", "Link it together: weaker buildings plus slower response means more people are killed or injured."], "a": "LICs usually have weaker buildings and less money for planning and emergency response, so more people die."}, "walk": [{"h": "Plates and margins", "b": "Convection currents in the mantle move tectonic plates a few centimetres a year. At a destructive margin, a dense oceanic plate sinks under a continental plate, causing violent volcanoes and earthquakes. At a constructive margin, plates move apart and magma rises, like at the Mid-Atlantic Ridge. At a conservative margin, such as the San Andreas Fault, plates slide past each other and cause earthquakes but no volcanoes."}, {"h": "Effects of hazards", "b": "Primary effects happen straight away, like buildings collapsing in an earthquake or lava destroying homes. Secondary effects come afterwards, such as fires from broken gas pipes, tsunamis, or disease from dirty water. For example, the 2015 Nepal earthquake (an LIC) triggered deadly avalanches and landslides."}, {"h": "Responses and reducing risk", "b": "Immediate responses save lives: search and rescue, medical care and shelter. Long-term responses include rebuilding and improving building laws. Risk is reduced by monitoring volcanoes (for example measuring gases and ground swelling), building earthquake-proof structures and practising drills."}], "qs": [["What are the large slabs that make up the Earth's crust called?", ["Tectonic plates", "Fault lines", "Magma chambers", "Continental shelves"], "The crust is broken into pieces called tectonic plates."], ["What happens at a constructive plate margin?", ["Plates move apart", "Plates move together", "Plates slide past each other", "Plates stop moving"], "At constructive margins plates move apart and magma rises to make new crust."], ["Which plate margin has earthquakes but no volcanoes?", ["Conservative", "Destructive", "Constructive", "All of them have volcanoes"], "No magma reaches the surface where plates slide past each other."], ["At a destructive margin, why does the oceanic plate sink under the continental plate?", ["It is denser", "It is thicker", "It is older than the Earth", "It is lighter"], "Denser oceanic crust is subducted beneath lighter continental crust."], ["Which is a primary effect of an earthquake?", ["Buildings collapsing", "Disease spreading weeks later", "Fires from broken gas pipes", "Loss of tourism income"], "Primary effects are caused directly by the ground shaking."], ["Which is a long-term response to a tectonic hazard?", ["Rebuilding homes with stronger designs", "Search and rescue", "Handing out bottled water", "Setting up emergency tents"], "Long-term responses happen months or years later, like rebuilding."], ["What does 'monitoring' a volcano involve?", ["Measuring signs like gas release and ground swelling", "Building a wall around it", "Moving everyone away permanently", "Pouring water into the crater"], "Scientists watch for warning signs to predict an eruption."], ["Why do people continue to live near volcanoes?", ["Fertile soils for farming", "Volcanoes never erupt", "The land is always free", "It is always cooler there"], "Volcanic ash breaks down into very fertile soil, which is good for farming."], ["What powers the movement of tectonic plates?", ["Convection currents in the mantle", "The Moon's gravity alone", "Ocean waves", "Wind patterns"], "Heat from the core causes convection currents in the mantle that move plates."], ["Why do earthquakes in LICs often cause more deaths than in HICs?", ["Weaker buildings and slower emergency response", "The earthquakes are always stronger", "HICs are not on plate margins", "LICs have more volcanoes"], "Poverty limits earthquake-proof building and emergency services."]], "bonus": ["What are the three main layers of the Earth, from the centre outwards?", ["Core, mantle, crust", "Crust, mantle, core", "Mantle, core, crust", "Core, crust, mantle"], "The core is in the middle, surrounded by the mantle, with the thin crust on the outside."]}, {"t": "Weather hazards and climate change", "intro": "Air moves around the planet in a pattern that controls our weather, from deserts to rainforests. Sometimes this produces dangerous weather like tropical storms. Our climate is also changing, and humans are a big part of the reason why.", "how": ["Global atmospheric circulation is made of three cells in each hemisphere: Hadley, Ferrel and Polar.", "Rising air creates low pressure (clouds and rain); sinking air creates high pressure (dry, clear weather).", "Tropical storms form over warm oceans of at least 27 °C, between about 5° and 30° from the Equator.", "Evidence for climate change includes ice cores, retreating glaciers, rising sea levels and temperature records.", "Natural causes include volcanic eruptions and changes in the Sun; human causes are burning fossil fuels, farming and deforestation."], "example": {"q": "Explain why tropical storms lose strength when they move over land.", "steps": ["Tropical storms get their energy from warm, moist air rising off the ocean.", "Over land there is no warm sea water to supply heat and moisture.", "Friction with the land surface also slows the winds.", "With no energy supply, the storm weakens and dies out."], "a": "Over land the storm loses its supply of warm, moist air from the ocean, so it weakens."}, "walk": [{"h": "Global circulation", "b": "The Sun heats the Equator most, so warm air rises there, creating low pressure and lots of rain. That is why tropical rainforests are near the Equator. The air sinks again around 30° north and south, creating high pressure and dry weather, which is where many hot deserts are."}, {"h": "Tropical storms", "b": "Tropical storms (hurricanes, cyclones and typhoons) form over oceans warmer than 27 °C. Warm, moist air rises fast, cools and condenses, releasing huge amounts of energy. The Earth's spin makes the storm rotate around a calm centre called the eye. Effects include strong winds, heavy rain, flooding and storm surges."}, {"h": "Climate change", "b": "The Earth has warmed by about 1 °C since around 1900. Evidence comes from ice cores (which trap old air), shrinking glaciers and rising sea levels. Burning fossil fuels releases CO₂, a greenhouse gas that traps heat. We can respond by mitigation (cutting emissions, e.g. renewable energy) and adaptation (living with change, e.g. flood defences)."}], "qs": [["What type of air pressure is found where air rises?", ["Low pressure", "High pressure", "No pressure", "Freezing pressure"], "Rising air creates low pressure, often bringing clouds and rain."], ["Roughly what minimum sea temperature do tropical storms need?", ["27 °C", "10 °C", "0 °C", "50 °C"], "Tropical storms need ocean water of at least about 27 °C."], ["What is the calm centre of a tropical storm called?", ["The eye", "The wall", "The core", "The front"], "The eye has light winds and clear skies, surrounded by the violent eyewall."], ["Which is NOT another name for a tropical storm?", ["Tornado", "Hurricane", "Cyclone", "Typhoon"], "Tornadoes are much smaller and form over land; the other three are regional names for tropical storms."], ["Why are many hot deserts found around 30° north and south of the Equator?", ["Air sinks there, creating dry high pressure", "Air rises there, causing rain", "They are closest to the Sun", "The oceans are coldest there"], "Sinking air at about 30° forms high pressure, so there is little rain."], ["What is a storm surge?", ["A rise in sea level pushed onto land by strong winds", "A sudden drop in temperature", "An earthquake under the sea", "A long period with no rain"], "Strong winds and low pressure push sea water onto the coast, causing floods."], ["How do ice cores provide evidence of climate change?", ["They contain trapped air showing past CO₂ and temperature", "They show how fast glaciers move today", "They measure modern rainfall", "They record past earthquakes"], "Bubbles in ancient ice preserve old air we can analyse."], ["Which is a human cause of climate change?", ["Burning fossil fuels", "Volcanic eruptions", "Changes in the Sun's output", "Changes in the Earth's orbit"], "Burning coal, oil and gas releases CO₂, which traps heat."], ["Which is an example of mitigation?", ["Switching to renewable energy", "Building higher flood walls", "Growing drought-resistant crops", "Moving homes away from the coast"], "Mitigation reduces the causes of climate change; the others are adaptation."], ["Why do tropical storms weaken over land?", ["They lose their supply of warm, moist air", "Land is always colder than 0 °C", "The eye closes up", "Mountains absorb all the rain"], "Without warm ocean water to provide energy, the storm runs out of power."]], "bonus": ["What is the line of latitude at 0° called?", ["The Equator", "The Prime Meridian", "The Tropic of Cancer", "The Arctic Circle"], "The Equator is at 0° latitude and divides the northern and southern hemispheres."]}, {"t": "Ecosystems and tropical rainforests", "intro": "An ecosystem is a community of plants and animals interacting with each other and with the non-living environment, like soil and climate. Tropical rainforests are the most biodiverse ecosystems on Earth, but they are under threat from deforestation.", "how": ["Producers (plants) make food using sunlight; consumers eat other living things; decomposers break down dead material.", "A food chain shows one feeding path; a food web shows many linked chains.", "A change to one part of an ecosystem can affect all the other parts.", "Rainforest plants and animals have adaptations to survive heat, heavy rain and competition for light.", "Deforestation has causes (logging, farming, mining, roads) and effects (lost biodiversity, soil erosion, climate change)."], "example": {"q": "In a food web, grass is eaten by rabbits, and rabbits are eaten by foxes. Explain what might happen if disease killed most of the rabbits.", "steps": ["Identify the links: grass → rabbits → foxes.", "Foxes lose much of their food, so the fox population falls.", "Fewer rabbits eat the grass, so the grass grows more.", "Foxes may eat other animals more, affecting other parts of the web."], "a": "Fox numbers would fall, grass would increase, and other prey of foxes might be eaten more."}, "walk": [{"h": "How ecosystems work", "b": "Energy enters an ecosystem through producers, which use sunlight. It passes to consumers when they eat. Decomposers like fungi and bacteria break down dead leaves, returning nutrients to the soil so plants can grow again. This is called nutrient cycling."}, {"h": "Rainforest features and adaptations", "b": "Rainforests are hot (about 27 °C all year) and wet (over 2000 mm of rain a year). They have layers: emergents, canopy, under canopy and shrub layer. Drip-tip leaves let rain run off quickly, buttress roots support tall trees in shallow soil, and sloths move slowly to save energy."}, {"h": "Deforestation", "b": "Rainforests are cut down for logging, cattle ranching, palm oil, mining and roads, as in the Amazon in Brazil. This destroys habitats, washes away soil and releases CO₂ when trees are burned. Sustainable management includes selective logging, replanting, ecotourism and protecting areas as national parks."}], "qs": [["What is a producer in a food chain?", ["A plant that makes food using sunlight", "An animal that eats plants", "An animal that eats other animals", "A fungus that breaks down dead material"], "Producers use photosynthesis to make their own food."], ["What role do decomposers play in an ecosystem?", ["Break down dead material and return nutrients", "Make food using sunlight", "Hunt other animals", "Pollinate flowers"], "Decomposers recycle nutrients back into the soil."], ["What is the difference between a food chain and a food web?", ["A food web shows many linked food chains", "A food chain has more animals", "A food web has no plants", "They are exactly the same"], "A food web joins many food chains to show all the feeding links."], ["Roughly what is the average temperature in a tropical rainforest?", ["27 °C", "5 °C", "−10 °C", "45 °C"], "Rainforests are hot all year, about 27 °C on average."], ["Why do many rainforest leaves have drip tips?", ["To let heavy rain run off quickly", "To catch more insects", "To store water for dry seasons", "To scare away animals"], "Drip tips shed water so leaves do not rot or grow algae."], ["What are buttress roots for?", ["Supporting very tall trees in shallow soil", "Storing water underground", "Catching falling leaves", "Producing fruit"], "Large ridged roots above ground help hold up tall trees."], ["Which layer of the rainforest forms a thick 'roof' of leaves?", ["The canopy", "The shrub layer", "The forest floor", "The emergents"], "The canopy is a dense layer that catches most of the sunlight."], ["Which is a major cause of deforestation in the Amazon?", ["Cattle ranching", "Ecotourism", "Replanting trees", "Creating national parks"], "Large areas are cleared to make pasture for cattle."], ["How does deforestation contribute to climate change?", ["Burning trees releases CO₂ and fewer trees absorb it", "Trees produce greenhouse gases when growing", "It makes the Sun hotter", "It increases rainfall everywhere"], "Trees store carbon; cutting and burning them adds CO₂ to the air."], ["Which is an example of sustainable rainforest management?", ["Selective logging and replanting", "Clear-felling whole areas", "Building more roads into the forest", "Burning land for farming"], "Taking only some trees and replanting lets the forest recover."]], "bonus": ["What do plants need to make food by photosynthesis?", ["Sunlight, water and carbon dioxide", "Oxygen, soil and sugar", "Only soil", "Darkness and heat"], "Plants use light energy to turn water and carbon dioxide into glucose."]}, {"t": "Urban issues and challenges", "intro": "More than half the world's people now live in towns and cities, and that number is growing fast. Cities bring jobs and services, but also problems like slums, traffic and pollution. This topic looks at why cities grow and how they can become more sustainable.", "how": ["Urbanisation is the increasing percentage of people living in towns and cities.", "Cities grow through rural–urban migration and natural increase (more births than deaths).", "Push factors drive people away from the countryside; pull factors attract them to the city.", "Cities offer opportunities (jobs, education, healthcare) and challenges (slums, waste, congestion, pollution).", "Sustainable cities meet today's needs without harming future generations, using things like green spaces, recycling and good public transport."], "example": {"q": "Give one push factor and one pull factor that lead people to move to a city like Rio de Janeiro or Lagos.", "steps": ["A push factor makes people want to leave the countryside.", "Example push factor: lack of jobs or failing harvests in rural areas.", "A pull factor attracts people to the city.", "Example pull factor: better-paid jobs and access to schools and hospitals."], "a": "Push: few jobs in the countryside; pull: more jobs and better services in the city."}, "walk": [{"h": "Why cities grow", "b": "Urbanisation is fastest in lower-income and newly emerging countries. People move from the countryside because of push factors like poverty and drought, and pull factors like jobs and education. Young migrants then have children in the city, adding natural increase."}, {"h": "Opportunities and challenges", "b": "Cities offer more jobs, schools, hospitals and entertainment. But rapid growth can mean squatter settlements (slums) such as the favelas of Rio, with poor sanitation, crime and informal jobs without contracts. In UK cities like London, challenges include high house prices, traffic congestion and inequality."}, {"h": "Sustainable cities", "b": "A sustainable city uses resources carefully so future generations can live well too. Ideas include solar panels, rainwater collection, recycling, green spaces, and cheap, clean public transport. Brownfield sites (old, previously used land) can be redeveloped instead of building on greenfield countryside."}], "qs": [["What is urbanisation?", ["An increase in the percentage of people living in towns and cities", "People moving from cities to the countryside", "Building more farms", "The growth of rural villages"], "Urbanisation means a growing share of people live in urban areas."], ["Which is a pull factor attracting people to a city?", ["Better job opportunities", "Drought in the countryside", "Lack of schools in villages", "Crop failure"], "Pull factors attract people to a place; the others push people away from rural areas."], ["What is natural increase?", ["When births are higher than deaths", "When people move into a city", "When a city expands its boundaries", "When deaths are higher than births"], "Natural increase is population growth from more births than deaths."], ["What is rural–urban migration?", ["People moving from the countryside to cities", "People moving between two countries", "People moving from cities to the countryside", "Animals moving between habitats"], "Rural means countryside and urban means town or city."], ["What is a squatter settlement?", ["Housing built illegally on land people do not own", "A luxury housing estate", "A government office building", "A city park"], "Squatter settlements are often built from scrap materials without permission."], ["What is the informal economy?", ["Jobs without contracts or taxes, like street selling", "Jobs in large banks", "Government jobs", "Online shopping"], "Informal jobs are unofficial, often low-paid and unprotected."], ["What is a brownfield site?", ["Land that has been built on before", "Untouched countryside", "A farm growing crops", "A national park"], "Brownfield land was previously developed and can be reused."], ["Which is a feature of a sustainable city?", ["Efficient public transport", "More cars on the roads", "Sending all waste to landfill", "Building on all green spaces"], "Good public transport reduces congestion and pollution."], ["Where is urbanisation happening fastest today?", ["Lower-income and newly emerging countries", "Only in the UK", "Mostly in Antarctica", "Equally everywhere"], "Rapid growth is mainly in LICs and NEEs in Africa and Asia."], ["Why can traffic congestion be a problem in big cities?", ["It causes air pollution and wastes time", "It reduces the number of cars", "It makes cities quieter", "It creates more green space"], "Slow traffic produces more exhaust fumes and delays journeys."]], "bonus": ["What is the capital city of the United Kingdom?", ["London", "Manchester", "Birmingham", "Edinburgh"], "London is the capital of the UK; Edinburgh is the capital of Scotland."]}]}, {"id": "computing", "name": "Computer Science", "weeks": [{"t": "Binary and data representation", "intro": "Computers store everything as binary: just 1s and 0s. Learning how binary works helps you understand how numbers, text and pictures are stored inside a computer.", "how": ["Binary is base 2, so each place value is double the one to its right: 128, 64, 32, 16, 8, 4, 2, 1.", "To change binary to denary, add up the place values that have a 1 under them.", "To change denary to binary, take away the biggest place values that fit and put a 1 under each one you use.", "A bit is a single 1 or 0, a nibble is 4 bits and a byte is 8 bits.", "Hexadecimal is base 16 and uses 0–9 then A–F, where A = 10 and F = 15; one hex digit stands for one nibble."], "example": {"q": "Convert the denary number 45 into 8-bit binary.", "steps": ["Write the place values: 128, 64, 32, 16, 8, 4, 2, 1.", "128 and 64 are too big, so they get 0. 32 fits: 45 − 32 = 13, so 32 gets a 1.", "16 is too big for 13, so 0. 8 fits: 13 − 8 = 5, so 8 gets a 1.", "4 fits: 5 − 4 = 1, so 4 gets a 1. 2 gets 0. 1 fits, so 1 gets a 1.", "Read along the row: 0 0 1 0 1 1 0 1."], "a": "45 in 8-bit binary is 00101101."}, "walk": [{"h": "Why binary?", "b": "Inside a computer are billions of tiny switches called transistors, which can only be on or off. We write on as 1 and off as 0. One switch gives one bit, and 8 bits make a byte, which can store one letter such as 'A'."}, {"h": "Binary and denary", "b": "Denary is our normal base 10 system. In binary the columns are 128, 64, 32, 16, 8, 4, 2, 1. So 00001011 = 8 + 2 + 1 = 11. The biggest 8-bit number is 11111111, which is 255."}, {"h": "Units and hex", "b": "Units grow in size: bit, nibble (4 bits), byte (8 bits), kilobyte, megabyte, gigabyte, terabyte. Hex is a shorter way to write binary: split a byte into two nibbles and turn each into one hex digit. For example, 1011 0110 is 11 and 6, which is B6."}], "qs": [["How many bits are in one byte?", ["8", "4", "16", "1024"], "A byte is a group of 8 bits."], ["What is the binary number 1010 in denary?", ["10", "12", "8", "5"], "1010 has 1s under 8 and 2, and 8 + 2 = 10."], ["How many bits are in a nibble?", ["4", "8", "2", "16"], "A nibble is half a byte, so it has 4 bits."], ["What is 13 in 8-bit binary?", ["00001101", "00001011", "00001110", "00010011"], "13 = 8 + 4 + 1, so there are 1s under 8, 4 and 1."], ["What is the largest denary number you can store in 8 bits?", ["255", "256", "128", "512"], "11111111 = 128+64+32+16+8+4+2+1 = 255."], ["Which list goes from smallest to largest?", ["bit, nibble, byte, kilobyte", "byte, bit, nibble, kilobyte", "nibble, bit, byte, kilobyte", "bit, byte, nibble, kilobyte"], "A bit is 1 binary digit, a nibble is 4 bits, a byte is 8 bits and a kilobyte is about 1000 bytes."], ["What is the hex digit A in denary?", ["10", "11", "1", "16"], "Hex counts 0–9 and then A = 10, B = 11, up to F = 15."], ["What is the hex number 2F in denary?", ["47", "215", "32", "45"], "2 sixteens is 32 and F is 15, so 32 + 15 = 47."], ["What is the binary number 11111111 written in hex?", ["FF", "255", "EE", "F8"], "Each nibble 1111 is 15, which is F in hex, so the byte is FF."], ["Why do computers use binary?", ["Their circuits are made of switches that are on or off", "Binary numbers are shorter than denary numbers", "Binary is easier for people to read", "Computers can only count up to 2"], "Transistors have two states, on and off, which match 1 and 0."]], "bonus": ["In the denary number 4352, what is the value of the digit 3?", ["300", "3", "30", "3000"], "The 3 is in the hundreds column, so it is worth 300; binary uses columns in the same way."]}, {"t": "Algorithms", "intro": "An algorithm is a set of clear steps to solve a problem, like a recipe. Good algorithms help computers search and sort data quickly, which is why apps and websites feel fast.", "how": ["Decomposition means breaking a big problem into smaller problems that are easier to solve.", "Abstraction means removing details you do not need so you can focus on what matters.", "In flowcharts, a rounded box is start or end, a rectangle is a process, a parallelogram is input or output and a diamond is a decision.", "A linear search checks each item in turn; a binary search only works on a sorted list and keeps halving it.", "A bubble sort compares neighbouring pairs and swaps them if they are in the wrong order, repeating passes until no swaps happen."], "example": {"q": "Use a binary search to find 23 in the sorted list 3, 8, 12, 17, 23, 30, 41.", "steps": ["There are 7 items, so the middle item is the 4th one: 17.", "23 is bigger than 17, so throw away 17 and everything before it. Left: 23, 30, 41.", "The middle of these is 30. 23 is smaller than 30, so throw away 30 and 41. Left: 23.", "Check 23: it matches, so the search stops."], "a": "23 is found after checking 3 items (17, 30, then 23)."}, "walk": [{"h": "Thinking like a programmer", "b": "Decomposition breaks a problem down: making a game could be split into drawing the screen, moving the player and keeping score. Abstraction ignores what does not matter: a map of the Tube does not show every street, just the stations and lines."}, {"h": "Searching", "b": "A linear search starts at the first item and checks each one until it finds the target, so in a list of 10 items it may need up to 10 checks. A binary search looks at the middle item of a sorted list, then throws away the half that cannot contain the target. Each check halves the list, so it is much faster on big lists."}, {"h": "Bubble sort", "b": "Compare items 1 and 2, swap if needed, then items 2 and 3, and so on to the end: that is one pass. For 5, 2, 4, 1 the first pass gives 2, 4, 1, 5, so the largest item has 'bubbled' to the end. Keep doing passes until a whole pass has no swaps."}], "qs": [["What is breaking a problem down into smaller parts called?", ["Decomposition", "Abstraction", "Iteration", "Compilation"], "Decomposition means splitting a problem into smaller, easier problems."], ["What is removing unnecessary detail from a problem called?", ["Abstraction", "Decomposition", "Selection", "Sorting"], "Abstraction keeps only the details that matter."], ["In a flowchart, what does a diamond shape show?", ["A decision", "The start or end", "An input or output", "A process"], "A diamond asks a yes/no question, so the path splits."], ["What must be true of a list before you can use a binary search?", ["It must be sorted", "It must have fewer than 10 items", "It must contain only numbers", "It must be unsorted"], "Binary search relies on order to know which half to throw away."], ["How does a linear search work?", ["It checks each item in turn from the start", "It always checks the middle item first", "It sorts the list then picks the first item", "It checks only the last item"], "Linear means in a line: one item after another."], ["A linear search looks for an item in a list of 10. What is the most checks it could need?", ["10", "5", "1", "4"], "In the worst case the item is last or missing, so all 10 are checked."], ["Binary search for 27 in 2, 5, 9, 14, 20, 27, 33. Which item is checked first?", ["14", "2", "20", "33"], "There are 7 items, so the middle one is the 4th: 14."], ["What is the list 4, 3, 2, 1 after ONE pass of bubble sort?", ["3, 2, 1, 4", "1, 2, 3, 4", "3, 4, 2, 1", "4, 3, 2, 1"], "4 is swapped with 3, then 2, then 1, so it ends up last: 3, 2, 1, 4."], ["How does a bubble sort know the list is fully sorted?", ["A whole pass happens with no swaps", "It has done exactly one pass", "The first item is the smallest", "It has checked the middle item"], "If nothing needed swapping in a full pass, every pair is in order."], ["Why is binary search usually faster than linear search on a big sorted list?", ["Each check halves the number of items left", "It checks two items at the same time", "It starts at the end of the list", "It does not need to compare any items"], "Halving each time means a list of 1000 needs only about 10 checks."]], "bonus": ["Which shows 12, 3, 45, 7 in ascending order?", ["3, 7, 12, 45", "45, 12, 7, 3", "3, 12, 7, 45", "12, 3, 45, 7"], "Ascending means smallest to largest, which is exactly what a sort algorithm produces."]}, {"t": "Programming basics in Python", "intro": "Programming means writing instructions a computer can follow. Python is a popular, readable language, and the same three building blocks of sequence, selection and iteration appear in every program you will write.", "how": ["A variable is a named box that stores a value, for example score = 0.", "Main data types are integer (whole number), float (decimal), string (text in quotes) and Boolean (True or False).", "Use = to store a value and == to check if two things are equal.", "Selection uses if, elif and else to choose which code runs, and the code inside must be indented.", "Iteration means repeating: use a for loop when you know how many times, and a while loop to repeat while a condition is True."], "example": {"q": "What does this code print? total = 0, then for i in range(1, 4): total = total + i, then print(total)", "steps": ["range(1, 4) gives 1, 2, 3 because it stops before 4.", "Loop 1: total = 0 + 1 = 1.", "Loop 2: total = 1 + 2 = 3.", "Loop 3: total = 3 + 3 = 6. Then the loop ends and print(total) runs."], "a": "The program prints 6."}, "walk": [{"h": "Variables and data types", "b": "name = 'Sam' stores a string, age = 13 stores an integer and height = 1.55 stores a float. input() always gives a string, so use int(input()) when you need a number. Python can also do maths: 17 // 5 gives 3 (whole number division) and 17 % 5 gives 2 (the remainder)."}, {"h": "Selection with if", "b": "if age >= 16: print('You can apply') else: print('Too young') runs only one of the two prints. Comparison operators are ==, !=, <, >, <= and >=. Remember the colon at the end of the if line and to indent the code underneath."}, {"h": "Loops with for and while", "b": "for i in range(3): print(i) prints 0, 1 and 2, because range starts at 0 and stops before 3. A while loop keeps going while its condition is True, for example while guess != 7: guess = int(input()). Use while when you do not know how many repeats you need."}], "qs": [["What data type is the value 3.7?", ["Float", "Integer", "String", "Boolean"], "A float is a number with a decimal point."], ["What data type is the value '42' (in quotes)?", ["String", "Integer", "Float", "Boolean"], "Anything inside quotes is text, which is a string."], ["What does the line x = 7 do?", ["Stores 7 in the variable x", "Checks whether x is equal to 7", "Prints the number 7", "Adds 7 to x"], "A single = assigns a value to a variable."], ["Which operator checks if two values are equal?", ["==", "=", "!=", "=>"], "== compares; = assigns; != means not equal."], ["What does for i in range(3): print(i) print?", ["0 1 2", "1 2 3", "0 1 2 3", "3"], "range(3) starts at 0 and stops before 3."], ["age = 15, then if age >= 16: print('Yes') else: print('No'). What is printed?", ["No", "Yes", "Yes and No", "Nothing"], "15 is not greater than or equal to 16, so the else part runs."], ["Which loop is best when you do not know how many times to repeat?", ["A while loop", "A for loop", "An if statement", "A print statement"], "A while loop repeats until its condition becomes False."], ["What is the result of int('5') + 2 in Python?", ["7", "52", "25", "An error"], "int('5') turns the string into the integer 5, and 5 + 2 = 7."], ["n = 1, then while n < 10: n = n * 2. What is n at the end?", ["16", "8", "10", "32"], "n goes 1, 2, 4, 8, 16; at 16 the condition n < 10 is False so it stops."], ["What is 17 % 5 in Python?", ["2", "3", "3.4", "12"], "% gives the remainder: 17 ÷ 5 is 3 remainder 2."]], "bonus": ["Using the correct order of operations (BIDMAS), what is 2 + 3 × 4?", ["14", "20", "24", "9"], "Multiply first: 3 × 4 = 12, then 2 + 12 = 14, and Python follows the same rule."]}, {"t": "Computer systems", "intro": "A computer system is the hardware and software working together. Knowing what the CPU, memory and storage do helps you understand why some computers are faster than others.", "how": ["The CPU (Central Processing Unit) processes instructions and data; it contains the ALU, control unit, cache and registers.", "The CPU repeats the fetch–decode–execute cycle billions of times a second.", "RAM is volatile main memory that holds programs and data in use; it is wiped when the power goes off.", "ROM is non-volatile and holds the start-up (boot) instructions, which cannot normally be changed.", "Secondary storage keeps files permanently: magnetic (hard disk), solid state (SSD, USB stick) and optical (CD, DVD, Blu-ray)."], "example": {"q": "Describe what the CPU does to run one instruction.", "steps": ["Fetch: the CPU gets the instruction from RAM, using the address held in the program counter.", "The program counter goes up by 1 so it points to the next instruction.", "Decode: the control unit works out what the instruction means.", "Execute: the instruction is carried out, for example the ALU adds two numbers.", "The cycle then repeats for the next instruction."], "a": "The CPU fetches the instruction from memory, decodes it, then executes it, and repeats."}, "walk": [{"h": "Inside the CPU", "b": "The ALU (Arithmetic Logic Unit) does maths and comparisons, like 5 + 3 or 'is 7 > 4?'. The control unit directs the flow of data and decodes instructions. Cache is a tiny, very fast memory that stores instructions the CPU uses often. Registers like the program counter hold single values the CPU is working on right now."}, {"h": "Fetch–decode–execute", "b": "Every program runs by repeating fetch, decode, execute. Clock speed tells you how many cycles happen each second: 3 GHz means 3 billion cycles per second. A higher clock speed, more cores or more cache can all make a CPU faster."}, {"h": "Memory and storage", "b": "RAM holds what you are using now, like an open game, but forgets it when switched off. ROM keeps the boot instructions even without power. Secondary storage such as an SSD keeps your files when the computer is off; SSDs use flash memory with no moving parts, hard disks use spinning magnetic platters, and optical discs are read by a laser."}], "qs": [["What does CPU stand for?", ["Central Processing Unit", "Computer Processing Unit", "Central Program Unit", "Core Processing Utility"], "CPU stands for Central Processing Unit."], ["Which part of the CPU carries out calculations and comparisons?", ["ALU", "Control unit", "Cache", "RAM"], "The Arithmetic Logic Unit does the maths and logic."], ["What is the correct order of the CPU cycle?", ["Fetch, decode, execute", "Decode, fetch, execute", "Execute, fetch, decode", "Fetch, execute, decode"], "An instruction must be fetched, then understood, then carried out."], ["Which register holds the address of the next instruction?", ["Program counter", "ALU", "Cache", "Control unit"], "The program counter keeps track of where the next instruction is."], ["What happens to the contents of RAM when the power is turned off?", ["They are lost", "They are saved to ROM", "They stay in RAM", "They move to the cache"], "RAM is volatile, so it needs power to keep its data."], ["What is ROM used to store?", ["The start-up (boot) instructions", "Documents you are editing", "Photos you have taken", "Programs you have just opened"], "ROM is non-volatile and holds the instructions needed to start the computer."], ["Which type of storage uses flash memory and has no moving parts?", ["Solid state (SSD)", "Magnetic hard disk", "Optical (Blu-ray)", "RAM"], "Solid state storage uses flash memory chips, so nothing moves."], ["What is cache memory?", ["Small, very fast memory that stores often-used instructions", "Large, slow storage for saving files", "Memory that holds the boot instructions", "A disc read by a laser"], "Cache sits in or near the CPU so it can reach common data quickly."], ["Which change would most likely make a CPU run faster?", ["A higher clock speed", "A larger hard disk", "A bigger monitor", "A slower fan"], "More cycles per second means more instructions run each second."], ["Why does a computer need secondary storage as well as RAM?", ["It keeps data when the power is off", "It is faster than RAM", "It does the calculations", "It decodes instructions"], "Secondary storage is non-volatile, so your files are kept permanently."]], "bonus": ["Which of these is an input device?", ["Keyboard", "Monitor", "Speaker", "Printer"], "A keyboard sends data into the computer; the others send data out."]}]}, {"id": "french", "name": "French", "weeks": [{"t": "Me, my family and friends", "intro": "This topic is about introducing yourself and describing the people in your life. It is one of the first things you learn because you will use it in speaking and writing exams again and again.", "how": ["Introduce yourself with Je m'appelle..., J'ai ... ans and J'habite à...", "Use avoir for age: J'ai treize ans means I am thirteen (literally I have thirteen years).", "Describe people with il est / elle est + adjective, and il a / elle a + hair or eyes.", "Adjectives agree: add -e for feminine and -s for plural, e.g. petit, petite, petits, petites.", "Most adjectives go after the noun (un chat noir), but some short common ones go before (une petite sœur)."], "example": {"q": "Translate into French: My sister is tall and funny.", "steps": ["My sister = ma sœur (sœur is feminine, so use ma).", "is = est.", "tall = grand, but it must agree with a feminine noun, so add -e: grande.", "funny = drôle, which already ends in -e, so it stays the same.", "Put it together: Ma sœur est grande et drôle."], "a": "Ma sœur est grande et drôle."}, "walk": [{"h": "Introducing yourself", "b": "Say Je m'appelle Alizah (My name is Alizah), J'ai quatorze ans (I am 14) and J'habite à Londres (I live in London). Add your family: J'ai un frère et une sœur (I have a brother and a sister). Je suis fille unique or fils unique means I am an only child."}, {"h": "Describing people", "b": "Use être for personality: Mon père est gentil (My dad is kind). Use avoir for hair and eyes: Elle a les cheveux bruns et les yeux bleus (She has brown hair and blue eyes). Useful words: grand (tall), petit (short), sympa (nice), bavard (chatty), timide (shy)."}, {"h": "Adjective agreement", "b": "Adjectives match the noun: mon frère est petit, ma sœur est petite, mes frères sont petits, mes sœurs sont petites. Adjectives already ending in -e, like timide, do not add another -e. Some change more: sportif becomes sportive, gentil becomes gentille and beau becomes belle."}], "qs": [["What does Je m'appelle Tom mean?", ["My name is Tom", "I live with Tom", "I call Tom", "I am Tom's friend"], "Je m'appelle literally means I call myself, used to give your name."], ["How do you say I am 13 years old in French?", ["J'ai treize ans", "Je suis treize ans", "J'ai trois ans", "Je suis treize"], "French uses avoir for age, so I have thirteen years."], ["What does ma grand-mère mean?", ["My grandmother", "My big sister", "My aunt", "My mother"], "Grand-mère is grandmother; ma is my for a feminine noun."], ["Choose the correct word: Ma mère est ___.", ["grande", "grand", "grands", "grandes"], "Mère is feminine singular, so add -e: grande."], ["Choose the correct word: Mes frères sont ___.", ["petits", "petit", "petite", "petites"], "Frères is masculine plural, so add -s: petits."], ["What does Elle a les yeux verts mean?", ["She has green eyes", "She has green hair", "She is wearing green", "He has green eyes"], "Elle a means she has, and les yeux means the eyes."], ["Which is correct for She is sporty?", ["Elle est sportive", "Elle est sportif", "Elle a sportive", "Elle est sportifs"], "Sportif becomes sportive in the feminine."], ["Choose the correct word: Mon ami ___ très bavard.", ["est", "a", "es", "sont"], "Il est means he is, and we need is with a personality adjective."], ["What does Je m'entends bien avec mon frère mean?", ["I get on well with my brother", "I argue with my brother", "I listen to my brother", "I look like my brother"], "S'entendre bien avec means to get on well with someone."], ["Translate: My friends (all girls) are kind.", ["Mes amies sont gentilles", "Mes amis sont gentils", "Mes amies sont gentils", "Mon amie est gentille"], "Amies is feminine plural, so gentil becomes gentilles."]], "bonus": ["In the sentence My tall brother laughs, which word is the adjective?", ["tall", "brother", "laughs", "My"], "An adjective describes a noun, and tall describes brother."]}, {"t": "School", "intro": "This topic covers school subjects, your opinions about them and telling the time. Giving opinions with reasons is one of the quickest ways to boost your marks in French.", "how": ["Learn subject names such as les maths, l'anglais, l'histoire, la géographie, les sciences and le dessin (art).", "Give opinions with j'aime, j'adore, je n'aime pas and je déteste, followed by le, la, l' or les.", "Add a reason with parce que (because) or car, e.g. parce que c'est intéressant.", "Use c'est + adjective for reasons: c'est facile, c'est difficile, c'est utile, c'est ennuyeux.", "Tell the time with Il est ... heures, adding et quart, et demie or moins le quart."], "example": {"q": "Translate into French: I love maths because it is useful.", "steps": ["I love = j'adore.", "With opinion verbs you keep the article, so maths = les maths.", "because = parce que.", "it is useful = c'est utile.", "Put it together: J'adore les maths parce que c'est utile."], "a": "J'adore les maths parce que c'est utile."}, "walk": [{"h": "Subjects", "b": "Some subjects are masculine (le français, le dessin), some feminine (la musique, la géographie) and some plural (les maths, les sciences). Use l' before a vowel or silent h: l'anglais, l'histoire. Ma matière préférée, c'est la musique means My favourite subject is music."}, {"h": "Opinions and reasons", "b": "Opinion verbs go from strong like to strong dislike: j'adore, j'aime, je n'aime pas, je déteste. Unlike English, French keeps the article: J'aime la musique (I like music). Add a reason: Je déteste l'histoire car c'est ennuyeux (I hate history because it is boring)."}, {"h": "Telling the time", "b": "Il est huit heures means It is 8 o'clock. Add et quart for quarter past, et demie for half past, and moins le quart for quarter to: Il est neuf heures moins le quart is 8:45. To say at a time, use à: Les cours commencent à huit heures et demie (Lessons start at 8:30)."}], "qs": [["What does l'histoire mean?", ["History", "Geography", "Story time", "Science"], "L'histoire is history (it can also mean story, but as a subject it is history)."], ["What does je déteste mean?", ["I hate", "I like", "I love", "I don't mind"], "Je déteste is the strongest negative opinion: I hate."], ["Which means because?", ["parce que", "mais", "aussi", "avec"], "Parce que means because; mais means but."], ["How do you say It's easy in French?", ["C'est facile", "C'est difficile", "C'est ennuyeux", "C'est utile"], "Facile means easy; difficile means difficult."], ["Choose the correct word: J'aime ___ musique.", ["la", "le", "les", "l'"], "Musique is feminine singular, so it takes la."], ["What time is Il est trois heures et demie?", ["3:30", "3:15", "2:30", "3:45"], "Et demie means half past, so it is half past three."], ["What time is Il est dix heures moins le quart?", ["9:45", "10:15", "10:45", "9:15"], "Moins le quart means quarter to, so quarter to ten is 9:45."], ["Translate: I don't like science.", ["Je n'aime pas les sciences", "Je n'aime les sciences", "J'aime pas sciences", "Je ne pas aime les sciences"], "The negative ne...pas wraps around the verb: n'aime pas."], ["What does Ma matière préférée, c'est l'anglais mean?", ["My favourite subject is English", "My English teacher is great", "I prefer English to other subjects", "I have English first"], "Matière means subject and préférée means favourite."], ["Which sentence is correct French?", ["J'adore le dessin car c'est amusant", "J'adore dessin car c'est amusant", "Je adore le dessin car c'est amusant", "J'adore le dessin car est amusant"], "It needs j' before a vowel, the article le and c'est for it is."]], "bonus": ["Which word is the verb in I love maths?", ["love", "I", "maths", "There is no verb"], "A verb is a doing or being word, and love is the action."]}, {"t": "Free time and hobbies", "intro": "This topic is about what you do in your spare time, such as sport, music and going out. You will learn two key verbs, jouer and faire, and how to say how often you do things.", "how": ["Use jouer à for sports and games: je joue au foot, je joue aux cartes.", "Use jouer de for musical instruments: je joue du piano, je joue de la guitare.", "Use faire de for many activities: je fais du vélo, je fais de la natation, je fais de l'équitation.", "à + le becomes au, à + les becomes aux; de + le becomes du, de + les becomes des.", "Say how often with frequency words: toujours, souvent, quelquefois, de temps en temps, rarement, ne...jamais."], "example": {"q": "Translate into French: I often go cycling.", "steps": ["Cycling uses faire + le vélo, so I go cycling = je fais du vélo.", "de + le joins up to make du.", "often = souvent, which goes straight after the verb.", "Put it together: Je fais souvent du vélo."], "a": "Je fais souvent du vélo."}, "walk": [{"h": "Jouer à and jouer de", "b": "Jouer à is for sports and games: je joue au tennis (à + le = au), je joue à la pétanque, je joue aux jeux vidéo (à + les = aux). Jouer de is for instruments: je joue du violon (de + le = du), je joue de la batterie (drums)."}, {"h": "Faire de", "b": "Faire means to do or make, and is used for lots of activities: je fais du sport, je fais de la danse, je fais de l'escalade (climbing). Remember the joining rule: de + le = du, de + la stays de la, and de + l' stays de l'. Faire is irregular: je fais, tu fais, il fait, nous faisons, vous faites, ils font."}, {"h": "How often?", "b": "Frequency words usually go straight after the verb: Je joue souvent au foot (I often play football). Other useful ones are tous les jours (every day), le week-end (at the weekend) and une fois par semaine (once a week). For never, wrap ne...jamais round the verb: Je ne fais jamais de natation (I never go swimming)."}], "qs": [["What does Je joue au foot mean?", ["I play football", "I watch football", "I like football", "I do football training"], "Je joue means I play, and au foot is football."], ["What does souvent mean?", ["Often", "Never", "Sometimes", "Always"], "Souvent means often; jamais means never."], ["Choose the correct word: Je fais ___ natation.", ["de la", "du", "au", "des"], "Natation is feminine, so de + la stays de la."], ["Choose the correct word: Je joue ___ piano.", ["du", "au", "de la", "à la"], "Instruments take jouer de, and de + le = du."], ["Choose the correct word: Je joue ___ tennis.", ["au", "du", "de la", "aux"], "Sports take jouer à, and à + le = au."], ["What does de temps en temps mean?", ["From time to time", "All the time", "On time", "Every day"], "De temps en temps means now and then, or from time to time."], ["Which is the correct form: nous ___ du vélo?", ["faisons", "faites", "font", "fais"], "Faire with nous is faisons."], ["Translate: I never play video games.", ["Je ne joue jamais aux jeux vidéo", "Je joue jamais aux jeux vidéo", "Je ne joue pas aux jeux vidéo", "Je ne jamais joue aux jeux vidéo"], "Ne...jamais wraps around the verb, and à + les = aux."], ["What does Je fais de l'équitation une fois par semaine mean?", ["I go horse riding once a week", "I go horse riding every weekend", "I go swimming once a week", "I did horse riding last week"], "Équitation is horse riding and une fois par semaine is once a week."], ["Choose the correct word: Mes parents ___ de la randonnée.", ["font", "fait", "faisons", "faites"], "Mes parents is ils (they), and faire with ils is font."]], "bonus": ["In English, which word in I sometimes play tennis tells you how often?", ["sometimes", "play", "tennis", "I"], "Sometimes is an adverb of frequency, just like souvent or quelquefois in French."]}, {"t": "Talking about the past", "intro": "The passé composé is the main French past tense for saying what you did, like I played or I went. Using it well shows examiners you can handle more than one time frame, which is key for higher marks.", "how": ["The passé composé has two parts: a helper verb (avoir or être) in the present tense, plus a past participle.", "For -er verbs the past participle ends in -é (jouer becomes joué), for -ir verbs -i (finir becomes fini), for -re verbs -u (vendre becomes vendu).", "Most verbs use avoir: j'ai mangé (I ate), il a fini (he finished).", "About 14 verbs of movement, like aller, venir, arriver, partir, sortir and rester, use être instead: je suis allé (I went).", "With être, the past participle agrees with the subject: elle est allée, ils sont allés, elles sont allées."], "example": {"q": "Translate into French: Last weekend, my sister went to the cinema.", "steps": ["Last weekend = le week-end dernier.", "Aller (to go) takes être, and with my sister (elle) that is est.", "The past participle of aller is allé; with être it agrees, so for a girl add -e: allée.", "to the cinema = au cinéma (à + le = au).", "Put it together: Le week-end dernier, ma sœur est allée au cinéma."], "a": "Le week-end dernier, ma sœur est allée au cinéma."}, "walk": [{"h": "Avoir + past participle", "b": "Take the present tense of avoir (j'ai, tu as, il/elle a, nous avons, vous avez, ils/elles ont) and add the past participle: J'ai regardé un film (I watched a film). Some common verbs have irregular participles: faire becomes fait, voir becomes vu, boire becomes bu and prendre becomes pris."}, {"h": "Être verbs", "b": "Verbs of movement or change use être (je suis, tu es, il/elle est, nous sommes, vous êtes, ils/elles sont). Examples: Je suis allé en ville (I went to town), Il est arrivé à midi (He arrived at midday). A trick to remember many of them is MRS VAN DER TRAMP (monter, rester, sortir, venir, aller, naître, descendre, entrer, rentrer, tomber, retourner, arriver, mourir, partir)."}, {"h": "Agreement and time words", "b": "With être, add -e for a female subject and -s for a plural one: Elles sont parties (They (girls) left). With avoir, there is no agreement with the subject: Elle a mangé. Signal the past with hier (yesterday), le week-end dernier (last weekend) and l'année dernière (last year)."}], "qs": [["What does J'ai joué au tennis mean?", ["I played tennis", "I play tennis", "I will play tennis", "I like tennis"], "J'ai joué is the passé composé, so it is in the past."], ["What is the past participle of manger?", ["mangé", "mangi", "mangu", "manger"], "-er verbs make their past participle with -é."], ["What does hier mean?", ["Yesterday", "Tomorrow", "Today", "Last year"], "Hier means yesterday and signals the past tense."], ["Which helper verb does aller use in the passé composé?", ["être", "avoir", "faire", "aller"], "Aller is a verb of movement, so it uses être."], ["Choose the correct word: Nous ___ fini nos devoirs.", ["avons", "sommes", "ont", "avez"], "Finir uses avoir, and avoir with nous is avons."], ["What is the past participle of faire?", ["fait", "faisé", "faité", "fu"], "Faire is irregular, and its past participle is fait."], ["Choose the correct form: Elle est ___ à la plage.", ["allée", "allé", "allés", "allées"], "Aller uses être, so the participle agrees with elle: allée."], ["Translate: They (boys) arrived at six o'clock.", ["Ils sont arrivés à six heures", "Ils ont arrivé à six heures", "Ils sont arrivé à six heures", "Ils sont arrivées à six heures"], "Arriver uses être and agrees with a masculine plural subject: arrivés."], ["Which sentence is correct?", ["Hier, j'ai vu un film", "Hier, je suis vu un film", "Hier, j'ai voir un film", "Hier, j'ai vuu un film"], "Voir uses avoir and its past participle is vu."], ["Translate: My sisters went out on Saturday.", ["Mes sœurs sont sorties samedi", "Mes sœurs ont sorti samedi", "Mes sœurs sont sortis samedi", "Mes sœurs sont sortie samedi"], "Sortir uses être, so add -es for a feminine plural subject."]], "bonus": ["Which English sentence is in the past tense?", ["I visited my cousin", "I visit my cousin", "I will visit my cousin", "I am visiting my cousin"], "Visited describes something that has already happened."]}]}, {"id": "spanish", "name": "Spanish", "weeks": [{"t": "Me, my family and friends", "intro": "This topic is about introducing yourself and describing the people in your life. You will use it all the way through GCSE, especially in speaking and writing tasks.", "how": ["Introduce yourself with Me llamo..., Tengo ... años and Vivo en...", "Use tener for age: Tengo trece años means I am thirteen (literally I have thirteen years).", "Use ser for personality and looks: Mi madre es simpática. Use tener for hair and eyes: Tengo los ojos azules.", "Adjectives agree: -o changes to -a for feminine and you add -s for plural, e.g. alto, alta, altos, altas.", "In Spanish, adjectives usually go after the noun: un perro negro (a black dog)."], "example": {"q": "Translate into Spanish: My sister is tall and funny.", "steps": ["My sister = mi hermana.", "is (for describing what someone is like) = es, from ser.", "tall = alto, but hermana is feminine, so change -o to -a: alta.", "funny = divertido, which also becomes divertida.", "Put it together: Mi hermana es alta y divertida."], "a": "Mi hermana es alta y divertida."}, "walk": [{"h": "Introducing yourself", "b": "Say Me llamo Alizah (My name is Alizah), Tengo catorce años (I am 14) and Vivo en Londres (I live in London). Add your family: Tengo un hermano y una hermana (I have a brother and a sister). Soy hijo único or hija única means I am an only child."}, {"h": "Ser and tener", "b": "Ser (to be) describes what someone is like: soy, eres, es, somos, sois, son. Tener (to have) is used for age, hair and eyes: tengo, tienes, tiene, tenemos, tenéis, tienen. So Mi padre es alto y tiene el pelo corto means My dad is tall and has short hair."}, {"h": "Adjective agreement", "b": "Adjectives match the noun: mi hermano es bajo, mi hermana es baja, mis hermanos son bajos, mis hermanas son bajas. Adjectives ending in -e or a consonant, like inteligente or joven, do not change for feminine, but still add -s or -es for plural: inteligentes, jóvenes. Trabajador becomes trabajadora for a female."}], "qs": [["What does Me llamo Lucía mean?", ["My name is Lucía", "I live with Lucía", "I call Lucía", "I am Lucía's friend"], "Me llamo literally means I call myself, used to give your name."], ["How do you say I am 13 years old in Spanish?", ["Tengo trece años", "Soy trece años", "Tengo tres años", "Estoy trece años"], "Spanish uses tener for age, so I have thirteen years."], ["What does mi abuela mean?", ["My grandmother", "My aunt", "My mother", "My cousin"], "Abuela is grandmother; abuelo is grandfather."], ["Choose the correct word: Mi madre ___ simpática.", ["es", "tiene", "son", "soy"], "Ser describes personality, and with mi madre it is es."], ["Choose the correct word: Mi hermana es ___.", ["baja", "bajo", "bajos", "bajas"], "Hermana is feminine singular, so bajo becomes baja."], ["Choose the correct word: Mis amigos son ___.", ["divertidos", "divertido", "divertida", "divertidas"], "Amigos is masculine plural, so add -s to divertido."], ["What does Tiene los ojos verdes mean?", ["He/She has green eyes", "He/She has green hair", "I have green eyes", "He/She is wearing green"], "Tiene means he or she has, and los ojos are the eyes."], ["Which is correct for My cousins (girls) are intelligent?", ["Mis primas son inteligentes", "Mis primas son inteligentas", "Mis primas son inteligente", "Mis primas tienen inteligentes"], "Inteligente does not change for feminine, but adds -s for plural."], ["Choose the correct word: Mis padres ___ el pelo negro.", ["tienen", "son", "tiene", "tenemos"], "Tener is used for hair, and with they (mis padres) it is tienen."], ["What does Me llevo bien con mi hermano mean?", ["I get on well with my brother", "I argue with my brother", "I take my brother out", "I look like my brother"], "Llevarse bien con means to get on well with someone."]], "bonus": ["In the sentence My tall brother laughs, which word is the adjective?", ["tall", "brother", "laughs", "My"], "An adjective describes a noun, and tall describes brother."]}, {"t": "School", "intro": "This topic covers school subjects, your opinions about them and the reasons why. Giving opinions with reasons is one of the easiest ways to make your Spanish sound more impressive.", "how": ["Learn subject names such as las matemáticas, el inglés, la historia, la geografía, las ciencias and el dibujo (art).", "Gustar works like to please: me gusta + one thing (me gusta el inglés), me gustan + plural things (me gustan las ciencias).", "Encantar (to love) works the same way: me encanta la música, me encantan las matemáticas.", "For dislike, put no in front: no me gusta la historia.", "Add a reason with porque (because), e.g. porque es fácil, porque es útil, porque es aburrido."], "example": {"q": "Translate into Spanish: I love maths because it is interesting.", "steps": ["Maths = las matemáticas, which is plural.", "Because it is plural, I love = me encantan (not me encanta).", "because = porque.", "they are interesting = son interesantes (agreeing with the plural subject).", "Put it together: Me encantan las matemáticas porque son interesantes."], "a": "Me encantan las matemáticas porque son interesantes."}, "walk": [{"h": "Subjects", "b": "Some subjects are masculine (el español, el dibujo), some feminine (la música, la historia) and some plural (las matemáticas, las ciencias). Unlike English, you keep the article: Me gusta el teatro (I like drama). Mi asignatura favorita es la música means My favourite subject is music."}, {"h": "Gustar and encantar", "b": "Me gusta literally means it pleases me, so the verb matches the thing liked. One thing: me gusta el inglés. Plural things: me gustan los idiomas. The same goes for encantar: me encanta la geografía, me encantan las ciencias. For strong dislike, odio means I hate: odio la historia."}, {"h": "Giving reasons", "b": "Join your opinion and reason with porque: No me gusta el dibujo porque es difícil (I don't like art because it is difficult). Useful adjectives: fácil (easy), difícil (difficult), útil (useful), aburrido (boring), interesante (interesting), divertido (fun). For a reason about the teacher: el profesor es muy simpático (the teacher is very nice)."}], "qs": [["What does la historia mean as a school subject?", ["History", "Geography", "Story time", "Science"], "La historia is history."], ["What does me encanta mean?", ["I love", "I hate", "I don't like", "I don't mind"], "Encantar shows strong liking: me encanta means I love it."], ["Which word means because?", ["porque", "pero", "también", "con"], "Porque means because; pero means but."], ["How do you say It's easy in Spanish?", ["Es fácil", "Es difícil", "Es aburrido", "Es útil"], "Fácil means easy; difícil means difficult."], ["Choose the correct word: Me ___ el inglés.", ["gusta", "gustan", "gusto", "gustas"], "El inglés is one thing, so use gusta."], ["Choose the correct word: Me ___ las ciencias.", ["gustan", "gusta", "gusto", "gustamos"], "Las ciencias is plural, so use gustan."], ["How do you say I don't like geography?", ["No me gusta la geografía", "Me no gusta la geografía", "No gusta la geografía", "Me gusta no la geografía"], "No goes right at the front, before me gusta."], ["What does Odio el dibujo porque es aburrido mean?", ["I hate art because it is boring", "I love art because it is fun", "I hate drawing because it is hard", "I like art because it is boring"], "Odio is I hate, el dibujo is art and aburrido is boring."], ["What does Mi asignatura favorita es la música mean?", ["My favourite subject is music", "My music teacher is my favourite", "I prefer music to other subjects", "I have music first"], "Asignatura means subject and favorita means favourite."], ["Which sentence is correct Spanish?", ["Me encantan los idiomas porque son útiles", "Me encanta los idiomas porque son útiles", "Me encantan idiomas porque es útil", "Encanto los idiomas porque son útiles"], "Idiomas is plural, so it needs encantan and son útiles."]], "bonus": ["Which word is the verb in I love maths?", ["love", "I", "maths", "There is no verb"], "A verb is a doing or being word, and love is the action."]}, {"t": "Free time and hobbies", "intro": "This topic is about what you do in your spare time, such as sport, music and going out. You will learn two key verbs, jugar and hacer, and how to say how often you do things.", "how": ["Use jugar a for sports and games: juego al fútbol, juego a los videojuegos.", "a + el joins up to make al: juego al tenis (not a el tenis).", "Use hacer for many activities: hago deporte, hago natación, hago equitación.", "Use tocar (not jugar) for musical instruments: toco la guitarra.", "Say how often with frequency words: siempre, a menudo, a veces, de vez en cuando, nunca."], "example": {"q": "Translate into Spanish: I often play football at the weekend.", "steps": ["I play (a sport) = juego a.", "a + el fútbol joins up to make al fútbol.", "often = a menudo, and at the weekend = el fin de semana.", "Put it together: A menudo juego al fútbol el fin de semana."], "a": "A menudo juego al fútbol el fin de semana."}, "walk": [{"h": "Jugar a", "b": "Jugar is a stem-changing verb, so u changes to ue: juego, juegas, juega, jugamos, jugáis, juegan. Always use a after it, and remember a + el = al: juego al baloncesto, juega a la pelota, jugamos a los videojuegos."}, {"h": "Hacer", "b": "Hacer means to do or make: hago, haces, hace, hacemos, hacéis, hacen. Use it for activities like hago natación (I go swimming), hago ciclismo (I go cycling) and hago los deberes (I do homework). Notice the yo form is hago, with a g."}, {"h": "How often?", "b": "Frequency words often go at the start or end of the sentence: A veces hago equitación (Sometimes I go horse riding). Others are todos los días (every day), una vez a la semana (once a week) and los fines de semana (at weekends). Nunca means never: Nunca juego al golf, or No juego nunca al golf."}], "qs": [["What does Juego al fútbol mean?", ["I play football", "I watch football", "I like football", "I played football"], "Juego is I play, and al fútbol is football."], ["What does a menudo mean?", ["Often", "Never", "Sometimes", "Always"], "A menudo means often; nunca means never."], ["What does nunca mean?", ["Never", "Always", "Every day", "Sometimes"], "Nunca means never."], ["Choose the correct words: Juego ___ tenis.", ["al", "a el", "el", "de"], "Jugar takes a, and a + el joins up to make al."], ["Choose the correct word: ___ natación los sábados.", ["Hago", "Juego", "Hacer", "Haco"], "Swimming uses hacer, and the yo form is hago."], ["Which verb do you use for playing a musical instrument?", ["tocar", "jugar", "hacer", "ir"], "Spanish uses tocar for instruments: toco el piano."], ["What does de vez en cuando mean?", ["From time to time", "All the time", "On time", "Every day"], "De vez en cuando means now and then, or from time to time."], ["Choose the correct word: Mis amigos ___ al baloncesto.", ["juegan", "juega", "jugan", "jugamos"], "Jugar changes u to ue, and with they it is juegan."], ["What does Hago equitación una vez a la semana mean?", ["I go horse riding once a week", "I go horse riding every weekend", "I go swimming once a week", "I did horse riding last week"], "Equitación is horse riding and una vez a la semana is once a week."], ["Choose the correct word: Nosotros ___ los deberes después del colegio.", ["hacemos", "hacen", "hago", "haceis"], "Hacer with nosotros is hacemos."]], "bonus": ["In English, which word in I sometimes play tennis tells you how often?", ["sometimes", "play", "tennis", "I"], "Sometimes is an adverb of frequency, just like a veces in Spanish."]}, {"t": "Talking about the past", "intro": "The preterite is the main Spanish past tense for saying what you did, like I played or I went. Using it well shows examiners you can handle more than one time frame, which is key for higher marks.", "how": ["Take off -ar, -er or -ir to find the stem, then add the preterite ending.", "-ar endings: -é, -aste, -ó, -amos, -asteis, -aron (hablé = I spoke).", "-er and -ir endings are the same: -í, -iste, -ió, -imos, -isteis, -ieron (comí = I ate, viví = I lived).", "Accents matter: hablo means I speak, but habló means he or she spoke.", "Ir (to go) and ser (to be) share the same irregular preterite: fui, fuiste, fue, fuimos, fuisteis, fueron."], "example": {"q": "Translate into Spanish: Yesterday I went to the beach and I swam.", "steps": ["Yesterday = ayer.", "I went uses the irregular ir: fui, and to the beach = a la playa.", "To swim is nadar, an -ar verb, so I swam = nadé.", "Put it together: Ayer fui a la playa y nadé."], "a": "Ayer fui a la playa y nadé."}, "walk": [{"h": "Regular -ar verbs", "b": "For jugar or hablar, take the stem and add -é, -aste, -ó, -amos, -asteis, -aron. So hablé (I spoke), hablaste (you spoke), habló (he/she spoke). Watch out: jugar becomes jugué in the yo form (the u keeps the hard g sound), then jugaste, jugó and so on."}, {"h": "Regular -er and -ir verbs", "b": "-er and -ir verbs share one set of endings: -í, -iste, -ió, -imos, -isteis, -ieron. So comer gives comí (I ate) and comió (he/she ate), and salir gives salí (I went out) and salieron (they went out). Bebí una limonada means I drank a lemonade."}, {"h": "Ir and ser", "b": "Ir (to go) and ser (to be) look exactly the same in the preterite: fui, fuiste, fue, fuimos, fuisteis, fueron. You work out which one it is from the sentence: Fui al cine means I went to the cinema, but Fue divertido means It was fun. Use time phrases like ayer (yesterday), el fin de semana pasado (last weekend) and el año pasado (last year)."}], "qs": [["What does ayer mean?", ["Yesterday", "Tomorrow", "Today", "Last year"], "Ayer means yesterday and signals the past tense."], ["What does Jugué al tenis mean?", ["I played tennis", "I play tennis", "I will play tennis", "He played tennis"], "Jugué is the yo form of jugar in the preterite."], ["What does fui mean in Fui al cine?", ["I went", "I was", "I go", "He went"], "Fui comes from ir here, because al cine shows movement to a place."], ["What is the yo form of hablar in the preterite?", ["hablé", "hablo", "habló", "hablí"], "-ar verbs take -é in the yo form of the preterite."], ["What is the yo form of comer in the preterite?", ["comí", "comé", "comió", "como"], "-er verbs take -í in the yo form of the preterite."], ["What does Mi madre compró un regalo mean?", ["My mother bought a present", "My mother buys a present", "I bought my mother a present", "My mother will buy a present"], "Compró has -ó, which is the he/she form of an -ar verb in the preterite."], ["Choose the correct word: Ayer mis amigos ___ (salir) a las ocho.", ["salieron", "salaron", "salimos", "salió"], "Salir is an -ir verb, and the they form ends in -ieron."], ["What does Fue increíble mean?", ["It was incredible", "I went incredibly", "It is incredible", "It will be incredible"], "Here fue comes from ser, so it means it was."], ["Choose the correct word: El fin de semana pasado nosotros ___ a Madrid.", ["fuimos", "fueron", "vamos", "fui"], "Ir with nosotros in the preterite is fuimos."], ["Translate: Last year my sister lived in Spain.", ["El año pasado mi hermana vivió en España", "El año pasado mi hermana vive en España", "El año pasado mi hermana vivó en España", "El año pasado mi hermana viví en España"], "Vivir is an -ir verb, so he/she form is vivió."]], "bonus": ["Which English sentence is in the past tense?", ["I visited my cousin", "I visit my cousin", "I will visit my cousin", "I am visiting my cousin"], "Visited describes something that has already happened."]}]}, {"id": "pe", "name": "Physical Education", "weeks": [{"t": "Skeleton and muscles", "intro": "Your skeleton and muscles work together to hold you up and make you move. In this topic you will learn what the skeleton does, the different types of bones and joints, the main muscles, and how muscles work in pairs. Knowing this helps you explain any sporting movement, from a kick to a jump shot.", "how": ["The skeleton gives support, protection, movement, shape, blood cell production and mineral storage.", "Long bones are for movement, short bones for stability, flat bones for protection, irregular bones for protection and muscle attachment.", "Hinge joints (elbow, knee) allow flexion and extension; ball and socket joints (shoulder, hip) allow movement in all directions.", "Tendons join muscle to bone, and ligaments join bone to bone.", "Muscles work in antagonistic pairs: the agonist contracts to cause the movement while the antagonist relaxes."], "example": {"q": "A footballer bends her leg at the knee to prepare to kick. Name the joint type, the movement, the agonist and the antagonist.", "steps": ["The knee is a hinge joint, so it can only bend and straighten.", "Bending the leg at the knee decreases the angle at the joint, so this is flexion.", "The muscle at the back of the thigh pulls to bend the knee, so the hamstrings are the agonist.", "The quadriceps at the front of the thigh relax to allow this, so they are the antagonist."], "a": "The knee is a hinge joint doing flexion, with the hamstrings as agonist and quadriceps as antagonist."}, "walk": [{"h": "What the skeleton does", "b": "The skeleton has several functions. It supports the body, protects organs (the cranium protects the brain, the ribs protect the heart and lungs) and gives muscles something to pull on to make movement. Bone marrow makes red and white blood cells, and bones store minerals such as calcium."}, {"h": "Bones and joints", "b": "Long bones like the femur and humerus act as levers for big movements. Short bones like the carpals in the wrist give stability, flat bones like the cranium and sternum protect organs, and irregular bones like the vertebrae protect the spinal cord. Where bones meet is a joint: hinge joints allow flexion and extension, while ball and socket joints also allow abduction (away from the midline), adduction (towards the midline), rotation and circumduction."}, {"h": "Muscles in pairs", "b": "Muscles can only pull, never push, so they work in antagonistic pairs. In a bicep curl, the biceps (agonist) contract to flex the elbow while the triceps (antagonist) relax; to straighten the arm the roles swap. Other pairs are hamstrings and quadriceps at the knee, and gastrocnemius and tibialis anterior at the ankle, where pointing the toes is plantar flexion and pulling them up is dorsiflexion."}], "qs": [["Which bone protects the brain?", ["Cranium", "Sternum", "Femur", "Scapula"], "The cranium (skull) is a flat bone that surrounds and protects the brain."], ["What joins a muscle to a bone?", ["A tendon", "A ligament", "Cartilage", "Synovial fluid"], "Tendons attach muscle to bone; ligaments attach bone to bone."], ["Which of these is a function of the skeleton?", ["Producing blood cells", "Digesting food", "Pumping blood", "Exchanging gases"], "Bone marrow inside some bones produces red and white blood cells."], ["What type of bone is the femur?", ["Long bone", "Short bone", "Flat bone", "Irregular bone"], "The femur is a long bone that acts as a lever for movement."], ["What type of joint is the shoulder?", ["Ball and socket", "Hinge", "Pivot", "Fixed"], "The shoulder is a ball and socket joint, allowing movement in all directions."], ["Which movement takes a limb away from the midline of the body?", ["Abduction", "Adduction", "Flexion", "Extension"], "Abduction means moving away from the midline; adduction brings the limb back towards it."], ["In the upward phase of a bicep curl, which muscle is the agonist?", ["Biceps", "Triceps", "Deltoid", "Pectorals"], "The biceps contract to flex the elbow, so they are the agonist while the triceps relax."], ["A sprinter pushes off the blocks by pointing the toes. What is this movement?", ["Plantar flexion", "Dorsiflexion", "Abduction", "Rotation"], "Pointing the toes away from the shin at the ankle is plantar flexion, caused by the gastrocnemius."], ["Which muscle works antagonistically with the quadriceps?", ["Hamstrings", "Gastrocnemius", "Gluteals", "Tibialis anterior"], "The quadriceps and hamstrings are an antagonistic pair at the knee."], ["Why are the vertebrae classed as irregular bones?", ["Their unusual shape protects the spinal cord", "They are long levers for movement", "They are flat to protect the heart", "They are small and cube-shaped"], "Vertebrae have a complex shape that protects the spinal cord and gives muscle attachment."]], "bonus": ["Which of these is an organ protected by the ribs?", ["The heart", "The brain", "The stomach", "The bladder"], "The rib cage surrounds and protects the heart and lungs."]}, {"t": "Heart, lungs and exercise", "intro": "The heart, blood vessels and lungs work as a team to get oxygen to your muscles and remove carbon dioxide. In this topic you will learn how blood travels, how breathing works, the two types of respiration and what exercise does to your body straight away and over time. This explains why you get out of breath and why fit people recover faster.", "how": ["The right side of the heart pumps deoxygenated blood to the lungs; the left side pumps oxygenated blood to the body.", "Arteries carry blood away from the heart, veins carry it back, and capillaries are where gases are exchanged.", "Aerobic respiration uses oxygen: glucose + oxygen → carbon dioxide + water (+ energy).", "Anaerobic respiration happens without oxygen: glucose → lactic acid (+ energy), which is fast but tiring.", "Cardiac output = stroke volume × heart rate, and minute ventilation = tidal volume × breathing rate."], "example": {"q": "A runner has a stroke volume of 70 ml and a heart rate of 80 beats per minute. What is her cardiac output?", "steps": ["Use the formula: cardiac output = stroke volume × heart rate.", "Put in the numbers: 70 ml × 80 beats per minute.", "70 × 80 = 5600, so the cardiac output is 5600 ml per minute.", "There are 1000 ml in a litre, so 5600 ml = 5.6 litres per minute."], "a": "Her cardiac output is 5600 ml (5.6 litres) per minute."}, "walk": [{"h": "Heart and blood vessels", "b": "The heart has four chambers: two atria at the top and two ventricles at the bottom. The left ventricle has the thickest wall because it pumps blood all round the body. Arteries have thick, muscular walls to cope with high pressure, veins have valves to stop blood flowing backwards, and capillaries have walls only one cell thick so oxygen and carbon dioxide can diffuse through."}, {"h": "Breathing and respiration", "b": "When you breathe in, the diaphragm contracts and flattens and the intercostal muscles lift the ribs up and out, so air rushes in. In the alveoli, oxygen diffuses into the blood and carbon dioxide diffuses out. A marathon runner mainly uses aerobic respiration, while a 100 m sprinter relies on anaerobic respiration, which makes lactic acid."}, {"h": "Effects of exercise", "b": "Straight after starting exercise your heart rate, breathing rate and depth, and body temperature all rise, and you may sweat. Afterwards you keep breathing hard to repay the oxygen debt, called EPOC (excess post-exercise oxygen consumption), which helps remove lactic acid. Training for months brings long-term effects such as a bigger, stronger heart, a larger stroke volume and a lower resting heart rate."}], "qs": [["Which blood vessels carry blood away from the heart?", ["Arteries", "Veins", "Capillaries", "Alveoli"], "Arteries carry blood away from the heart under high pressure."], ["Where in the lungs does gas exchange take place?", ["Alveoli", "Trachea", "Bronchi", "Diaphragm"], "The alveoli are tiny air sacs with a huge surface area where gases diffuse."], ["Which blood vessels contain valves to stop blood flowing backwards?", ["Veins", "Arteries", "Capillaries", "The aorta"], "Blood in veins is at low pressure, so valves stop it flowing the wrong way."], ["What does the diaphragm do when you breathe in?", ["Contracts and flattens", "Relaxes and domes upwards", "Pushes the ribs down", "Squeezes the lungs"], "When the diaphragm contracts it flattens, making the chest bigger so air flows in."], ["What is made during anaerobic respiration in muscles?", ["Lactic acid", "Oxygen", "Water", "Carbon dioxide only"], "Anaerobic respiration breaks down glucose without oxygen and produces lactic acid."], ["Which athlete relies most on aerobic respiration?", ["A marathon runner", "A 100 m sprinter", "A shot putter", "A high jumper"], "Long, steady events like the marathon use oxygen, so they are mainly aerobic."], ["Which side of the heart pumps deoxygenated blood to the lungs?", ["The right side", "The left side", "Both sides equally", "Neither side"], "The right side sends deoxygenated blood to the lungs to pick up oxygen."], ["What is EPOC?", ["Extra oxygen used after exercise to recover", "Oxygen used before a warm-up", "Air left in the lungs at rest", "Blood pumped in one beat"], "EPOC is the excess oxygen consumed after exercise to repay the oxygen debt."], ["A person's tidal volume is 0.5 litres and breathing rate is 12 per minute. What is their minute ventilation?", ["6 litres per minute", "0.6 litres per minute", "12.5 litres per minute", "24 litres per minute"], "Minute ventilation = tidal volume × breathing rate = 0.5 × 12 = 6 litres per minute."], ["Which is a long-term effect of regular aerobic training?", ["Lower resting heart rate", "Higher resting heart rate", "Smaller heart", "Smaller stroke volume"], "A stronger heart pumps more blood per beat, so it needs fewer beats at rest."]], "bonus": ["Which gas do our bodies need to take in from the air to respire aerobically?", ["Oxygen", "Carbon dioxide", "Nitrogen", "Hydrogen"], "Aerobic respiration needs oxygen and releases carbon dioxide as a waste product."]}, {"t": "Fitness and training", "intro": "Fitness is made up of different parts, and every sport needs a different mix of them. In this topic you will learn the components of fitness, how to test them, the principles of training and the main training methods. You will also learn to work out training zones from your heart rate.", "how": ["Components of fitness include cardiovascular endurance, muscular endurance, strength, speed, power, agility, balance, coordination, flexibility and reaction time.", "Each component has a test, such as the multi-stage fitness test for cardiovascular endurance and the sit and reach test for flexibility.", "SPORT stands for Specificity, Progression, Overload, Reversibility and Tedium.", "FITT stands for Frequency, Intensity, Time and Type, which are the ways you can overload.", "Maximum heart rate = 220 − age; the aerobic zone is 60–80% of it and the anaerobic zone is 80–90%."], "example": {"q": "Sam is 20 years old. What is his aerobic training zone?", "steps": ["Work out maximum heart rate: 220 − age = 220 − 20 = 200 bpm.", "The aerobic zone is 60% to 80% of maximum heart rate.", "60% of 200 = 0.6 × 200 = 120 bpm.", "80% of 200 = 0.8 × 200 = 160 bpm."], "a": "Sam's aerobic training zone is 120 to 160 beats per minute."}, "walk": [{"h": "Components and tests", "b": "Each component of fitness has its own test. Cardiovascular endurance is tested with the multi-stage fitness test or Cooper 12-minute run, speed with a 30 m sprint, strength with a handgrip dynamometer, power with a vertical jump, agility with the Illinois agility run, balance with the stork stand and reaction time with the ruler drop. Power combines strength and speed, which is why a long jumper needs lots of it."}, {"h": "Principles of training", "b": "Specificity means training matches your sport, so a swimmer should swim. Progression means building up gradually, overload means working harder than normal, reversibility means fitness is lost if you stop, and tedium means you should vary training so it does not get boring. You can overload by changing the FITT factors: train more often, harder, for longer or in a different way."}, {"h": "Methods and heart rate zones", "b": "Continuous training is steady exercise for at least 20 minutes, fartlek means speed play with changes of pace or terrain, and interval training mixes hard work with rest. Circuit training uses stations, weight training builds strength, and plyometrics uses jumping to build power. For a 40-year-old, maximum heart rate is 220 − 40 = 180 bpm, so the aerobic zone is 108 to 144 bpm."}], "qs": [["Which fitness test measures flexibility?", ["Sit and reach test", "Handgrip dynamometer", "30 m sprint", "Ruler drop test"], "The sit and reach test measures flexibility of the hamstrings and lower back."], ["Which fitness test measures cardiovascular endurance?", ["Multi-stage fitness test", "Vertical jump test", "Stork stand test", "Handgrip dynamometer"], "The multi-stage (bleep) test measures how long you can keep running, so it tests stamina."], ["What does the O stand for in SPORT?", ["Overload", "Organisation", "Oxygen", "Outcome"], "Overload means making the body work harder than it normally does."], ["Which principle says fitness is lost if you stop training?", ["Reversibility", "Progression", "Specificity", "Tedium"], "Reversibility means improvements are lost when training stops, for example after injury."], ["What is the maximum heart rate of a 15-year-old?", ["205 bpm", "235 bpm", "200 bpm", "215 bpm"], "Maximum heart rate = 220 − age = 220 − 15 = 205 bpm."], ["Which training method involves changes of speed and terrain, like running uphill then jogging?", ["Fartlek training", "Continuous training", "Weight training", "Plyometrics"], "Fartlek means speed play: changing pace and terrain during a continuous run."], ["Power is a combination of which two components?", ["Strength and speed", "Balance and agility", "Flexibility and coordination", "Speed and reaction time"], "Power = strength × speed, as used in jumping or throwing."], ["In FITT, what does the I stand for?", ["Intensity", "Interval", "Improvement", "Injury"], "Intensity means how hard you work, for example running faster."], ["What is the aerobic training zone (60–80%) for a 30-year-old?", ["114 to 152 bpm", "120 to 160 bpm", "152 to 171 bpm", "108 to 144 bpm"], "Max HR = 220 − 30 = 190; 0.6 × 190 = 114 and 0.8 × 190 = 152 bpm."], ["A 40-year-old trains at 90% of maximum heart rate. What heart rate is this?", ["162 bpm", "144 bpm", "171 bpm", "180 bpm"], "Max HR = 220 − 40 = 180; 0.9 × 180 = 162 bpm, which is in the anaerobic zone."]], "bonus": ["What is 80% of 200?", ["160", "180", "120", "140"], "80% means 0.8 × 200 = 160, a skill you need to work out training zones."]}, {"t": "Sports psychology and health", "intro": "Sport is not just about the body: the mind matters too. In this topic you will learn the difference between skill and ability, how to set good goals, how coaches guide and give feedback, and how exercise improves physical, mental and social health. These ideas help you learn faster and live a healthier life.", "how": ["A skill is learned through practice, while an ability is something you are born with, like good balance or quick reactions.", "SMART goals are Specific, Measurable, Achievable, Realistic and Time-bound.", "The four types of guidance are visual (showing), verbal (telling), manual (physically guiding) and mechanical (using equipment).", "Feedback can be intrinsic (from your own feelings) or extrinsic (from others), and positive or negative.", "Health is a state of complete physical, mental and social well-being, not just the absence of illness."], "example": {"q": "Is this a SMART goal? \"I want to get better at running.\" Improve it.", "steps": ["Check Specific: \"better at running\" is vague, so name an event, such as the 1500 m.", "Check Measurable: add a number you can check, such as a time.", "Check Achievable and Realistic: aim for a small improvement on your current best, for example 10 seconds.", "Check Time-bound: add a deadline, such as by the end of the summer term."], "a": "It is not SMART; a better goal is \"I will cut 10 seconds off my 1500 m time by the end of the summer term.\""}, "walk": [{"h": "Skill, ability and goals", "b": "A skill, such as a tennis serve, has to be learned and practised. Abilities, such as coordination or reaction time, are inherited and help you learn skills more easily. Setting SMART goals keeps you motivated, because you can see your progress and know when you have reached your target."}, {"h": "Guidance and feedback", "b": "Beginners learn well from visual guidance, like watching a demonstration, while manual guidance means the coach physically moves your body into position. Mechanical guidance uses equipment, such as a float in swimming. Feedback can be knowledge of results (did the shot go in?) or knowledge of performance (how good was your technique?); beginners need lots of positive extrinsic feedback, while experts can use intrinsic feedback from how a movement felt."}, {"h": "Health and well-being", "b": "Exercise improves physical health by strengthening the heart and lowering the risk of illnesses such as type 2 diabetes. It helps mental health by reducing stress and releasing feel-good hormones like serotonin. It boosts social health by helping you make friends and work in a team, for example at a local cycling or football club."}], "qs": [["What does the M stand for in SMART goals?", ["Measurable", "Motivating", "Maximum", "Mental"], "A measurable goal has a number or result you can check, like a time or score."], ["A coach shows a student a video of a perfect forward roll. What type of guidance is this?", ["Visual", "Verbal", "Manual", "Mechanical"], "Showing a demonstration or video is visual guidance."], ["Which of these is an ability rather than a skill?", ["Good hand-eye coordination", "A tennis serve", "A penalty kick", "A lay-up shot"], "Coordination is an inherited trait; the others are skills learned through practice."], ["A swimming teacher gives a learner a float to help them. What type of guidance is this?", ["Mechanical", "Manual", "Visual", "Verbal"], "Using equipment or an aid is mechanical guidance."], ["Which is an example of social well-being from sport?", ["Making new friends at a club", "Lower blood pressure", "Stronger bones", "Feeling less stressed"], "Social well-being is about friendships, belonging and working with others."], ["Which is a mental health benefit of exercise?", ["Reduced stress and anxiety", "Improved stroke volume", "Increased muscle size", "Making friends"], "Exercise releases feel-good hormones like serotonin, which reduce stress."], ["A gymnast feels that her landing was balanced. What type of feedback is this?", ["Intrinsic", "Extrinsic", "Knowledge of results", "Mechanical"], "Intrinsic feedback comes from inside, from how the movement felt to the performer."], ["A coach tells a player their shooting technique was good. What type of feedback is this?", ["Knowledge of performance", "Knowledge of results", "Intrinsic feedback", "Manual guidance"], "Knowledge of performance is about the quality of the technique, not the outcome."], ["Which of these is the best SMART goal?", ["Score 3 more goals than last season by May", "Be the best player in the world", "Try harder at football", "Get fitter soon"], "It is specific, measurable, realistic and has a deadline."], ["Why does manual guidance suit a beginner learning a dangerous skill?", ["The coach supports them, so it is safer", "It needs no coach at all", "It is only for experts", "It uses long explanations"], "Manual guidance means the coach physically supports the performer, reducing risk and building confidence."]], "bonus": ["Which of these is a healthy habit?", ["Exercising for 60 minutes a day", "Sitting at a screen all evening", "Skipping breakfast every day", "Sleeping only 4 hours a night"], "UK guidance says young people should be active for about 60 minutes each day."]}]}, {"id": "resistant-materials", "name": "Resistant Materials", "weeks": [{"t": "Timbers", "intro": "Timber is wood that has been cut and prepared for making things, from furniture to toys. It comes in three main groups: hardwoods, softwoods and manufactured boards. Knowing their properties helps you pick the right wood for each job, which is a big part of GCSE D&T.", "how": ["Hardwoods come from deciduous, broad-leaved trees, such as oak, beech, ash and mahogany.", "Softwoods come from coniferous trees with needles and cones, such as pine, spruce and cedar.", "Manufactured boards like MDF, plywood and chipboard are made by gluing wood fibres, chips or layers together.", "Choose a timber by thinking about its properties: strength, appearance, durability, cost and how easy it is to work.", "When planning cuts from a sheet, divide each side of the sheet by the matching side of the piece and multiply."], "example": {"q": "How many 300 mm × 200 mm pieces of MDF can be cut from a 1200 mm × 600 mm sheet, ignoring saw cuts?", "steps": ["Pieces along the length: 1200 ÷ 300 = 4.", "Pieces along the width: 600 ÷ 200 = 3.", "Multiply the two: 4 × 3 = 12.", "Check with area: 1200 × 600 = 720 000 mm² and 300 × 200 = 60 000 mm², so 720 000 ÷ 60 000 = 12."], "a": "12 pieces can be cut from the sheet."}, "walk": [{"h": "Hardwoods and softwoods", "b": "The name tells you the type of tree, not how hard the wood feels. Hardwoods come from broad-leaved trees that usually lose their leaves in autumn and grow slowly, so they are often more expensive. Softwoods come from evergreen conifers that grow quickly, so they are cheaper. For example, oak is a hardwood used for quality furniture, and pine is a softwood used for building frames."}, {"h": "Manufactured boards", "b": "Manufactured boards are made from wood waste or thin layers, glued with resin. MDF is wood fibres pressed together, giving a smooth surface with no grain or knots, great for painting. Plywood is thin layers called veneers glued with the grain at 90° to each other, usually an odd number like 3, 5 or 7, which makes it strong in all directions. Boards come in large flat sheets, often 2440 mm × 1220 mm."}, {"h": "Choosing the right timber", "b": "Match the property to the product. Oak is hard, strong and attractive, so it suits a dining table. Cedar resists rot, so it suits garden sheds and fences. Balsa is very light and easy to cut, so it suits model aircraft, and surprisingly it is a hardwood."}], "qs": [["Hardwoods come from which type of tree?", ["Deciduous, broad-leaved trees", "Coniferous trees with needles", "Any tree whose wood feels hard", "Only palm trees in hot countries"], "Hardwoods are named after broad-leaved deciduous trees, not how hard the wood feels."], ["Which of these is a softwood?", ["Pine", "Oak", "Beech", "Mahogany"], "Pine comes from a coniferous tree, so it is a softwood; the others are hardwoods."], ["Which of these is a hardwood?", ["Oak", "Pine", "Spruce", "Larch"], "Oak comes from a broad-leaved deciduous tree, while pine, spruce and larch are conifers."], ["What is MDF made from?", ["Wood fibres glued together with resin", "Thin veneers glued with grain at 90°", "Large wood chips pressed together", "Solid planks joined edge to edge"], "MDF stands for medium density fibreboard: fine wood fibres bonded with resin."], ["Why are plywood layers glued with the grain at 90° to each other?", ["To make it strong and stable in all directions", "To make it completely waterproof", "To make it lighter than balsa", "To give it a smooth surface for paint"], "Crossing the grain stops the board splitting and warping, so it is strong in every direction."], ["How many layers does plywood usually have?", ["An odd number, such as 3, 5 or 7", "Always exactly 2", "An even number, such as 4 or 6", "One single thick layer"], "An odd number of layers keeps the outer grain going the same way, so the board stays balanced and flat."], ["Balsa is very soft and light. How is it classed?", ["As a hardwood", "As a softwood", "As a manufactured board", "As a polymer"], "Balsa comes from a broad-leaved tree, so it is a hardwood even though it is soft."], ["Why are softwoods usually cheaper than hardwoods?", ["They grow faster, so they are quicker to replace", "They are always imported from abroad", "They are much heavier than hardwoods", "They have no grain at all"], "Conifers grow quickly, so softwood is more plentiful and cheaper."], ["Which material is best for a cheap shelf that will be painted smoothly?", ["MDF, because it is smooth with no grain or knots", "Oak, because it is the hardest wood", "Balsa, because it is very light", "Cedar, because it resists rot outdoors"], "MDF is cheap and has an even, smooth surface that takes paint well."], ["How many 300 mm × 200 mm pieces can be cut from a 1500 mm × 600 mm sheet, ignoring saw cuts?", ["15", "10", "12", "18"], "1500 ÷ 300 = 5 and 600 ÷ 200 = 3, so 5 × 3 = 15 pieces."]], "bonus": ["How many millimetres are there in 1 metre?", ["1000", "100", "10", "10 000"], "1 m = 100 cm and 1 cm = 10 mm, so 1 m = 100 × 10 = 1000 mm, the unit used in D&T drawings."]}, {"t": "Metals and alloys", "intro": "Metals are strong materials used for everything from bike frames to saucepans. They are split into ferrous metals, which contain iron, and non-ferrous metals, which do not. Mixing metals to make alloys lets designers improve properties like strength and resistance to rust.", "how": ["Ferrous metals contain iron, such as mild steel, cast iron and stainless steel; most rust and are picked up by a magnet.", "Non-ferrous metals contain no iron, such as aluminium, copper, zinc and tin; they do not rust.", "An alloy is a mixture of a metal with other elements, such as brass (copper and zinc) or stainless steel (steel with chromium).", "Learn the key properties: hardness, malleability, ductility, toughness and resistance to corrosion.", "Choose a metal by matching its properties to where and how the product will be used."], "example": {"q": "A garden gate latch will be outside in the rain all year. Should it be made from plain mild steel or stainless steel?", "steps": ["Think about the conditions: the latch will be wet for long periods.", "Mild steel is a ferrous metal that rusts (corrodes) when exposed to water and air.", "Stainless steel is an alloy containing chromium, which stops it rusting.", "So stainless steel will last longer without needing paint or another finish."], "a": "Stainless steel, because the chromium in it resists corrosion in wet conditions."}, "walk": [{"h": "Ferrous or non-ferrous", "b": "Ferrous means containing iron. Mild steel is cheap and strong, so it is used for car bodies and gates, but it rusts unless it is painted or coated. Non-ferrous metals like aluminium and copper do not rust. A quick magnet test helps: a magnet picks up mild steel but not aluminium or copper."}, {"h": "What alloys are", "b": "An alloy is a metal mixed with other elements to give better properties. Brass is copper and zinc, which is hard-wearing and looks gold, so it is used for door handles. Bronze is copper and tin. Stainless steel is steel with chromium (and often nickel), so it resists rust and is used for cutlery and sinks."}, {"h": "Key metal properties", "b": "Hardness means resisting scratches, wear and dents, like a drill bit. Malleability means being hammered or pressed into shape without cracking, like aluminium foil. Ductility means being stretched into a wire, like copper cable. Corrosion is when a metal is slowly eaten away by reacting with air and water, like iron rusting."}], "qs": [["What do all ferrous metals contain?", ["Iron", "Copper", "Aluminium", "Zinc"], "Ferrous comes from the Latin word for iron, so all ferrous metals contain iron."], ["Which of these is a non-ferrous metal?", ["Aluminium", "Mild steel", "Cast iron", "Stainless steel"], "Aluminium contains no iron; all the steels and cast iron do."], ["What is an alloy?", ["A metal mixed with other elements", "A pure metal with nothing added", "A metal with a coat of paint", "A metal that has gone rusty"], "Alloys are mixtures made to improve a metal's properties."], ["Brass is an alloy of which two metals?", ["Copper and zinc", "Copper and tin", "Iron and carbon", "Aluminium and zinc"], "Brass is copper and zinc; copper and tin makes bronze."], ["Which element mainly stops stainless steel from rusting?", ["Chromium", "Lead", "Copper", "Extra carbon"], "Chromium forms a thin protective layer on the surface that resists corrosion."], ["What does malleable mean?", ["It can be hammered or pressed into shape without cracking", "It can be stretched into a thin wire", "It resists scratches and wear", "It never rusts"], "Malleability is about squashing and shaping, like pressing a car body panel."], ["What does ductile mean?", ["It can be stretched into a wire without breaking", "It can be hammered flat without cracking", "It resists scratching and denting", "It conducts heat well"], "Ductility is about pulling and stretching, which is why copper is drawn into wire."], ["A drill bit must be very hard. What does hardness mean?", ["Resisting scratches, wear and dents", "Absorbing a sudden impact", "Bending easily without breaking", "Being very light for its size"], "Hardness is resistance to wear and indentation; absorbing impact is toughness."], ["Why does a mild steel bike shed frame need a finish such as paint?", ["Mild steel rusts when exposed to air and water", "Mild steel is non-ferrous", "Mild steel is too soft to hold together", "Paint makes the steel more malleable"], "Mild steel is a ferrous metal that corrodes, so a finish keeps water and air out."], ["Why is aluminium often used for lightweight bike frames?", ["It is light and resists corrosion", "It is ferrous and very magnetic", "It is the heaviest common metal", "It is an alloy of copper and tin"], "Aluminium has a low density and does not rust, so frames are light and long-lasting."]], "bonus": ["Which of these is a pure element rather than a mixture?", ["Iron", "Steel", "Brass", "Bronze"], "Iron is a single element; steel, brass and bronze are alloys, which are mixtures."]}, {"t": "Polymers", "intro": "Polymers, often called plastics, are made of long chain molecules and are mostly made from crude oil. They are split into thermoplastics, which can be reshaped with heat, and thermosetting polymers, which set permanently. Polymers are used in huge numbers of products because they can be shaped easily, are waterproof and come in many colours.", "how": ["Thermoplastics soften when heated and can be reshaped many times, such as acrylic, HIPS and polypropylene.", "Thermosetting polymers set permanently when first heated and cannot be reshaped, such as epoxy resin and melamine formaldehyde.", "Vacuum forming heats a sheet (often HIPS) and sucks it over a mould to make trays and packaging.", "Line bending uses a strip heater to soften acrylic along a straight line so it can be bent.", "Injection moulding forces melted polymer into a mould and is used for mass production, while laser cutting cuts flat shapes accurately."], "example": {"q": "A company needs 500 thin plastic trays to hold chocolates in a box. Which process and polymer should they use?", "steps": ["The tray is thin with a hollow shape, made from a sheet.", "500 is a batch, not millions, so an expensive injection mould is not worth it.", "Vacuum forming makes thin hollow shapes from sheet using a cheap mould.", "HIPS is a thermoplastic sheet that vacuum forms well and is cheap."], "a": "Vacuum forming using HIPS sheet, because it makes thin hollow trays cheaply in batches."}, "walk": [{"h": "Two families of polymer", "b": "Thermoplastics have chains that slide apart when heated, so they soften and can be reshaped and often recycled. Acrylic is used for signs, HIPS for packaging and polypropylene for lunchboxes and living hinges on flip-top lids. Thermosetting polymers form strong cross-links when they set, so they never soften again. Epoxy resin is used as a strong glue, and melamine formaldehyde for heat-resistant kitchen surfaces."}, {"h": "Shaping sheet polymers", "b": "In vacuum forming, a HIPS sheet is heated until floppy, a mould is raised into it, and air is sucked out so the sheet wraps the mould. Moulds need tapered sides (a draft angle) and rounded corners so they can be removed. In line bending, a strip heater softens acrylic along one line, then it is bent, often on a jig, to make things like phone stands."}, {"h": "Moulding and cutting", "b": "Injection moulding melts polymer granules and forces them into a closed metal mould under high pressure. The mould is very expensive, so it is used for mass production, such as bottle tops and toy bricks. Laser cutting uses a CAD drawing to cut acrylic, MDF or plywood very accurately. PVC must never be laser cut because it gives off toxic chlorine gas."}], "qs": [["What is special about a thermoplastic?", ["It can be reheated and reshaped many times", "It sets permanently and cannot be reshaped", "It is made from wood fibres", "It is a type of metal alloy"], "Thermoplastics soften every time they are heated, so they can be reshaped."], ["What is special about a thermosetting polymer?", ["Once set, it cannot be softened by heating again", "It softens every time it is heated", "It is always see-through", "It is always very bendy"], "Thermosets form permanent cross-links, so heat will not soften them again."], ["Which of these is a thermosetting polymer?", ["Epoxy resin", "Acrylic", "HIPS", "Polypropylene"], "Epoxy resin sets permanently; the other three are thermoplastics."], ["Which polymer sheet is most often used for vacuum forming in schools?", ["HIPS", "Epoxy resin", "Melamine formaldehyde", "Urea formaldehyde"], "HIPS (high impact polystyrene) is a cheap thermoplastic sheet that forms well."], ["Why should a vacuum forming mould have tapered (draft) sides?", ["So the mould can be removed from the formed sheet", "To make the plastic sheet thicker", "To stop the plastic from melting", "To make the plastic see-through"], "Tapered sides let the mould slide out instead of getting stuck."], ["What equipment is used for line bending acrylic?", ["A strip heater", "A laser cutter", "A vacuum former", "An injection moulder"], "A strip heater softens the acrylic along a narrow line so it can be bent."], ["Which polymer is used for living hinges, like a flip-top shampoo lid?", ["Polypropylene", "Acrylic", "Epoxy resin", "Melamine formaldehyde"], "Polypropylene is tough and flexible, so it can bend thousands of times without snapping."], ["Injection moulding is best suited to which type of production?", ["Mass production of many identical items", "A single one-off prototype", "Bending sheets along a line", "Cutting flat shapes from sheet"], "The mould is very expensive, so it is only worth it for very large numbers."], ["Why must PVC never be cut on a laser cutter?", ["It gives off toxic chlorine gas", "It is far too hard for the laser", "It is a thermosetting polymer", "It is too transparent to cut"], "Burning PVC releases harmful chlorine gas that can hurt people and damage the machine."], ["Why is a saucepan handle made from a thermosetting polymer?", ["It will not soften when it gets hot", "It can easily be reshaped by heating", "It is the cheapest thermoplastic", "It melts to grip the pan better"], "Thermosets stay rigid when heated, so the handle stays safe and solid near heat."]], "bonus": ["Most polymers are made from which raw material?", ["Crude oil", "Iron ore", "Trees", "Sand"], "Most plastics are made from chemicals extracted from crude oil, a finite resource."]}, {"t": "Tools, joining and safety", "intro": "This topic covers how you actually make things in the workshop: marking out, cutting, shaping, joining and finishing. Using the right tool and process gives accurate, good-looking results. Working safely matters most of all, so you protect yourself and others.", "how": ["Mark out accurately: use a try square for 90° lines and a marking gauge for lines parallel to an edge on wood, and a scriber on metal.", "Use the right saw: a tenon saw for straight cuts in wood, a coping saw for curves and a hacksaw for metal.", "Choose joints and adhesives to suit the materials, such as PVA for wood and Tensol cement for acrylic.", "Apply a finish to protect and improve appearance, such as varnish on wood or powder coating on metal.", "Always wear the right PPE, tie back long hair, follow teacher instructions and never leave a machine running."], "example": {"q": "List the safe steps for drilling a hole in a piece of acrylic on a pillar drill.", "steps": ["Put on safety goggles, tie back long hair and tuck in loose clothing; do not wear gloves.", "Mark the hole position and clamp the acrylic in a machine vice so it cannot spin.", "Fit the correct drill bit, remove the chuck key and lower the guard.", "Switch on, feed the drill down slowly and steadily, then switch off and wait for it to stop."], "a": "Wear goggles, secure hair and clothing, clamp the work, use the guard and drill slowly."}, "walk": [{"h": "Marking out and cutting", "b": "Accurate marking makes accurate products. On wood, a try square marks lines at 90° to an edge and a marking gauge marks lines parallel to an edge. On metal, a scriber scratches a clear line and a centre punch makes a small dent so the drill bit does not wander. Then cut with a tenon saw for straight wood cuts, a coping saw for curves, or a hacksaw for metal, and smooth edges with files and glasspaper."}, {"h": "Joints and adhesives", "b": "A butt joint is simple but weak, because there is little gluing area. Stronger joints like housing, finger (comb), dowel and mortise and tenon joints have more gluing surface and lock together. PVA glue is used for wood, Tensol cement for acrylic and epoxy resin for joining different materials, such as metal to wood."}, {"h": "Finishes and safety", "b": "Finishes protect a product and make it look good. Varnish seals wood against moisture and dirt while showing the grain. Powder coating sprays charged powder onto metal, which is then baked in an oven to form a tough, even coat, often used on bike frames. In the workshop, use PPE such as goggles and aprons, know where the emergency stop is, and never use machines without permission."}], "qs": [["Which PPE protects your eyes when using a pillar drill?", ["Safety goggles", "An apron", "Gloves", "Ear defenders"], "Goggles stop swarf and chips flying into your eyes; gloves can get caught in a drill."], ["Which tool marks a line at 90° to the edge of a piece of wood?", ["Try square", "Marking gauge", "Coping saw", "Centre punch"], "The try square's blade sits at exactly 90° to its stock, which rests against the edge."], ["Which tool marks a line parallel to the edge of a piece of wood?", ["Marking gauge", "Try square", "Tenon saw", "Hacksaw"], "A marking gauge has a pin set at a fixed distance that slides along the edge."], ["Which tool scratches a clear marking line onto metal?", ["Scriber", "Pencil", "Marking gauge", "Tenon saw"], "Pencil does not show well on metal, so a hardened steel scriber scratches a fine line."], ["Which saw is best for cutting curves in thin wood or acrylic?", ["Coping saw", "Tenon saw", "Hacksaw", "Panel saw"], "A coping saw has a thin blade that can turn to follow curves."], ["Which wood joint is weakest because it has very little gluing area?", ["Butt joint", "Mortise and tenon joint", "Dovetail joint", "Finger joint"], "A butt joint just glues two flat ends together, so there is little surface to bond."], ["Which adhesive is normally used to join two pieces of wood?", ["PVA", "Tensol cement", "Polystyrene cement", "Glue stick"], "PVA soaks into wood fibres and sets strong; Tensol cement is for acrylic."], ["Why would you varnish a wooden toy?", ["To protect it from moisture and dirt while showing the grain", "To make the wood more flexible", "To hide all of the grain completely", "To make the wood magnetic"], "Varnish forms a clear, protective seal on the surface of the wood."], ["How is powder coating applied to metal?", ["Charged powder is sprayed on, then baked in an oven", "The metal is dipped into molten zinc", "Wax is rubbed on with a soft cloth", "Varnish is brushed on in thin coats"], "The powder melts in the oven to form a tough, even coating; dipping in zinc is galvanising."], ["Why do you use a centre punch before drilling a hole in metal?", ["To make a dent so the drill bit does not wander", "To mark a line parallel to the edge", "To remove sharp burrs after drilling", "To cool the drill bit down"], "The small dent guides the tip of the drill bit so the hole starts in the right place."]], "bonus": ["In safety, what is a hazard?", ["Something that could cause harm", "A rule you must always follow", "A tool used for measuring", "A type of protective clothing"], "A hazard is anything that could hurt someone, like a sharp blade or a trailing cable."]}]}, {"id": "art", "name": "Drawing & Painting", "weeks": [{"t": "The formal elements", "intro": "The formal elements are the building blocks every artist uses: line, tone, shape, form, texture, colour, pattern and composition. Knowing them gives you the words to talk about any artwork, and the tools to plan your own. Examiners expect you to use these words in your annotations.", "how": ["Line is a mark that joins two points; it can be thick, thin, smooth, broken or expressive.", "Tone means how light or dark something is, and it is what makes flat drawings look solid.", "Shape is flat and two-dimensional (2D), while form is three-dimensional (3D) and has volume.", "Texture is how a surface feels or looks like it would feel, and pattern is a design that repeats.", "Composition is how everything is arranged in the picture, including where the eye is drawn first."], "example": {"q": "You are drawing an apple on a table. Which formal elements would you use, and how?", "steps": ["Use line lightly to sketch the outline and the table edge.", "Add tone, light where the light hits and dark on the shadow side, to turn the flat shape into a 3D form.", "Show texture with tiny marks for the speckled skin and the shine of the highlight.", "Plan the composition by placing the apple slightly off-centre, roughly on a rule-of-thirds line."], "a": "Line for the outline, tone to create form, marks for texture and an off-centre composition make a strong, believable drawing."}, "walk": [{"h": "Line and shape", "b": "Line is the simplest mark you can make, and when a line joins up it creates a shape. A circle drawn on paper is a shape: it is flat and has only height and width. Try varying your line: press harder for a bold edge and lighter for a soft one."}, {"h": "Tone, form and texture", "b": "When you add tone (light and dark) to a circle, it starts to look like a ball, which is a form. Texture is the surface quality, such as rough bark or smooth glass. Actual texture can be touched, while implied texture is drawn to look rough or smooth."}, {"h": "Colour, pattern, composition", "b": "Colour adds mood: blues can feel calm while reds feel lively. Pattern is a repeated design, like stripes on a shirt or scales on a fish. Composition ties it all together; the rule of thirds splits the page into a 3 × 3 grid, and placing the main subject on a line or crossing point often looks more interesting than dead centre."}], "qs": [["Which formal element describes how light or dark something is?", ["Tone", "Texture", "Pattern", "Line"], "Tone is the lightness or darkness of a colour or area."], ["What is the main difference between shape and form?", ["Shape is flat (2D); form is 3D", "Shape is 3D; form is flat (2D)", "Shape is coloured; form is not", "There is no difference"], "A shape has only height and width, while a form also has depth."], ["A design that repeats, like stripes or spots, is called…", ["Pattern", "Texture", "Composition", "Tone"], "Pattern is a repeated arrangement of lines, shapes or colours."], ["How a surface feels, or looks like it would feel, is…", ["Texture", "Tone", "Form", "Line"], "Texture describes the surface quality, such as rough, smooth or furry."], ["Drawing to make paper look like rough bark shows…", ["Implied (visual) texture", "Actual texture", "Pattern", "Two-point perspective"], "It looks rough but the paper is smooth, so the texture is implied."], ["Which of these is a 3D form rather than a 2D shape?", ["Sphere", "Circle", "Square", "Triangle"], "A sphere has depth and volume, whereas the others are flat shapes."], ["What does 'composition' mean in art?", ["How elements are arranged in the artwork", "The type of paint used", "The size of the paper only", "The darkest tone in a drawing"], "Composition is the arrangement of everything in the picture."], ["The rule of thirds divides the picture into a grid of…", ["9 equal parts (3 × 3)", "4 equal parts (2 × 2)", "3 equal parts", "6 equal parts (2 × 3)"], "Two lines across and two lines down make a 3 × 3 grid of 9 parts."], ["What is the 'focal point' of a composition?", ["The area that draws the eye first", "The edge of the paper", "The lightest pencil you use", "The horizon line"], "The focal point is the main point of interest the viewer notices first."], ["Which change would turn a flat circle into a convincing ball?", ["Adding tone from light to dark", "Adding a thicker outline", "Colouring it one flat colour", "Drawing a pattern of stripes"], "Gradual tone shows light falling on a curved surface, creating form."]], "bonus": ["Which of these is a flat 2D shape?", ["Square", "Cube", "Cylinder", "Cone"], "A square has only length and width; the others are 3D solids."]}, {"t": "Drawing skills", "intro": "Drawing is the foundation of all art and design, and observational drawing means drawing what you actually see, not what you think something looks like. Good drawing uses a range of marks, careful shading, accurate proportion and perspective. These skills help you record ideas for the AO3 assessment objective.", "how": ["Look at your subject more than your paper, and draw what you see rather than what you expect.", "Use H pencils for light, fine lines and B pencils (such as 2B, 4B, 6B) for darker, softer tones.", "Shade with hatching (parallel lines), cross-hatching (crossing lines), stippling (dots) or smooth blending.", "Check proportion by measuring with your pencil held at arm's length, closing one eye.", "In perspective, lines going away from you meet at a vanishing point on the horizon line (your eye level)."], "example": {"q": "How do you shade a sphere so it looks 3D, with light coming from the top left?", "steps": ["Draw a light circle and decide the light comes from the top left.", "Leave a small highlight of white paper near the top left.", "Shade gradually darker towards the bottom right, making a band of core shadow just inside the edge.", "Leave a slightly lighter strip of reflected light right at the bottom-right edge.", "Add a dark cast shadow on the table to the bottom right, darkest where the ball touches it."], "a": "A highlight, smooth gradation to a core shadow, reflected light and a cast shadow make the sphere look solid and lit from the top left."}, "walk": [{"h": "Observe and make marks", "b": "Observational drawing trains your eye to see real shapes, angles and tones. Mark-making means experimenting with different marks, such as dots, dashes, scribbles and smudges, using pencil, pen, charcoal or ink. For example, quick short dashes can suggest grass, while a smudged charcoal patch suggests a stormy sky."}, {"h": "Shading and tone", "b": "Hatching uses parallel lines; drawing them closer together makes the area look darker. Cross-hatching adds another layer of lines at an angle for even darker tones. Stippling builds tone with dots, and blending uses a soft pencil smoothed gently for a gradual change. A tonal scale, a strip of boxes from white to very dark, is a great warm-up."}, {"h": "Proportion and perspective", "b": "Proportion is getting sizes right compared with each other: on a face, the eyes sit about halfway down the head. One-point perspective has one vanishing point, great for looking straight down a road or corridor. Two-point perspective has two vanishing points on the horizon line, used when you see the corner of a building, and vertical lines stay vertical."}], "qs": [["What is observational drawing?", ["Drawing from what you can actually see", "Drawing only from memory", "Copying a photo by tracing it", "Drawing with a ruler only"], "Observational drawing records a real subject that you are looking at."], ["Which shading technique uses lots of small dots?", ["Stippling", "Hatching", "Cross-hatching", "Blending"], "Stippling builds up tone using dots; more dots make it darker."], ["Shading with parallel lines drawn close together is called…", ["Hatching", "Stippling", "Smudging", "Scumbling"], "Hatching is a set of parallel lines; closer lines look darker."], ["Which pencil is softest and makes the darkest marks?", ["6B", "2H", "HB", "2B"], "B pencils are soft and dark, and the higher the number the softer."], ["Cross-hatching is made by…", ["Layering lines that cross at an angle", "Drawing a single outline", "Rubbing with your finger only", "Making random dots"], "Cross-hatching adds a second set of lines crossing the first for darker tone."], ["On a sphere, the dark shadow thrown onto the table is the…", ["Cast shadow", "Highlight", "Reflected light", "Mid-tone"], "A cast shadow is the shadow an object throws onto another surface."], ["Roughly where do the eyes sit on a human head?", ["About halfway down the head", "About a quarter of the way down", "Near the very top", "Level with the bottom of the nose"], "People often draw eyes too high, but they sit near the halfway line."], ["In perspective drawing, the horizon line represents…", ["The viewer's eye level", "The bottom of the paper", "The tallest object", "The edge of the shadow"], "The horizon line is always at the eye level of the viewer."], ["How many vanishing points does one-point perspective use?", ["One", "Two", "Three", "None"], "One-point perspective has a single vanishing point on the horizon line."], ["In two-point perspective of a building's corner, vertical edges…", ["Stay vertical", "Go to the left vanishing point", "Go to the right vanishing point", "Curve towards the horizon"], "In two-point perspective only horizontal receding lines go to the vanishing points."]], "bonus": ["Which tool is best for drawing a perfectly straight horizon line?", ["A ruler", "A rubber", "A sharpener", "A paintbrush"], "A ruler guides your pencil so the line is straight."]}, {"t": "Colour theory and painting", "intro": "Colour theory explains how colours are mixed and how they work together to create mood and contrast. Painting techniques such as watercolour washes and acrylic layering let you use colour with control. Understanding both helps you experiment and refine your work for AO2.", "how": ["The primary colours are red, yellow and blue; mix two primaries to get a secondary: orange, green or purple.", "A tertiary colour is a primary mixed with a neighbouring secondary, such as blue-green or red-orange.", "Complementary colours sit opposite on the colour wheel; harmonious colours sit next to each other.", "Add white to make a tint, add black to make a shade; warm colours seem to come forward and cool ones go back.", "Watercolour is transparent, so work from light to dark; acrylic dries quickly and is opaque, so you can layer light over dark."], "example": {"q": "How would you paint a graded watercolour wash for a sky that is darker blue at the top and fades to pale near the horizon?", "steps": ["Tilt your board slightly and mix plenty of blue paint with water before you start.", "Paint a strong band of blue across the top with a large brush.", "Add a little more water to the mix for each band as you work down, overlapping the wet edge.", "Near the bottom, use almost clear water so the colour fades to pale.", "Leave it flat to dry without going back over it."], "a": "By adding more water to each band, the wash fades smoothly from strong blue at the top to pale near the horizon."}, "walk": [{"h": "The colour wheel", "b": "A 12-part colour wheel has 3 primary, 3 secondary and 6 tertiary colours. For example, red + yellow = orange (a secondary), and orange + yellow = yellow-orange (a tertiary). Complementary pairs are red and green, blue and orange, and yellow and purple; placed side by side they make each other look brighter."}, {"h": "Tints, shades and temperature", "b": "A tint is a colour plus white, like pink from red, and a shade is a colour plus black, like dark navy from blue. Warm colours (reds, oranges, yellows) feel energetic and seem to advance, while cool colours (blues, greens, purples) feel calm and seem to recede. Harmonious colours, such as blue, blue-green and green, sit next to each other and feel peaceful together."}, {"h": "Watercolour and acrylic", "b": "Watercolour washes include a flat wash (even colour), a graded wash (fading) and wet-on-wet, where paint is dropped onto wet paper so it spreads softly. Keep the white paper for highlights, because watercolour is see-through. Acrylic is water-based, dries fast and becomes waterproof, so you can build layers and paint light details over dark ones. Blend acrylic quickly while it is still wet."}], "qs": [["Which three colours are the traditional primary colours in painting?", ["Red, yellow and blue", "Red, green and blue", "Orange, green and purple", "Black, white and grey"], "Painters treat red, yellow and blue as the primaries that mix other colours."], ["Mixing blue and yellow makes which secondary colour?", ["Green", "Purple", "Orange", "Brown"], "Blue and yellow are the two primaries that make green."], ["What is the complementary colour of red?", ["Green", "Orange", "Purple", "Blue"], "Green sits directly opposite red on the colour wheel."], ["Adding white to a colour creates a…", ["Tint", "Shade", "Tertiary colour", "Complementary colour"], "A tint is any colour lightened by adding white."], ["Which of these is a tertiary colour?", ["Yellow-green", "Green", "Yellow", "Purple"], "Yellow-green mixes a primary (yellow) with a neighbouring secondary (green)."], ["Which group shows harmonious (analogous) colours?", ["Yellow, yellow-orange, orange", "Red and green", "Blue and orange", "Yellow and purple"], "Harmonious colours sit next to each other on the colour wheel."], ["Which colours are usually called cool colours?", ["Blues, greens and purples", "Reds, oranges and yellows", "Black and white", "Browns and greys"], "Blues, greens and purples are linked with water, shade and calm."], ["In watercolour, how do you usually keep a bright white highlight?", ["Leave the white paper unpainted", "Paint white watercolour on last", "Use black paint around it", "Add lots of water on top"], "Watercolour is transparent, so the paper itself provides the whites."], ["Painting onto paper that is already wet so colours spread softly is…", ["Wet-on-wet", "Wet-on-dry", "Dry brush", "Impasto"], "Wet-on-wet lets paint flow and blend softly into damp paper."], ["Why can you paint light details over dark areas in acrylic?", ["Acrylic is opaque and dries quickly", "Acrylic is always transparent", "Acrylic never dries", "Acrylic only comes in pale colours"], "Opaque, fast-drying acrylic covers the layer underneath once it is dry."]], "bonus": ["Which two colours mix to make orange?", ["Red and yellow", "Blue and yellow", "Red and blue", "Yellow and green"], "Red and yellow are the primaries that combine to make orange."]}, {"t": "Artists, sketchbooks and AOs", "intro": "In GCSE Art you research artists, explore ideas in a sketchbook and build up to a final piece. Your work is marked against four assessment objectives (AO1 to AO4), each worth an equal share of the marks. Knowing what each AO asks for helps you plan every page.", "how": ["AO1 Develop: investigate artists and sources and show you understand them, using them to grow your ideas.", "AO2 Refine: experiment with different media, materials and techniques and improve your ideas as you go.", "AO3 Record: record observations and ideas through drawings, photos and written annotation.", "AO4 Present: produce a personal, meaningful final response that pulls your journey together.", "Annotate using the formal elements: say what you see, how it was made, what it makes you feel and how it affects your work."], "example": {"q": "How should you respond to the work of an artist such as Vincent van Gogh in your sketchbook?", "steps": ["Research the artist: Van Gogh was a Dutch Post-Impressionist known for thick, swirling brushstrokes and bold colour.", "Make a small study in his style, testing thick paint and directional marks.", "Annotate using formal elements, such as line, colour and texture, and say what you notice and feel.", "Explain how you will use his ideas, for example expressive marks, in your own original piece."], "a": "Research, a practical study, analytical annotation and a clear link to your own ideas show a strong response that meets AO1."}, "walk": [{"h": "Research an artist", "b": "Pick artists whose work links to your theme. For example, Claude Monet was a French Impressionist who painted outdoors to capture changing light, and Frida Kahlo was a Mexican artist famous for her self-portraits. Georges Seurat used tiny dots of colour, called pointillism, which links nicely to stippling. Don't just copy facts from the internet; focus on how the art is made."}, {"h": "Sketchbook and annotation", "b": "Your sketchbook shows your journey: observational drawings, photos, experiments, artist studies and notes. Annotation should be thoughtful, not just 'I like it'. A good line might be: 'Monet's short broken brushstrokes make the water shimmer, so I will try dabbing paint to show light on my river.'"}, {"h": "The four AOs", "b": "AO1 is about developing ideas from sources, AO2 about experimenting and refining, AO3 about recording, and AO4 about presenting a personal final outcome. Each is worth 25% of the marks. The portfolio of coursework is worth 60% and the externally set assignment is worth 40%, ending in 10 hours of supervised time to make your final piece."}], "qs": [["Which assessment objective is about recording ideas and observations?", ["AO3", "AO1", "AO2", "AO4"], "AO3 rewards recording through drawing, photography and annotation."], ["Testing watercolour, charcoal and collage to find what works best fits…", ["AO2 (refine and experiment)", "AO1 (develop)", "AO3 (record)", "AO4 (present)"], "AO2 is about exploring and selecting media, materials and techniques."], ["What does AO4 reward?", ["A personal, meaningful final response", "Copying facts about artists", "Only the neatest drawing", "The number of pages you fill"], "AO4 is about presenting a final outcome that realises your intentions."], ["Investigating artists to develop your own ideas is mainly…", ["AO1", "AO2", "AO3", "AO4"], "AO1 is about developing ideas through investigating sources."], ["Roughly how much is each of the four AOs worth?", ["25% each", "10% each", "40% each", "AO4 is worth 100%"], "The four AOs carry equal weight, so each is about a quarter."], ["Which is the best example of annotation?", ["The thick paint adds texture and energy", "I like this picture", "This is nice", "This took ages"], "Good annotation analyses the work using art vocabulary."], ["Claude Monet is best known as part of which art movement?", ["Impressionism", "Cubism", "Pop Art", "Surrealism"], "Monet was a leading French Impressionist who painted changing light."], ["Painting with tiny dots of colour, as Georges Seurat did, is called…", ["Pointillism", "Cross-hatching", "Impasto", "Sgraffito"], "Seurat developed pointillism, building images from small dots of colour."], ["Frida Kahlo is especially famous for painting…", ["Self-portraits", "Water lilies", "Comic-strip style pop art", "Abstract drip paintings"], "Many of Kahlo's best-known works are self-portraits."], ["The coursework portfolio and externally set assignment are worth…", ["60% and 40%", "50% and 50%", "40% and 60%", "75% and 25%"], "In GCSE Art the portfolio is 60% and the externally set assignment 40%."]], "bonus": ["What is a self-portrait?", ["A picture an artist makes of themselves", "A picture of a famous person", "A picture of a landscape", "A photo taken by someone else"], "'Self' means the artist is showing themselves."]}]}];
